&lt;b&gt;they didn't say&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:36.370 --> 00:44:38.639
&lt;b&gt;there was this sudden rise to fame&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:38.639 --> 00:44:41.141
&lt;b&gt;or at least their way of doing things is not&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:41.141 --> 00:44:43.377
&lt;b&gt;or their purpose is not to become suddenly famous&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:43.644 --> 00:44:45.579
&lt;b&gt;Right, I think so&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:46.246 --> 00:44:47.314
&lt;b&gt;Then what is it for?&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:47.948 --> 00:44:49.983
&lt;b&gt;It's for thinking problems through&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:50.550 --> 00:44:51.351
&lt;b&gt;Mm&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:51.551 --> 00:44:53.854
&lt;b&gt;How did your PhD work unfold?&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:54.521 --> 00:44:56.156
&lt;b&gt;The PhD work was also very interesting&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:56.156 --> 00:44:57.591
&lt;b&gt;PhD work&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:57.591 --> 00:44:59.459
&lt;b&gt;Um, I think it was also through&lt;/b&gt;

00:44:59.459 --> 00:45:00.994
&lt;b&gt;Professor Tu's hands-on mentoring&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:00.994 --> 00:45:02.129
&lt;b&gt;Right, but um&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:02.129 --> 00:45:03.997
&lt;b&gt;We had our first paper&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:04.164 --> 00:45:04.931
&lt;b&gt;By the way, I&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:04.931 --> 00:45:06.066
&lt;b&gt;During my PhD&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:06.233 --> 00:45:09.202
&lt;b&gt;I wasn't a successful PhD student either&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:09.202 --> 00:45:10.937
&lt;b&gt;by today's standards&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:10.937 --> 00:45:13.373
&lt;b&gt;I published maybe five or six&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:13.373 --> 00:45:14.408
&lt;b&gt;top conference papers&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:14.541 --> 00:45:16.476
&lt;b&gt;What level is that?&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:16.843 --> 00:45:17.444
&lt;b&gt;I don't know&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:17.444 --> 00:45:18.979
&lt;b&gt;That should have been fine for that era&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:18.979 --> 00:45:21.615
&lt;b&gt;the level to get a job at a top lab&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:21.815 --> 00:45:23.617
&lt;b&gt;Now it might already be&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:23.817 --> 00:45:24.451
&lt;b&gt;Right, now&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:24.451 --> 00:45:25.519
&lt;b&gt;now many of my students&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:25.519 --> 00:45:28.655
&lt;b&gt;publish many more papers than I did&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:28.755 --> 00:45:31.158
&lt;b&gt;and the quality of work is also much better&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:31.224 --> 00:45:32.526
&lt;b&gt;But anyway&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:32.526 --> 00:45:33.393
&lt;b&gt;At the beginning&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:33.393 --> 00:45:35.595
&lt;b&gt;I think we did a work called&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:35.595 --> 00:45:36.963
&lt;b&gt;Deeply Supervised Nets&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:36.963 --> 00:45:37.731
&lt;b&gt;Mm&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:37.731 --> 00:45:38.332
&lt;b&gt;This work&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:38.332 --> 00:45:40.667
&lt;b&gt;was actually&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Me and another more senior PhD student&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:40.734 --> 00:45:43.036
&lt;b&gt;completed it together in collaboration&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:43.036 --> 00:45:44.037
&lt;b&gt;And at this time&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:44.037 --> 00:45:47.741
&lt;b&gt;This was around 2013, 2014&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:47.841 --> 00:45:52.212
&lt;b&gt;And at this time, deep learning finally began to explode&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:52.212 --> 00:45:53.714
&lt;b&gt;But I think this was also a very interesting moment&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:53.714 --> 00:45:56.450
&lt;b&gt;Because actually many people didn't accept this&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:56.817 --> 00:45:58.552
&lt;b&gt;Especially many professors working in computer vision&lt;/b&gt;

00:45:58.552 --> 00:45:59.720
&lt;b&gt;didn't even accept this&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:00.187 --> 00:46:02.022
&lt;b&gt;Everyone thought&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:02.022 --> 00:46:06.560
&lt;b&gt;deep learning was still a kind of alchemy&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:07.961
&lt;b&gt;still a black box&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:07.961 --> 00:46:11.131
&lt;b&gt;people trusted traditional machine learning theory more&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:11.131 --> 00:46:14.334
&lt;b&gt;trusting SVMs, or trusting some&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:14.334 --> 00:46:15.802
&lt;b&gt;Bayesian theories&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:15.802 --> 00:46:17.003
&lt;b&gt;Right&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:17.003 --> 00:46:21.007
&lt;b&gt;being able to pivot in time to do deep learning research&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:21.007 --> 00:46:23.243
&lt;b&gt;This now, looking back&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:23.243 --> 00:46:24.945
&lt;b&gt;with the benefit of hindsight&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:25.212 --> 00:46:26.179
&lt;b&gt;is a no-brainer&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:26.179 --> 00:46:27.781
&lt;b&gt;you didn't need to make that choice&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:27.781 --> 00:46:29.116
&lt;b&gt;right, you should just do it&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:29.449 --> 00:46:31.918
&lt;b&gt;But at the time, making such a choice&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:31.918 --> 00:46:33.353
&lt;b&gt;I think required some courage&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:33.553 --> 00:46:35.122
&lt;b&gt;So Professor Tu actually is&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:35.122 --> 00:46:38.225
&lt;b&gt;another reason I admire him very, very much&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:38.225 --> 00:46:38.792
&lt;b&gt;and I&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:38.792 --> 00:46:42.395
&lt;b&gt;deeply affected by this&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:42.395 --> 00:46:43.363
&lt;b&gt;this one thing&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:43.363 --> 00:46:43.797
&lt;b&gt;That is to say&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:43.797 --> 00:46:46.767
&lt;b&gt;he actually pivoted very promptly&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:49.436
&lt;b&gt;So this Deeply Supervised Nets&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:49.436 --> 00:46:50.337
&lt;b&gt;was in this era&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:50.337 --> 00:46:52.873
&lt;b&gt;our first deep learning work&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:53.173 --> 00:46:55.442
&lt;b&gt;Right, so this thing&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:55.442 --> 00:46:56.543
&lt;b&gt;was actually simple&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:56.543 --> 00:46:58.078
&lt;b&gt;it was about how&lt;/b&gt;

00:46:58.245 --> 00:47:00.714
&lt;b&gt;all of these neural networks&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:00.714 --> 00:47:01.248
&lt;b&gt;Um&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:01.248 --> 00:47:04.017
&lt;b&gt;previously were just a single stream&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:04.017 --> 00:47:05.652
&lt;b&gt;a long chain&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:05.652 --> 00:47:07.387
&lt;b&gt;with your input&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:07.387 --> 00:47:08.722
&lt;b&gt;and getting your output&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:08.822 --> 00:47:11.100
&lt;b&gt;And now Deeply Supervised Nets&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:11.725
but this robotics isn't simple robotics&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;meaning&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:11.725 --> 00:47:14.227
&lt;b&gt;you can now actually have multiple branches&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:14.327 --> 00:47:16.997
&lt;b&gt;that is, your neural network&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:16.997 --> 00:47:19.533
&lt;b&gt;can actually have multiple exits&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:19.833 --> 00:47:21.768
&lt;b&gt;and at different exits&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:21.768 --> 00:47:23.937
&lt;b&gt;you can apply a supervision signal&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:23.937 --> 00:47:24.404
&lt;b&gt;In this way&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:24.404 --> 00:47:26.673
&lt;b&gt;the most direct benefit is&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:26.673 --> 00:47:28.141
&lt;b&gt;you can&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:28.141 --> 00:47:30.610
&lt;b&gt;not only from the signal at the far end&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:31.812
&lt;b&gt;do back propagation&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:31.812 --> 00:47:33.013
&lt;b&gt;back to&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:33.013 --> 00:47:34.748
&lt;b&gt;the early layers&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:34.748 --> 00:47:35.515
&lt;b&gt;back propagation&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:36.616 --> 00:47:37.384
&lt;b&gt;you don't need&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:37.384 --> 00:47:39.352
&lt;b&gt;to do back propagation from the far end&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:39.352 --> 00:47:41.154
&lt;b&gt;all the way to the beginning&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:41.154 --> 00:47:43.123
&lt;b&gt;you can actually from an intermediate node&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:43.123 --> 00:47:44.057
&lt;b&gt;do back propagation&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:44.124 --> 00:47:47.461
&lt;b&gt;This way&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;can partially solve the vanishing gradient problem&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:47.594 --> 00:47:48.395
&lt;b&gt;Mm&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:48.662 --> 00:47:51.198
&lt;b&gt;And this actually relates to what came later&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:51.198 --> 00:47:55.402
&lt;b&gt;for example, ResNet actually has some resemblance&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:55.402 --> 00:47:55.836
&lt;b&gt;it's actually&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:55.836 --> 00:47:56.736
&lt;b&gt;or in that era&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:56.736 --> 00:47:58.471
&lt;b&gt;everyone actually wanted to solve this problem&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:58.471 --> 00:47:59.773
&lt;b&gt;So Deeply Supervised Nets&lt;/b&gt;

00:47:59.873 --> 00:48:00.407
&lt;b&gt;was a&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:00.407 --> 00:48:02.542
&lt;b&gt;way to solve this problem&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:03.577 --> 00:48:05.812
&lt;b&gt;Actually this thing&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:05.812 --> 00:48:06.780
&lt;b&gt;though it was long ago&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:08.915
&lt;b&gt;right, this was again 12 years ago&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:08.915 --> 00:48:10.217
&lt;b&gt;but I think research is like this&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:10.383 --> 00:48:11.151
&lt;b&gt;12 years later&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:11.151 --> 00:48:12.652
&lt;b&gt;actually some of our current papers&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:12.719 --> 00:48:14.054
&lt;b&gt;are again using the same&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:14.054 --> 00:48:15.422
&lt;b&gt;kind of design&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:15.422 --> 00:48:17.757
&lt;b&gt;sometimes we don't even realize it&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:17.858 --> 00:48:19.593
&lt;b&gt;I think this is very interesting&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:19.659 --> 00:48:21.628
&lt;b&gt;But let's not talk about 12 years later&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:21.628 --> 00:48:23.163
&lt;b&gt;Right, so my second paper&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:23.163 --> 00:48:26.633
&lt;b&gt;was called Holistically-Nested Edge Detection (HED)&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:26.633 --> 00:48:28.635
&lt;b&gt;a work on edge detection&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:28.668 --> 00:48:29.736
&lt;b&gt;HED&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:29.736 --> 00:48:32.639
&lt;b&gt;Right, I think about this paper&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:32.639 --> 00:48:33.974
&lt;b&gt;I'm actually quite proud of it&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:33.974 --> 00:48:35.775
&lt;b&gt;Because frankly&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:35.775 --> 00:48:38.778
&lt;b&gt;it solved a research problem&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:39.512 --> 00:48:40.780
&lt;b&gt;um, it was both lucky&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:41.648
&lt;b&gt;and unlucky&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:41.648 --> 00:48:42.616
&lt;b&gt;The lucky part is&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:42.616 --> 00:48:44.551
&lt;b&gt;this paper was a good paper&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:44.751 --> 00:48:45.852
&lt;b&gt;The unlucky part is&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:46.052 --> 00:48:47.420
&lt;b&gt;once the problem was solved&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:48.521
&lt;b&gt;nobody worked on it afterward&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:48.521 --> 00:48:50.590
&lt;b&gt;so nobody cited your paper&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:50.590 --> 00:48:51.391
&lt;b&gt;[chuckles]&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:51.391 --> 00:48:52.759
&lt;b&gt;so it lost many citations&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:52.759 --> 00:48:53.793
&lt;b&gt;[chuckles]&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:53.860 --> 00:48:55.729
&lt;b&gt;Um, but um&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:55.729 --> 00:48:56.396
&lt;b&gt;But this work&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:56.396 --> 00:48:59.232
&lt;b&gt;is essentially a Deeply Supervised Nets&lt;/b&gt;

00:48:59.232 --> 00:49:01.568
&lt;b&gt;DSN applied to&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:01.668 --> 00:49:03.136
&lt;b&gt;image&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:03.136 --> 00:49:04.638
&lt;b&gt;or edge detection&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:04.638 --> 00:49:07.207
&lt;b&gt;but it's actually a global&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:07.207 --> 00:49:09.009
&lt;b&gt;what we call pixel labeling&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:09.009 --> 00:49:11.478
&lt;b&gt;pixel-level&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:11.478 --> 00:49:12.579
&lt;b&gt;annotation&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:12.579 --> 00:49:13.346
&lt;b&gt;task&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:13.346 --> 00:49:14.214
&lt;b&gt;implementation&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:14.214 --> 00:49:15.015
&lt;b&gt;Mm&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:15.048 --> 00:49:18.752
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:19.152 --> 00:49:20.587
&lt;b&gt;also opened up many new ways of thinking for me&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:20.587 --> 00:49:21.922
&lt;b&gt;because I would discover&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:21.988 --> 00:49:23.723
&lt;b&gt;that a neural network&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:23.723 --> 00:49:24.925
&lt;b&gt;each of its layers&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:25.158 --> 00:49:26.993
&lt;b&gt;actually has implicit structure&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:26.993 --> 00:49:28.061
&lt;b&gt;and information in it&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:28.762 --> 00:49:31.131
&lt;b&gt;your neural network, again&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:31.131 --> 00:49:32.966
&lt;b&gt;has not only input and output&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:33.066 --> 00:49:35.936
&lt;b&gt;in between there is a lot of information&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:35.969 --> 00:49:36.569
&lt;b&gt;it represents&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:36.569 --> 00:49:39.439
&lt;b&gt;a so-called hierarchical&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:39.439 --> 00:49:40.941
&lt;b&gt;hierarchical structure of the world&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:41.174 --> 00:49:42.642
&lt;b&gt;For edge detection&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:42.742 --> 00:49:43.343
&lt;b&gt;it represents&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:43.343 --> 00:49:45.979
&lt;b&gt;that your early layers&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:45.979 --> 00:49:47.314
&lt;b&gt;output edges that are&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:48.848
&lt;b&gt;more so-called coarse&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:48.848 --> 00:49:50.383
&lt;b&gt;more coarse edges&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:50.450 --> 00:49:52.018
&lt;b&gt;Right, and the further up&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:52.018 --> 00:49:53.620
&lt;b&gt;the more refined your edges become&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:53.820 --> 00:49:55.355
&lt;b&gt;So&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Finally you can take all of these edges&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:55.355 --> 00:49:56.356
&lt;b&gt;and fuse them together&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:56.389 --> 00:49:59.392
&lt;b&gt;to get one that best approximates human perception&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:59.392 --> 00:49:59.960
&lt;b&gt;such an edge&lt;/b&gt;

00:49:59.960 --> 00:50:01.861
&lt;b&gt;output result&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:02.395 --> 00:50:03.163
&lt;b&gt;I think this&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:03.163 --> 00:50:03.964
&lt;b&gt;was actually&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:04.130 --> 00:50:07.067
&lt;b&gt;also giving me a new understanding of deep learning&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:07.133 --> 00:50:09.970
&lt;b&gt;It's a very interesting, very interesting thing&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:11.871
&lt;b&gt;You can think of it as a black box&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:12.038 --> 00:50:14.374
&lt;b&gt;but each part of this black box&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:14.441 --> 00:50:16.242
&lt;b&gt;you can open up&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:16.242 --> 00:50:17.978
&lt;b&gt;connect some new inspiration&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:17.978 --> 00:50:19.412
&lt;b&gt;and reach some new goals&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:19.412 --> 00:50:21.381
&lt;b&gt;I think this was very enlightening for me&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:21.781 --> 00:50:24.618
&lt;b&gt;And this paper at the time&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:24.784 --> 00:50:26.152
&lt;b&gt;also had a big impact on my life&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:26.152 --> 00:50:29.122
&lt;b&gt;because it was published at ICCV&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:29.255 --> 00:50:31.057
&lt;b&gt;and also received an award&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:31.057 --> 00:50:32.525
&lt;b&gt;This award was the Marr Prize&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:32.525 --> 00:50:34.527
&lt;b&gt;the Best Paper Award nomination&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:34.527 --> 00:50:35.829
&lt;b&gt;not the Best Paper Award itself&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:35.862 --> 00:50:36.930
&lt;b&gt;just a nomination&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:38.465
&lt;b&gt;But actually for the Marr Prize&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:38.465 --> 00:50:40.567
&lt;b&gt;it selects two papers&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:40.567 --> 00:50:41.134
&lt;b&gt;which is equivalent to&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:41.134 --> 00:50:44.204
&lt;b&gt;the Marr Prize and Honorable Mention are two awards&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:44.204 --> 00:50:46.773
&lt;b&gt;So this made me feel&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:46.773 --> 00:50:47.974
&lt;b&gt;if you want to say sudden fame&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:47.974 --> 00:50:49.643
&lt;b&gt;I really did feel at the time&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:49.643 --> 00:50:51.745
&lt;b&gt;look, I also became famous at a young age&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:51.745 --> 00:50:52.612
&lt;b&gt;Now, of course&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:52.612 --> 00:50:55.048
&lt;b&gt;we have many Chinese students&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:55.048 --> 00:50:56.950
&lt;b&gt;also on the world stage&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:56.950 --> 00:50:58.952
&lt;b&gt;winning so many Best Papers&lt;/b&gt;

00:50:58.952 --> 00:51:01.554
&lt;b&gt;Right, but back then for me&lt;/b&gt;

00:51:01.554 --> 00:51:02.722
&lt;b&gt;walking onto that stage&lt;/b&gt;

00:51:02.722 --> 00:51:04.724
&lt;b&gt;or that podium&lt;/b&gt;

