01:16:23.208 --> 01:16:25.243
&lt;b&gt;I think people have become relatively more tolerant.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:25.243 --> 01:16:25.810
&lt;b&gt;Back then,&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:25.810 --> 01:16:28.747
&lt;b&gt;people were extremely nitpicky about details.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:28.747 --> 01:16:29.981
&lt;b&gt;Yeah, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:29.981 --> 01:16:30.549
&lt;b&gt;But it's fine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:30.549 --> 01:16:32.117
&lt;b&gt;We ended up submitting to AISTATS&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:32.117 --> 01:16:34.052
&lt;b&gt;— another conference —&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:34.052 --> 01:16:35.153
&lt;b&gt;a machine learning conference.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:35.253 --> 01:16:37.155
&lt;b&gt;And that paper&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:37.155 --> 01:16:39.391
&lt;b&gt;won their Test of Time Award last year.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:39.624 --> 01:16:41.826
&lt;b&gt;The Test of Time Award.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:42.193 --> 01:16:42.827
&lt;b&gt;So I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:42.827 --> 01:16:43.628
&lt;b&gt;After all this time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:43.662 --> 01:16:44.062
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:44.062 --> 01:16:46.031
&lt;b&gt;Because all Test of Time Awards evaluate&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:46.031 --> 01:16:47.866
&lt;b&gt;things 10 years later —&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:47.866 --> 01:16:49.935
&lt;b&gt;at the 10-year mark,&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:50.035 --> 01:16:53.171
&lt;b&gt;among all papers published 10 years ago,&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:53.271 --> 01:16:55.473
&lt;b&gt;which paper had the greatest influence&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:55.473 --> 01:16:57.075
&lt;b&gt;on the field.&lt;/b&gt;

01:16:57.242 --> 01:17:00.378
&lt;b&gt;Right. So I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:00.378 --> 01:17:01.379
&lt;b&gt;I suddenly felt at peace again.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:01.379 --> 01:17:02.647
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:02.647 --> 01:17:05.817
&lt;b&gt;Research truly is a long-term process.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:06.017 --> 01:17:06.818
&lt;b&gt;And so,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:06.818 --> 01:17:07.519
&lt;b&gt;That's also why&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:07.519 --> 01:17:10.055
&lt;b&gt;I tell many of my students this:&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:10.055 --> 01:17:12.390
&lt;b&gt;And I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:12.624 --> 01:17:16.595
&lt;b&gt;don't worry about&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:16.861 --> 01:17:19.631
&lt;b&gt;your wins and losses at every moment.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:19.631 --> 01:17:21.866
&lt;b&gt;Or, to describe it mathematically,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:21.866 --> 01:17:24.069
&lt;b&gt;don't worry about a point estimate.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:24.135 --> 01:17:25.804
&lt;b&gt;Don't, on this timeline,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:25.804 --> 01:17:26.771
&lt;b&gt;at every point,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:26.805 --> 01:17:29.207
&lt;b&gt;evaluate whether you're doing well or not.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:29.207 --> 01:17:31.276
&lt;b&gt;Because all evaluations&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:32.143 --> 01:17:34.479
&lt;b&gt;are ultimately an integral.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:34.679 --> 01:17:36.815
&lt;b&gt;You need the accumulation of time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:37.048 --> 01:17:37.983
&lt;b&gt;In the end, look —&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:37.983 --> 01:17:39.918
&lt;b&gt;everything you've ever done,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:39.918 --> 01:17:41.186
&lt;b&gt;added together,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:41.419 --> 01:17:44.055
&lt;b&gt;determines whether you're a good researcher.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:44.856 --> 01:17:45.590
&lt;b&gt;But in that moment,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:45.590 --> 01:17:47.392
&lt;b&gt;you'll still feel very down.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:47.726 --> 01:17:48.627
&lt;b&gt;Very down. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:48.627 --> 01:17:49.361
&lt;b&gt;Extremely down.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:49.361 --> 01:17:51.529
&lt;b&gt;In that moment it's hard to think about 10 years later.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:51.529 --> 01:17:52.564
&lt;b&gt;Hard to think about what happens 10 years from now.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:52.564 --> 01:17:53.632
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:54.265 --> 01:17:56.034
&lt;b&gt;When you finished your PhD,&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:56.034 --> 01:17:58.169
&lt;b&gt;what expectations did you have for your life?&lt;/b&gt;

01:17:58.169 --> 01:18:00.171
&lt;b&gt;You had published some good papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:00.405 --> 01:18:04.209
&lt;b&gt;you had 5 internship experiences,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:04.209 --> 01:18:07.145
&lt;b&gt;did you think you should go into research&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:07.145 --> 01:18:08.179
&lt;b&gt;or into industry?&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:08.179 --> 01:18:09.147
&lt;b&gt;Did you make that choice?&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:09.147 --> 01:18:10.849
&lt;b&gt;I was never very confident back then.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:10.849 --> 01:18:13.585
&lt;b&gt;At that time I never even considered a faculty position.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:13.585 --> 01:18:15.720
&lt;b&gt;Because I thought I didn't deserve it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:15.720 --> 01:18:17.555
&lt;b&gt;[laughter] Because&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:17.622 --> 01:18:20.125
&lt;b&gt;Why did you feel unworthy at every moment?&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:20.125 --> 01:18:20.992
&lt;b&gt;It's a bit better now.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:20.992 --> 01:18:21.660
&lt;b&gt;But, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:21.660 --> 01:18:22.861
&lt;b&gt;Maybe that's a bit of an exaggeration.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:22.861 --> 01:18:24.062
&lt;b&gt;It's not that I really felt unworthy.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:24.062 --> 01:18:26.665
&lt;b&gt;But compared to my peers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:26.665 --> 01:18:29.200
&lt;b&gt;they were on the established track,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:29.200 --> 01:18:29.768
&lt;b&gt;like I said,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:29.768 --> 01:18:32.737
&lt;b&gt;moving step by step toward a good faculty position.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:32.937 --> 01:18:34.039
&lt;b&gt;That path.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:34.039 --> 01:18:35.640
&lt;b&gt;I felt I wasn't on that path.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:35.640 --> 01:18:36.374
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:36.374 --> 01:18:36.675
&lt;b&gt;Or rather,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:36.675 --> 01:18:38.543
&lt;b&gt;What you just said makes a lot of sense.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:38.576 --> 01:18:40.245
&lt;b&gt;If your final destination&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:40.245 --> 01:18:41.179
&lt;b&gt;was really a faculty position,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:41.179 --> 01:18:43.014
&lt;b&gt;at least at that point in time,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:43.148 --> 01:18:44.516
&lt;b&gt;you shouldn't have gone to 5 places&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:44.516 --> 01:18:45.650
&lt;b&gt;for 5 internships,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:45.650 --> 01:18:47.018
&lt;b&gt;working on 5 different projects.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:47.218 --> 01:18:48.653
&lt;b&gt;That's very unfavorable for&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:48.653 --> 01:18:49.721
&lt;b&gt;finding a faculty position.&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:49.721 --> 01:18:51.156
&lt;b&gt;If you wanted a faculty position,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:51.156 --> 01:18:52.857
&lt;b&gt;staying in Kaiming He's team&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:52.857 --> 01:18:54.926
&lt;b&gt;would have let you publish more papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:54.926 --> 01:18:56.828
&lt;b&gt;gotten more results,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:56.828 --> 01:18:57.929
&lt;b&gt;during that period,&lt;/b&gt;

01:18:58.196 --> 01:19:00.598
&lt;b&gt;it might have been a smoother path&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:00.598 --> 01:19:02.167
&lt;b&gt;toward a definite goal.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:02.667 --> 01:19:04.402
&lt;b&gt;I don't know if it was a definite goal.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:04.402 --> 01:19:06.271
&lt;b&gt;I really think it's quite mysterious.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:06.271 --> 01:19:08.606
&lt;b&gt;All these decisions came down to:&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:08.606 --> 01:19:12.177
&lt;b&gt;I only thought about where I should go&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:12.177 --> 01:19:14.713
&lt;b&gt;to do what I most wanted to do,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:14.779 --> 01:19:17.148
&lt;b&gt;ideally with the people I most wanted to work with.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:17.148 --> 01:19:18.049
&lt;b&gt;Working together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:18.049 --> 01:19:20.185
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;This idea is actually very, very simple.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:20.218 --> 01:19:21.653
&lt;b&gt;So when job hunting back then,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:21.653 --> 01:19:23.121
&lt;b&gt;actually I&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:23.121 --> 01:19:24.322
&lt;b&gt;I was looking everywhere.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:24.322 --> 01:19:26.424
&lt;b&gt;There were quite a few offers from major companies.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:26.624 --> 01:19:27.125
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:27.125 --> 01:19:28.660
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:28.660 --> 01:19:31.563
&lt;b&gt;I've talked before about my OpenAI interview experience.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:31.563 --> 01:19:33.364
&lt;b&gt;It was actually pretty cool.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:33.364 --> 01:19:34.766
&lt;b&gt;Basically, I was in a small dark room&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:34.766 --> 01:19:36.334
&lt;b&gt;for five or six hours,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:36.334 --> 01:19:37.769
&lt;b&gt;working on one problem.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:37.769 --> 01:19:39.137
&lt;b&gt;When I came out, it was already dark.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:39.204 --> 01:19:41.673
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;I found the experience quite fascinating.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:41.673 --> 01:19:43.475
&lt;b&gt;It felt quite extraordinary.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:43.475 --> 01:19:44.876
&lt;b&gt;But back then actually&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:44.876 --> 01:19:45.443
&lt;b&gt;Who was the interviewer at OpenAI?&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:45.443 --> 01:19:46.244
&lt;b&gt;John Schulman (OpenAI co-founder, Thinking Machines co-founder and Chief Scientist)&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:46.444 --> 01:19:46.811
&lt;b&gt;Oh, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:46.811 --> 01:19:48.747
&lt;b&gt;I saw you wrote about this experience on Zhihu.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:48.747 --> 01:19:49.714
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Uh, not on Zhihu,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:49.714 --> 01:19:50.782
&lt;b&gt;it was on Twitter,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:50.782 --> 01:19:51.649
&lt;b&gt;on X.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:51.649 --> 01:19:52.450
&lt;b&gt;Right, Zhihu reposted it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:52.450 --> 01:19:53.017
&lt;b&gt;That's it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:53.017 --> 01:19:53.985
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:53.985 --> 01:19:55.754
&lt;b&gt;So his original&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:55.754 --> 01:19:57.455
&lt;b&gt;interview questions were on a single A4 sheet of paper,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:57.455 --> 01:19:58.723
&lt;b&gt;handwritten in pencil,&lt;/b&gt;

01:19:58.723 --> 01:20:01.326
&lt;b&gt;line by line, handwritten interview questions.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:01.860 --> 01:20:02.560
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:02.560 --> 01:20:04.429
&lt;b&gt;it really moved me deeply.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:04.429 --> 01:20:05.730
&lt;b&gt;I found it so fascinating.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:05.730 --> 01:20:07.365
&lt;b&gt;This place is very interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:07.565 --> 01:20:09.033
&lt;b&gt;And, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:09.033 --> 01:20:09.667
&lt;b&gt;In the end,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:09.667 --> 01:20:10.268
&lt;b&gt;Actually,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:10.268 --> 01:20:11.169
&lt;b&gt;There was an offer, of course,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:11.169 --> 01:20:12.804
&lt;b&gt;but in the end&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:12.804 --> 01:20:14.005
&lt;b&gt;I didn't go to OpenAI.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:14.205 --> 01:20:14.806
&lt;b&gt;I didn't go to OpenAI.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:14.806 --> 01:20:16.875
&lt;b&gt;This is where the timeline&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:16.875 --> 01:20:20.078
&lt;b&gt;— quantum mechanics — starts to diverge.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:20.078 --> 01:20:22.013
&lt;b&gt;That was 2018.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:22.013 --> 01:20:22.747
&lt;b&gt;So early.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:22.747 --> 01:20:23.615
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:23.615 --> 01:20:26.184
&lt;b&gt;So if I had gone to OpenAI, maybe, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:26.184 --> 01:20:28.119
&lt;b&gt;you'd now be part of the LLM world.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:28.219 --> 01:20:30.321
&lt;b&gt;Maybe. I don't think so.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:30.422 --> 01:20:30.955
&lt;b&gt;I don't know.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:30.955 --> 01:20:31.256
&lt;b&gt;I don't know.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:31.256 --> 01:20:32.724
&lt;b&gt;Don't know what would have happened.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:32.724 --> 01:20:34.025
&lt;b&gt;Back then I didn't even think about it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:34.025 --> 01:20:35.727
&lt;b&gt;I just wanted to go to FAIR.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:35.727 --> 01:20:36.795
&lt;b&gt;If FAIR gave me the offer,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:36.795 --> 01:20:37.595
&lt;b&gt;I would definitely go.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:38.163 --> 01:20:39.764
&lt;b&gt;Your reason for wanting to go to FAIR was Kaiming?&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:39.964 --> 01:20:40.465
&lt;b&gt;Uh, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:40.465 --> 01:20:42.133
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming, Piotr Dollar,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:42.133 --> 01:20:43.034
&lt;b&gt;Ross Girshick.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:43.334 --> 01:20:44.669
&lt;b&gt;The so-called&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:44.669 --> 01:20:46.971
&lt;b&gt;the three pillars of computer vision back then.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:47.138 --> 01:20:49.574
&lt;b&gt;They weren't that senior —&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:49.574 --> 01:20:50.608
&lt;b&gt;university professors or anything like that —&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:50.608 --> 01:20:52.944
&lt;b&gt;they were all&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:53.077 --> 01:20:54.045
&lt;b&gt;young to mid-career,&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:54.045 --> 01:20:55.313
&lt;b&gt;researchers.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:55.313 --> 01:20:57.015
&lt;b&gt;But the absolute top three.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:57.048 --> 01:20:58.149
&lt;b&gt;Right, they were there.&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:58.149 --> 01:20:59.551
&lt;b&gt;And the research they were doing was&lt;/b&gt;

01:20:59.551 --> 01:21:01.586
&lt;b&gt;the absolute top-tier computer vision research.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:01.619 --> 01:21:02.187
&lt;b&gt;So for me,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:02.187 --> 01:21:03.154
&lt;b&gt;there was no choice to make.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:03.354 --> 01:21:04.355
&lt;b&gt;So it was kind of fun back then.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:04.355 --> 01:21:05.523
&lt;b&gt;Here's the thing —&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:05.523 --> 01:21:07.759
&lt;b&gt;Ilya (Ilya Sutskever, SSI founder and CEO, OpenAI co-founder and former Chief Scientist)&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:07.759 --> 01:21:08.927
&lt;b&gt;called me, and I said almost nothing,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:08.927 --> 01:21:09.961
&lt;b&gt;and I rejected OpenAI.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:09.961 --> 01:21:11.062
&lt;b&gt;They sent me an offer,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:11.062 --> 01:21:12.263
&lt;b&gt;and I said I'm not going, sorry.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:12.263 --> 01:21:13.465
&lt;b&gt;What did Ilya say on the call?&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:13.665 --> 01:21:14.532
&lt;b&gt;Uh, he was very angry.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:14.532 --> 01:21:16.367
&lt;b&gt;He asked me,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:16.367 --> 01:21:18.436
&lt;b&gt;"Why didn't you even discuss it&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:18.436 --> 01:21:19.771
&lt;b&gt;before rejecting the offer?"&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:20.138 --> 01:21:21.773
&lt;b&gt;"Is the money not enough?"&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:21.773 --> 01:21:22.574
&lt;b&gt;How much was it?&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:22.674 --> 01:21:23.608
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:23.608 --> 01:21:24.375
&lt;b&gt;I don't remember exactly.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:24.375 --> 01:21:25.276
&lt;b&gt;It was actually very, very low.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:25.276 --> 01:21:26.544
&lt;b&gt;Maybe, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:26.544 --> 01:21:27.345
&lt;b&gt;probably in the hundreds of thousands.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:27.345 --> 01:21:29.247
&lt;b&gt;Back then the pay for&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:29.247 --> 01:21:31.850
&lt;b&gt;a top PhD student&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:31.950 --> 01:21:33.952
&lt;b&gt;around 2008 would be&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:33.952 --> 01:21:36.187
&lt;b&gt;roughly $400K to $500K&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:36.321 --> 01:21:37.055
&lt;b&gt;dollars.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:37.055 --> 01:21:38.590
&lt;b&gt;Dollars. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:38.590 --> 01:21:40.725
&lt;b&gt;And now it's at least tripled.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:40.725 --> 01:21:41.826
&lt;b&gt;But anyway,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:41.826 --> 01:21:43.394
&lt;b&gt;at that time&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:43.394 --> 01:21:44.729
&lt;b&gt;OpenAI was at that level too,&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:44.729 --> 01:21:45.563
&lt;b&gt;which was fine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:46.197 --> 01:21:46.664
&lt;b&gt;Right. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:46.664 --> 01:21:47.465
&lt;b&gt;But Ilya was very angry.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:47.465 --> 01:21:48.233
&lt;b&gt;So I&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:48.233 --> 01:21:49.434
&lt;b&gt;I could only give vague responses&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:49.434 --> 01:21:49.801
&lt;b&gt;and told him&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:49.801 --> 01:21:51.603
&lt;b&gt;that I couldn't go.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:51.603 --> 01:21:52.937
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:52.937 --> 01:21:53.638
&lt;b&gt;At that time indeed&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:53.638 --> 01:21:55.039
&lt;b&gt;what did he say when angry?&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:55.240 --> 01:21:55.940
&lt;b&gt;Uh, not much actually.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:55.940 --> 01:21:57.742
&lt;b&gt;His tone was just very stern.&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:57.742 --> 01:21:59.844
&lt;b&gt;Why did he decide to make this call?&lt;/b&gt;

01:21:59.978 --> 01:22:00.345
&lt;b&gt;I don't know.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:00.345 --> 01:22:02.914
&lt;b&gt;That shows he really cared about recruiting.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:02.914 --> 01:22:04.048
&lt;b&gt;He had never been rejected before.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:04.449 --> 01:22:05.183
&lt;b&gt;Uh, no.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:05.183 --> 01:22:05.950
&lt;b&gt;I don't think that's the case.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:05.950 --> 01:22:06.784
&lt;b&gt;In 2018,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:06.784 --> 01:22:08.953
&lt;b&gt;I think he was probably often rejected.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:08.953 --> 01:22:10.221
&lt;b&gt;Because FAIR at that time&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:10.755 --> 01:22:12.123
&lt;b&gt;— not just in Vision —&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:12.123 --> 01:22:13.524
&lt;b&gt;in many areas,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:13.825 --> 01:22:16.127
&lt;b&gt;for the top PhD graduates,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:16.127 --> 01:22:20.765
&lt;b&gt;FAIR was more certain than OpenAI,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:21.032 --> 01:22:22.567
&lt;b&gt;more open,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:22.634 --> 01:22:24.736
&lt;b&gt;more like an academic environment.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:24.736 --> 01:22:26.471
&lt;b&gt;Such an institution.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:26.571 --> 01:22:29.474
&lt;b&gt;I think, at least at that time,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:29.474 --> 01:22:30.375
&lt;b&gt;everyone around me,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:30.375 --> 01:22:31.843
&lt;b&gt;if given that choice,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:32.944 --> 01:22:33.344
&lt;b&gt;unless&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:33.344 --> 01:22:36.714
&lt;b&gt;they really wanted to do what OpenAI was already doing,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:36.714 --> 01:22:38.116
&lt;b&gt;the things OpenAI excelled at,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:38.116 --> 01:22:40.018
&lt;b&gt;I think most people would still lean toward FAIR.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:40.385 --> 01:22:43.187
&lt;b&gt;Did you get the FAIR offer smoothly?&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:43.421 --> 01:22:44.923
&lt;b&gt;Uh, not that smoothly.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:44.923 --> 01:22:46.958
&lt;b&gt;I think it was also quite&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:46.958 --> 01:22:48.860
&lt;b&gt;rocky all the way.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:48.860 --> 01:22:49.994
&lt;b&gt;When you rejected OpenAI,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:49.994 --> 01:22:50.995
&lt;b&gt;was it because you already had the FAIR offer?&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:50.995 --> 01:22:52.263
&lt;b&gt;Yes, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:52.263 --> 01:22:52.964
&lt;b&gt;But at FAIR,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:52.964 --> 01:22:54.399
&lt;b&gt;I gave a talk,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:54.632 --> 01:22:55.867
&lt;b&gt;this talk —&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:55.867 --> 01:22:57.135
&lt;b&gt;I had no experience at all,&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:57.135 --> 01:22:59.470
&lt;b&gt;it seemed everyone at my stage&lt;/b&gt;

01:22:59.470 --> 01:23:01.806
&lt;b&gt;was quite experienced at job hunting,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:01.873 --> 01:23:03.207
&lt;b&gt;while I knew nothing.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:03.207 --> 01:23:04.208
&lt;b&gt;So I gave a talk,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:04.208 --> 01:23:05.376
&lt;b&gt;and, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:05.376 --> 01:23:07.345
&lt;b&gt;the talk was scheduled for one hour.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:07.445 --> 01:23:10.715
&lt;b&gt;Normally you'd speak for 45 to 50 minutes&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:10.715 --> 01:23:13.051
&lt;b&gt;with 10 minutes for questions.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:13.217 --> 01:23:15.186
&lt;b&gt;But I finished in 30 minutes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:15.186 --> 01:23:16.120
&lt;b&gt;Done.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:18.189 --> 01:23:20.458
&lt;b&gt;Everyone looked at each other,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:20.458 --> 01:23:21.359
&lt;b&gt;not knowing what to do.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:21.359 --> 01:23:22.427
&lt;b&gt;of course,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:22.427 --> 01:23:24.796
&lt;b&gt;many of the researchers there&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:24.796 --> 01:23:25.997
&lt;b&gt;gave me a lot of face&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:25.997 --> 01:23:27.065
&lt;b&gt;and asked many questions,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:27.065 --> 01:23:30.168
&lt;b&gt;so the time was somehow stretched to 45 minutes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:30.234 --> 01:23:32.337
&lt;b&gt;It wasn't too awkward.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:32.337 --> 01:23:33.471
&lt;b&gt;Later Kaiming told me&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:33.471 --> 01:23:35.940
&lt;b&gt;that everyone thought this was&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:37.275
&lt;b&gt;first, very unconventional.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:37.275 --> 01:23:38.743
&lt;b&gt;How could you finish so fast?&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:38.876 --> 01:23:41.412
&lt;b&gt;Second,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Maybe interviews should all be like this —&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:41.412 --> 01:23:43.448
&lt;b&gt;a 30-minute talk works fine,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:43.448 --> 01:23:44.649
&lt;b&gt;saves everyone's time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:44.816 --> 01:23:46.351
&lt;b&gt;So many times&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:46.351 --> 01:23:47.785
&lt;b&gt;I've done things&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:47.785 --> 01:23:50.021
&lt;b&gt;without doing them perfectly.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:50.054 --> 01:23:51.656
&lt;b&gt;Hmm, why did you finish so quickly?&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:51.656 --> 01:23:53.157
&lt;b&gt;Why didn't you follow the rules?&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:53.157 --> 01:23:54.559
&lt;b&gt;I didn't know there was a rule.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:54.559 --> 01:23:55.360
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:55.426 --> 01:23:56.227
&lt;b&gt;Didn't read it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:56.628 --> 01:23:58.196
&lt;b&gt;Uh, I didn't know about this rule.&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:58.196 --> 01:23:58.796
&lt;b&gt;Like now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:58.796 --> 01:23:59.197
&lt;b&gt;for example,&lt;/b&gt;

01:23:59.197 --> 01:24:02.800
&lt;b&gt;Because&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;this rule is actually a job talk rule.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:03.034 --> 01:24:05.003
&lt;b&gt;Nobody told me this rule.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:05.003 --> 01:24:06.204
&lt;b&gt;Right, people just said,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:06.204 --> 01:24:08.139
&lt;b&gt;"There's a talk starting at 11,"&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:08.206 --> 01:24:10.608
&lt;b&gt;but this is actually an established convention&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:10.675 --> 01:24:13.678
&lt;b&gt;because that's how academic interviews work.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:13.678 --> 01:24:18.149
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;FAIR back then was actually an academic institution.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:18.216 --> 01:24:19.017
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:19.017 --> 01:24:20.318
&lt;b&gt;It was really like a university.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:20.451 --> 01:24:23.187
&lt;b&gt;Its operating model was like a PI&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:23.221 --> 01:24:24.856
&lt;b&gt;leading a group of young people —&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:24.856 --> 01:24:26.057
&lt;b&gt;whether interns&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:26.057 --> 01:24:28.126
&lt;b&gt;or newly joined members —&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:28.126 --> 01:24:29.260
&lt;b&gt;working together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:29.394 --> 01:24:31.629
&lt;b&gt;And when I joined FAIR,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:31.629 --> 01:24:33.765
&lt;b&gt;I was probably&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:33.765 --> 01:24:34.966
&lt;b&gt;among the first few — I'm not sure —&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:34.966 --> 01:24:36.801
&lt;b&gt;Chen Xinlei was probably the first,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:36.801 --> 01:24:37.935
&lt;b&gt;but I was probably the second —&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:37.935 --> 01:24:40.672
&lt;b&gt;a fresh PhD graduate who could join FAIR.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:41.005 --> 01:24:43.741
&lt;b&gt;At first they didn't recruit new PhD graduates.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:44.475 --> 01:24:46.344
&lt;b&gt;If you were just a PhD graduate,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:46.611 --> 01:24:47.845
&lt;b&gt;they didn't want you.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:47.845 --> 01:24:51.516
&lt;b&gt;They would only recruit people like Kaiming,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:51.516 --> 01:24:53.051
&lt;b&gt;who had already done very impressive work,&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:53.051 --> 01:24:54.552
&lt;b&gt;those kinds of researchers.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:54.552 --> 01:24:55.253
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:55.253 --> 01:24:55.987
&lt;b&gt;So I was also quite&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:55.987 --> 01:24:57.455
&lt;b&gt;lucky. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:58.556 --> 01:24:59.323
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:24:59.323 --> 01:25:01.426
&lt;b&gt;I think FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:01.426 --> 01:25:03.061
&lt;b&gt;really was the holy temple at that time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:03.061 --> 01:25:03.761
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:03.761 --> 01:25:04.595
&lt;b&gt;And so,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:04.595 --> 01:25:07.565
&lt;b&gt;I didn't agonize much over&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:07.565 --> 01:25:09.000
&lt;b&gt;too many other possibilities.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:09.000 --> 01:25:09.734
&lt;b&gt;Mm. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:09.734 --> 01:25:11.202
&lt;b&gt;About the Ilya situation,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:11.202 --> 01:25:12.570
&lt;b&gt;let me add one more thing.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:12.870 --> 01:25:16.474
&lt;b&gt;I've only talked to Ilya on the phone twice.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:16.541 --> 01:25:17.675
&lt;b&gt;This was the first time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:17.675 --> 01:25:18.976
&lt;b&gt;We can talk about the second time later.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:19.143 --> 01:25:19.944
&lt;b&gt;It was&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:19.944 --> 01:25:21.279
&lt;b&gt;in July 2024,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:21.279 --> 01:25:23.047
&lt;b&gt;right after he founded SSI.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:23.047 --> 01:25:24.582
&lt;b&gt;He emailed me and asked&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:24.582 --> 01:25:25.983
&lt;b&gt;if I'd be willing to come work together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:26.017 --> 01:25:27.018
&lt;b&gt;And you rejected him again.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:27.418 --> 01:25:28.386
&lt;b&gt;Uh, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:28.386 --> 01:25:29.454
&lt;b&gt;Why this time?&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:30.188 --> 01:25:33.591
&lt;b&gt;This time because I had just started at NYU.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:33.591 --> 01:25:34.459
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:35.193 --> 01:25:36.627
&lt;b&gt;Mm. I think there were several reasons.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:36.627 --> 01:25:37.995
&lt;b&gt;When I talked with him,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:38.396 --> 01:25:39.230
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:39.363 --> 01:25:40.832
&lt;b&gt;the main topic we discussed&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:40.832 --> 01:25:42.033
&lt;b&gt;wasn't salary or anything like that.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:42.033 --> 01:25:43.167
&lt;b&gt;We didn't talk about any of that.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:43.267 --> 01:25:44.769
&lt;b&gt;The main topic was&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:44.802 --> 01:25:47.171
&lt;b&gt;how to give future artificial intelligence&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:47.271 --> 01:25:48.573
&lt;b&gt;the ability to love.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:49.040 --> 01:25:50.808
&lt;b&gt;the ability to love.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:50.808 --> 01:25:52.577
&lt;b&gt;Discussing philosophy.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:52.577 --> 01:25:53.678
&lt;b&gt;Of course, I finally asked him&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:53.678 --> 01:25:54.078
&lt;b&gt;one question.&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:54.078 --> 01:25:57.648
&lt;b&gt;I asked how he viewed multimodality,&lt;/b&gt;

01:25:57.915 --> 01:26:00.218
&lt;b&gt;how he viewed computer vision,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:00.218 --> 01:26:04.155
&lt;b&gt;or general perception models —&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:04.755 --> 01:26:06.224
&lt;b&gt;what did he think?&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:06.224 --> 01:26:07.725
&lt;b&gt;Ilya's response was&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:07.859 --> 01:26:10.661
&lt;b&gt;he felt this was already solved well enough.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:11.129 --> 01:26:12.830
&lt;b&gt;Okay, so I thought&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:13.064 --> 01:26:14.265
&lt;b&gt;maybe, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:14.265 --> 01:26:17.568
&lt;b&gt;SSI has its own language-based&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:17.568 --> 01:26:19.337
&lt;b&gt;approach.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:19.337 --> 01:26:20.471
&lt;b&gt;And that approach,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:20.538 --> 01:26:21.873
&lt;b&gt;at least for now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:22.006 --> 01:26:24.208
&lt;b&gt;is not the path I want to pursue.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:24.375 --> 01:26:26.177
&lt;b&gt;This is your fundamental disagreement —&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:26.277 --> 01:26:29.547
&lt;b&gt;LLM versus vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:29.580 --> 01:26:31.449
&lt;b&gt;Right. We can talk more about this later.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:31.449 --> 01:26:34.919
&lt;b&gt;But I don't actually see this as a disagreement.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:35.620 --> 01:26:38.756
&lt;b&gt;I see it as an organism.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:38.756 --> 01:26:40.925
&lt;b&gt;Everyone is just in different places,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:40.925 --> 01:26:43.227
&lt;b&gt;doing different things at different times.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:43.361 --> 01:26:44.796
&lt;b&gt;I always like to say,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:44.796 --> 01:26:45.963
&lt;b&gt;"Brothers climbing a mountain,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:45.963 --> 01:26:47.331
&lt;b&gt;each making their own effort."&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:47.532 --> 01:26:49.267
&lt;b&gt;Everyone doing their own thing.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:49.267 --> 01:26:50.668
&lt;b&gt;No problem with that at all.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:50.668 --> 01:26:52.603
&lt;b&gt;It's not a fight to the death.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:53.171 --> 01:26:56.040
&lt;b&gt;LLMs don't conflict with what I want to do.&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:56.340 --> 01:26:59.277
&lt;b&gt;And without the recent developments in LLMs,&lt;/b&gt;

01:26:59.577 --> 01:27:00.478
&lt;b&gt;there might not have been&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:00.478 --> 01:27:02.847
&lt;b&gt;the current state of computer vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:02.847 --> 01:27:03.648
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:03.815 --> 01:27:04.782
&lt;b&gt;That topic you discussed —&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:04.782 --> 01:27:06.584
&lt;b&gt;how to give AI the ability to love —&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:06.584 --> 01:27:08.219
&lt;b&gt;did you reach any conclusions?&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:09.220 --> 01:27:10.922
&lt;b&gt;The conclusion is that this is very important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:11.189 --> 01:27:11.989
&lt;b&gt;Why?&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:12.356 --> 01:27:14.225
&lt;b&gt;Because without it,&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:14.358 --> 01:27:17.962
&lt;b&gt;we face a very uncertain&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:17.962 --> 01:27:19.163
&lt;b&gt;and very dangerous future.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:19.430 --> 01:27:21.732
&lt;b&gt;But with love comes hate.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:21.966 --> 01:27:23.935
&lt;b&gt;They're two sides of the same coin.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:23.935 --> 01:27:25.169
&lt;b&gt;It can't only have love.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:25.169 --> 01:27:26.337
&lt;b&gt;When it learns to love,&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:26.337 --> 01:27:27.238
&lt;b&gt;it will definitely&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:27.238 --> 01:27:29.440
&lt;b&gt;know what the opposite is.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:29.640 --> 01:27:30.474
&lt;b&gt;For me,&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:30.474 --> 01:27:31.809
&lt;b&gt;completely agree with you.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:31.809 --> 01:27:32.610
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:32.977 --> 01:27:35.546
&lt;b&gt;This becomes a philosophical proposition.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:35.546 --> 01:27:36.147
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:36.147 --> 01:27:39.584
&lt;b&gt;But let me ask a counter-question.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:39.584 --> 01:27:43.187
&lt;b&gt;Why do people trust their own children,&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:43.387 --> 01:27:44.822
&lt;b&gt;trust humans so much,&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:45.556 --> 01:27:48.025
&lt;b&gt;but have such worry and fear&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:48.025 --> 01:27:49.660
&lt;b&gt;about AI, this new&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:49.861 --> 01:27:51.629
&lt;b&gt;form of intelligent entity?&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:51.896 --> 01:27:55.833
&lt;b&gt;I don't have an answer to that.&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:55.867 --> 01:27:56.434
&lt;b&gt;But I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:56.434 --> 01:27:58.202
&lt;b&gt;There will be technical ways&lt;/b&gt;

01:27:58.202 --> 01:28:01.839
&lt;b&gt;to have control.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:02.173 --> 01:28:04.041
&lt;b&gt;We can use technical means&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:04.542 --> 01:28:07.612
&lt;b&gt;to make AI more trustworthy in the future,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:07.612 --> 01:28:08.479
&lt;b&gt;safer,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:08.479 --> 01:28:10.514
&lt;b&gt;and more controllable.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:10.715 --> 01:28:11.816
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Controllable.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:12.116 --> 01:28:14.919
&lt;b&gt;And this is also one reason&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:14.919 --> 01:28:15.653
&lt;b&gt;why we need to work on&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:15.653 --> 01:28:18.122
&lt;b&gt;world models.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:18.589 --> 01:28:20.825
&lt;b&gt;Why did he want to reach out to you?&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:20.825 --> 01:28:21.359
&lt;b&gt;Uh, I don't know.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:21.359 --> 01:28:22.860
&lt;b&gt;Maybe he reached out to&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:22.860 --> 01:28:23.394
&lt;b&gt;a thousand people,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:23.394 --> 01:28:24.362
&lt;b&gt;ten thousand people.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:24.996 --> 01:28:26.030
&lt;b&gt;I guess. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:27.231 --> 01:28:30.067
&lt;b&gt;When we were waiting in line at a restaurant that day,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:30.067 --> 01:28:32.236
&lt;b&gt;we actually walked through the streets of New York together,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:32.236 --> 01:28:34.805
&lt;b&gt;and our conversation naturally extended to&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:34.805 --> 01:28:37.141
&lt;b&gt;people who have greatly influenced you.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:37.208 --> 01:28:39.043
&lt;b&gt;In what you shared just now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:39.043 --> 01:28:40.678
&lt;b&gt;the human factor&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:40.678 --> 01:28:43.781
&lt;b&gt;takes up a very large share of many of your choices.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:44.348 --> 01:28:46.150
&lt;b&gt;Why are people so important to you?&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:46.150 --> 01:28:49.020
&lt;b&gt;And in your personal bio,&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:49.020 --> 01:28:51.322
&lt;b&gt;you clearly listed&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:51.322 --> 01:28:53.457
&lt;b&gt;which collaborators are important to you.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:53.457 --> 01:28:54.625
&lt;b&gt;That's very rare.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:54.625 --> 01:28:56.460
&lt;b&gt;Why are people so crucial to you?&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:56.627 --> 01:28:57.595
&lt;b&gt;Is this unusual?&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:57.595 --> 01:28:58.596
&lt;b&gt;I don't think it's unusual at all.&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:58.596 --> 01:28:59.997
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:28:59.997 --> 01:29:02.767
&lt;b&gt;In academic circles,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:02.767 --> 01:29:05.736
&lt;b&gt;this is a common behavioral pattern.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:05.736 --> 01:29:07.672
&lt;b&gt;People organize themselves into&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:07.672 --> 01:29:08.873
&lt;b&gt;these social networks.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:08.873 --> 01:29:13.644
&lt;b&gt;Mm. And these people shape your thinking,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:13.644 --> 01:29:15.179
&lt;b&gt;because they may be your students,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:15.179 --> 01:29:17.214
&lt;b&gt;they may be your teachers, right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:17.214 --> 01:29:19.650
&lt;b&gt;But teachers don't always teach students.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:19.650 --> 01:29:22.720
&lt;b&gt;Sometimes students teach the teachers.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:22.720 --> 01:29:24.722
&lt;b&gt;All of this can be true.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:24.722 --> 01:29:26.958
&lt;b&gt;So it's a huge graph&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:26.991 --> 01:29:29.593
&lt;b&gt;where everyone is connected.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:29.593 --> 01:29:30.795
&lt;b&gt;And I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:30.795 --> 01:29:33.731
&lt;b&gt;That's also why research,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:33.731 --> 01:29:35.967
&lt;b&gt;or science, is especially fascinating.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:36.167 --> 01:29:38.169
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Because many times&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:38.235 --> 01:29:40.438
&lt;b&gt;the mutual&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:40.438 --> 01:29:41.772
&lt;b&gt;trust between people,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:41.772 --> 01:29:43.474
&lt;b&gt;mutual appreciation,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:43.474 --> 01:29:47.044
&lt;b&gt;mutual feelings —&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:47.078 --> 01:29:48.379
&lt;b&gt;these aren't built through&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:48.379 --> 01:29:49.714
&lt;b&gt;living together&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:49.714 --> 01:29:51.782
&lt;b&gt;and being friends.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:51.782 --> 01:29:54.218
&lt;b&gt;Many times it's through scientific discovery,&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:54.218 --> 01:29:56.153
&lt;b&gt;kind of&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:56.153 --> 01:29:58.222
&lt;b&gt;this research aspect, that connections are built.&lt;/b&gt;

01:29:58.222 --> 01:29:59.423
&lt;b&gt;Relationships between people.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:00.024 --> 01:30:01.859
&lt;b&gt;I think this is actually very interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:01.926 --> 01:30:04.161
&lt;b&gt;For example, those who deeply influenced me —&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:04.161 --> 01:30:07.531
&lt;b&gt;I may get to know them personally,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:07.531 --> 01:30:09.633
&lt;b&gt;of course I try to get to know them personally,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:09.633 --> 01:30:12.003
&lt;b&gt;right, but that's not what matters most to me.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:12.003 --> 01:30:14.538
&lt;b&gt;I seem to understand them through their papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:14.638 --> 01:30:15.873
&lt;b&gt;learning their way of thinking.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:16.774 --> 01:30:17.641
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:17.641 --> 01:30:19.377
&lt;b&gt;I think that's the real meaning of research.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:19.610 --> 01:30:21.612
&lt;b&gt;I don't think the purpose of research is to publish papers.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:21.612 --> 01:30:22.113
&lt;b&gt;I&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:22.113 --> 01:30:23.047
&lt;b&gt;I don't think&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:23.414 --> 01:30:24.215
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:24.448 --> 01:30:25.216
&lt;b&gt;publishing papers&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:25.216 --> 01:30:28.052
&lt;b&gt;is the goal.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:28.052 --> 01:30:29.120
&lt;b&gt;Not at all.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:29.320 --> 01:30:30.221
&lt;b&gt;The purpose should be —&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:31.122 --> 01:30:32.390
&lt;b&gt;what is the purpose?&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:32.390 --> 01:30:33.190
&lt;b&gt;ah,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:33.557 --> 01:30:36.027
&lt;b&gt;Is it a journey through people?&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:36.594 --> 01:30:39.263
&lt;b&gt;What Kaiming told me the purpose is:&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:40.164 --> 01:30:40.965
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:41.399 --> 01:30:44.034
&lt;b&gt;at its core it means&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:44.435 --> 01:30:45.970
&lt;b&gt;sharing knowledge.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:46.537 --> 01:30:50.641
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;The purpose of publishing a paper isn't for others to see it,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:51.542 --> 01:30:54.045
&lt;b&gt;but so that after others see the paper,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:54.045 --> 01:30:55.179
&lt;b&gt;they have something to work on.&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:56.947 --> 01:30:57.715
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:57.715 --> 01:30:59.216
&lt;b&gt;You publish a paper,&lt;/b&gt;

01:30:59.350 --> 01:31:01.018
&lt;b&gt;others understand some of the content,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:01.018 --> 01:31:02.019
&lt;b&gt;and they feel&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:02.019 --> 01:31:04.388
&lt;b&gt;their own horizons have expanded.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:04.588 --> 01:31:06.357
&lt;b&gt;Mm. It's about helping others.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:06.357 --> 01:31:07.425
&lt;b&gt;Being helpful to others.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:07.525 --> 01:31:09.026
&lt;b&gt;Right. Being able to inspire others,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:09.026 --> 01:31:10.261
&lt;b&gt;or enlighten others.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:10.261 --> 01:31:12.263
&lt;b&gt;Oh, that's the purpose of research.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:12.563 --> 01:31:14.198
&lt;b&gt;I think that's the purpose of research.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:14.198 --> 01:31:15.966
&lt;b&gt;Or, to put it more romantically,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:15.966 --> 01:31:17.468
&lt;b&gt;the idea is&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:17.468 --> 01:31:20.805
&lt;b&gt;I think this —&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;this comes from Hannah Arendt (political philosopher),&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:21.005 --> 01:31:23.441
&lt;b&gt;and she said&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:23.641 --> 01:31:25.576
&lt;b&gt;she doesn't care about impact.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:25.676 --> 01:31:27.578
&lt;b&gt;She doesn't care about influence.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:27.645 --> 01:31:28.045
&lt;b&gt;Because&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:28.045 --> 01:31:29.447
&lt;b&gt;In researcher circles,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:29.447 --> 01:31:30.147
&lt;b&gt;people say&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:30.147 --> 01:31:33.317
&lt;b&gt;we publish papers to create some kind of impact,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:33.317 --> 01:31:33.918
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:33.918 --> 01:31:35.019
&lt;b&gt;In my own dictionary,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:35.019 --> 01:31:37.621
&lt;b&gt;I actually have a bit of an aversion to the word impact.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:37.755 --> 01:31:38.456
&lt;b&gt;Aversion.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:38.456 --> 01:31:39.356
&lt;b&gt;A bit of an aversion.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:39.356 --> 01:31:40.257
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:40.858 --> 01:31:41.826
&lt;b&gt;Uh, why?&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:41.826 --> 01:31:43.327
&lt;b&gt;What is it about it that you resist?&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:43.627 --> 01:31:44.762
&lt;b&gt;Again, Arendt&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:44.762 --> 01:31:45.729
&lt;b&gt;said that&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:45.729 --> 01:31:47.798
&lt;b&gt;she felt, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:47.798 --> 01:31:51.535
&lt;b&gt;the word "impact" is overly aggressive,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:51.535 --> 01:31:53.170
&lt;b&gt;overly masculine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:54.071 --> 01:31:55.473
&lt;b&gt;For her,&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:55.473 --> 01:31:58.642
&lt;b&gt;the purpose of doing these things is not to create impact&lt;/b&gt;

01:31:58.809 --> 01:32:00.644
&lt;b&gt;but for understanding itself.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:01.378 --> 01:32:03.848
&lt;b&gt;If you can understand something,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:03.948 --> 01:32:05.983
&lt;b&gt;the feeling is wonderful.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:06.784 --> 01:32:09.820
&lt;b&gt;If you can write down what you've understood,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:09.854 --> 01:32:12.223
&lt;b&gt;whether it's an article or a paper,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:12.389 --> 01:32:13.524
&lt;b&gt;and spread it,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:13.824 --> 01:32:14.558
&lt;b&gt;then you can&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:14.558 --> 01:32:16.961
&lt;b&gt;potentially allow more people in the world&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:17.361 --> 01:32:18.629
&lt;b&gt;to understand&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:18.629 --> 01:32:21.365
&lt;b&gt;such a question in the same way you do.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:21.465 --> 01:32:23.734
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:23.734 --> 01:32:26.437
&lt;b&gt;will be transmitted step by step,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:26.437 --> 01:32:30.174
&lt;b&gt;creating a kind of resonance.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:30.341 --> 01:32:30.674
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:30.674 --> 01:32:32.943
&lt;b&gt;Arendt's view is that&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:33.144 --> 01:32:35.713
&lt;b&gt;she would find in this a sense of family —&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:35.713 --> 01:32:36.780
&lt;b&gt;a feeling of family.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:37.448 --> 01:32:40.251
&lt;b&gt;She would feel that she understood something,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:40.851 --> 01:32:41.785
&lt;b&gt;told others,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:41.785 --> 01:32:43.254
&lt;b&gt;allowed others to understand,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:43.721 --> 01:32:46.924
&lt;b&gt;which means these people also understood her to some degree.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:47.358 --> 01:32:47.958
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:47.958 --> 01:32:50.261
&lt;b&gt;But humans, as social beings,&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:50.361 --> 01:32:51.629
&lt;b&gt;need to be understood.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:51.962 --> 01:32:52.530
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:52.530 --> 01:32:53.797
&lt;b&gt;He reframed the word "influence"&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:53.797 --> 01:32:56.734
&lt;b&gt;in a very soft way —&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:56.734 --> 01:32:58.068
&lt;b&gt;seeking to be understood.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:58.202 --> 01:32:59.436
&lt;b&gt;I think so.&lt;/b&gt;

01:32:59.436 --> 01:33:00.137
&lt;b&gt;I think so.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:00.137 --> 01:33:01.472
&lt;b&gt;You agree more with this view?&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:01.472 --> 01:33:03.407
&lt;b&gt;I agree with her very much.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:03.474 --> 01:33:05.543
&lt;b&gt;Because I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:05.543 --> 01:33:08.245
&lt;b&gt;Creating impact is fine in itself.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:08.245 --> 01:33:09.747
&lt;b&gt;But it's very self-centered.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:09.747 --> 01:33:11.182
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:11.182 --> 01:33:12.917
&lt;b&gt;I'm going to create impact. Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:12.917 --> 01:33:14.218
&lt;b&gt;Right. Me-centered.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:14.218 --> 01:33:15.786
&lt;b&gt;And yes,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:15.786 --> 01:33:16.854
&lt;b&gt;you're absolutely right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:16.854 --> 01:33:18.422
&lt;b&gt;I'm going to create this impact,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:18.589 --> 01:33:20.157
&lt;b&gt;I'm going to change the world,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:21.225 --> 01:33:25.362
&lt;b&gt;but do the people in this world agree to be changed by me?&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:25.763 --> 01:33:26.564
&lt;b&gt;[laughs]&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:26.797 --> 01:33:28.732
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, many disasters in the world&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:28.732 --> 01:33:31.468
&lt;b&gt;are because people want to create impact,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:31.468 --> 01:33:34.805
&lt;b&gt;want to transform the world.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:34.805 --> 01:33:35.206
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:35.206 --> 01:33:36.373
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:36.373 --> 01:33:40.044
&lt;b&gt;I would tend to agree with this softer expression.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:40.044 --> 01:33:41.812
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:41.812 --> 01:33:45.316
&lt;b&gt;If all people in this world,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:45.316 --> 01:33:47.218
&lt;b&gt;through our research,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:47.218 --> 01:33:49.520
&lt;b&gt;can gain a new layer of understanding,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:49.520 --> 01:33:52.122
&lt;b&gt;a new layer of knowledge,&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:52.122 --> 01:33:55.259
&lt;b&gt;the total intelligence on Earth would increase.&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:55.559 --> 01:33:58.429
&lt;b&gt;And increasing total intelligence on Earth&lt;/b&gt;

01:33:58.429 --> 01:33:59.930
&lt;b&gt;is never wrong.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:00.831 --> 01:34:03.834
&lt;b&gt;It's always something beneficial to the world.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:04.068 --> 01:34:05.769
&lt;b&gt;Whether it's called impact&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:05.769 --> 01:34:08.138
&lt;b&gt;or being understood by more people.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:08.138 --> 01:34:11.942
&lt;b&gt;Do you want to be known and remembered by more people?&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:12.042 --> 01:34:15.279
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Do you have a need for fame?&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:15.379 --> 01:34:16.647
&lt;b&gt;I certainly don't have that need.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:16.647 --> 01:34:17.715
&lt;b&gt;You don't have that need.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:17.715 --> 01:34:18.148
&lt;b&gt;But I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:18.148 --> 01:34:19.416
&lt;b&gt;I don't have that need.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:19.416 --> 01:34:20.251
&lt;b&gt;But really?&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:20.351 --> 01:34:21.185
&lt;b&gt;Uh, I&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:21.185 --> 01:34:23.721
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, from where I stand now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:23.854 --> 01:34:26.390
&lt;b&gt;I'm actually a victim&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:26.390 --> 01:34:27.358
&lt;b&gt;of a kind of false fame.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:28.325 --> 01:34:29.426
&lt;b&gt;Uh, the reason is&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:29.426 --> 01:34:32.029
&lt;b&gt;people now take some of our papers&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:32.029 --> 01:34:33.631
&lt;b&gt;and post them on Xiaohongshu,&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:33.631 --> 01:34:37.334
&lt;b&gt;to discuss — and actually none of this&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:37.334 --> 01:34:39.270
&lt;b&gt;— people talk about the so-called top-three conferences&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:39.270 --> 01:34:41.538
&lt;b&gt;and promote the work, right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:42.306 --> 01:34:44.008
&lt;b&gt;I&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;I have never once&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:44.008 --> 01:34:47.177
&lt;b&gt;asked any such media outlet&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:47.411 --> 01:34:48.879
&lt;b&gt;to do this kind of promotion.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:48.879 --> 01:34:49.813
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:49.813 --> 01:34:51.849
&lt;b&gt;And I tell my students:&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:51.849 --> 01:34:54.151
&lt;b&gt;please don't go on Xiaohongshu&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:54.151 --> 01:34:55.219
&lt;b&gt;or Zhihu&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:55.519 --> 01:34:56.987
&lt;b&gt;to promote your own work.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:56.987 --> 01:34:58.122
&lt;b&gt;You can explain your work,&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:58.122 --> 01:34:59.556
&lt;b&gt;you can comment on your work.&lt;/b&gt;

01:34:59.556 --> 01:35:00.591
&lt;b&gt;That's fine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:00.591 --> 01:35:02.026
&lt;b&gt;Just don't promote yourself.&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:02.026 --> 01:35:03.827
&lt;b&gt;Why is it okay on X?&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:04.194 --> 01:35:05.929
&lt;b&gt;I think on X,&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:06.363 --> 01:35:09.166
&lt;b&gt;uh, it's more about&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:09.166 --> 01:35:10.868
&lt;b&gt;how you define promotion.&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:10.868 --> 01:35:12.436
&lt;b&gt;What I focus on&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:12.436 --> 01:35:15.839
&lt;b&gt;is briefly summarizing things&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:15.839 --> 01:35:17.474
&lt;b&gt;and telling people what it's about.&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:17.574 --> 01:35:20.077
&lt;b&gt;It's more like attracting people to look at my work,&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:20.077 --> 01:35:21.445
&lt;b&gt;and I think that's fine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:21.445 --> 01:35:22.346
&lt;b&gt;But the promotion I'm referring to&lt;/b&gt;

01:35:22.346 --> 01:35:24.982
&lt;b&gt;is more like the fame you mentioned,&lt;/b&gt;

