01:41:31.515 --> 01:41:32.315
&lt;b&gt;Right. Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:32.349 --> 01:41:33.950
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, it was he who chose me.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:33.950 --> 01:41:34.751
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:35.419 --> 01:41:35.952
&lt;b&gt;Important people.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:35.952 --> 01:41:36.953
&lt;b&gt;Are there others?&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:37.754 --> 01:41:39.556
&lt;b&gt;Mm. I think there are.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:39.556 --> 01:41:43.527
&lt;b&gt;For example, during my time at NYU,&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:43.527 --> 01:41:47.964
&lt;b&gt;I also collaborated with many other professors,&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:47.964 --> 01:41:50.534
&lt;b&gt;and one person who I think influenced me greatly&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:50.534 --> 01:41:52.235
&lt;b&gt;would be Professor Fei-Fei.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:52.335 --> 01:41:53.336
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:53.336 --> 01:41:54.805
&lt;b&gt;I think Professor Li Fei-Fei —&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:55.439 --> 01:41:58.175
&lt;b&gt;uh, everyone should definitely read the book she wrote.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:58.175 --> 01:41:59.242
&lt;b&gt;Right, her autobiography.&lt;/b&gt;

01:41:59.276 --> 01:42:00.644
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:00.644 --> 01:42:01.578
&lt;b&gt;And I've read it too.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:01.578 --> 01:42:04.781
&lt;b&gt;But after having deep conversations with her,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:04.781 --> 01:42:06.249
&lt;b&gt;I gained even more.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:06.516 --> 01:42:09.786
&lt;b&gt;Right. Sometimes I would&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:09.786 --> 01:42:10.554
&lt;b&gt;tell her&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:10.554 --> 01:42:12.322
&lt;b&gt;I was facing&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:12.322 --> 01:42:14.524
&lt;b&gt;this difficulty and challenge,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:15.058 --> 01:42:18.962
&lt;b&gt;and Professor Fei-Fei would tell me earnestly&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:18.962 --> 01:42:20.263
&lt;b&gt;some stories from her past.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:20.630 --> 01:42:21.531
&lt;b&gt;Mm. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:21.531 --> 01:42:23.734
&lt;b&gt;This was actually a great comfort to me.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:23.767 --> 01:42:25.602
&lt;b&gt;What kind of stories?&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:25.602 --> 01:42:26.169
&lt;b&gt;Specific things&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:26.169 --> 01:42:28.572
&lt;b&gt;might not be appropriate to share.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:28.772 --> 01:42:30.073
&lt;b&gt;But in short,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:30.073 --> 01:42:33.477
&lt;b&gt;her journey wasn't smooth sailing at all.&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:33.477 --> 01:42:37.614
&lt;b&gt;Mm. She also had to&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:38.582 --> 01:42:39.950
&lt;b&gt;wade through many thorns,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:39.950 --> 01:42:42.753
&lt;b&gt;overcoming many obstacles step by step,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:42.853 --> 01:42:43.987
&lt;b&gt;and now&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:44.154 --> 01:42:46.590
&lt;b&gt;standing on the world stage,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:46.756 --> 01:42:48.325
&lt;b&gt;becoming a pride of the Chinese community,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:48.325 --> 01:42:51.194
&lt;b&gt;or becoming a North Star for the entire research field,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:51.194 --> 01:42:54.431
&lt;b&gt;especially computer vision,&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:54.664 --> 01:42:57.634
&lt;b&gt;allowing everyone to see&lt;/b&gt;

01:42:57.634 --> 01:43:01.238
&lt;b&gt;what she's thinking&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:01.238 --> 01:43:02.973
&lt;b&gt;and being able to&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:02.973 --> 01:43:04.207
&lt;b&gt;in some sense&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:04.941 --> 01:43:07.077
&lt;b&gt;set some new directions.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:07.077 --> 01:43:09.412
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:09.579 --> 01:43:11.381
&lt;b&gt;Right, her influence on me has been enormous.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:11.615 --> 01:43:12.048
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:12.048 --> 01:43:12.716
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:12.716 --> 01:43:15.919
&lt;b&gt;And I think Professor Fei-Fei's greatest strength is&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:15.919 --> 01:43:19.156
&lt;b&gt;that she's someone who can define problems.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:19.356 --> 01:43:20.323
&lt;b&gt;Mm. This point&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:20.323 --> 01:43:22.993
&lt;b&gt;is actually not very intuitive.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:22.993 --> 01:43:27.230
&lt;b&gt;When people talk about Professor Fei-Fei,&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:27.264 --> 01:43:29.399
&lt;b&gt;her greatest achievement&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:29.399 --> 01:43:30.800
&lt;b&gt;is building ImageNet,&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:30.800 --> 01:43:31.868
&lt;b&gt;this dataset.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:32.469 --> 01:43:34.805
&lt;b&gt;But in fact, this isn't just a dataset.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:34.805 --> 01:43:37.874
&lt;b&gt;This isn't just data.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:37.874 --> 01:43:39.409
&lt;b&gt;It's hard to imagine&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:39.409 --> 01:43:41.811
&lt;b&gt;that back then, right,&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:41.811 --> 01:43:45.215
&lt;b&gt;around 2012 or 2011,&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:46.049 --> 01:43:49.653
&lt;b&gt;image classification wasn't a well-defined problem.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:49.653 --> 01:43:51.988
&lt;b&gt;Defining this problem clearly&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:51.988 --> 01:43:53.557
&lt;b&gt;was far more important&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:53.557 --> 01:43:54.524
&lt;b&gt;than building such a dataset —&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:54.524 --> 01:43:55.959
&lt;b&gt;far, far more important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:55.959 --> 01:43:57.060
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:57.561 --> 01:43:59.996
&lt;b&gt;And I think Professor Fei-Fei&lt;/b&gt;

01:43:59.996 --> 01:44:01.531
&lt;b&gt;set this agenda,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:01.531 --> 01:44:03.133
&lt;b&gt;defined this problem clearly,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:03.133 --> 01:44:04.935
&lt;b&gt;so that subsequently&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:04.935 --> 01:44:07.070
&lt;b&gt;Deep Learning could have a playground,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:07.070 --> 01:44:08.772
&lt;b&gt;have such a platform&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:08.772 --> 01:44:10.207
&lt;b&gt;to showcase its capabilities.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:10.340 --> 01:44:11.074
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:11.074 --> 01:44:12.809
&lt;b&gt;This is her greatest achievement,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:12.809 --> 01:44:14.644
&lt;b&gt;and also what I always want to learn from.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:14.778 --> 01:44:15.412
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:15.412 --> 01:44:16.980
&lt;b&gt;So I worked with her on&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:16.980 --> 01:44:17.747
&lt;b&gt;two pieces of work.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:17.747 --> 01:44:19.049
&lt;b&gt;One is Thinking Space,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:19.049 --> 01:44:19.916
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:19.916 --> 01:44:20.617
&lt;b&gt;this paper&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:20.617 --> 01:44:22.319
&lt;b&gt;mainly involves&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:22.319 --> 01:44:24.120
&lt;b&gt;within multimodal base models,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:24.120 --> 01:44:25.555
&lt;b&gt;how to solve,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:25.555 --> 01:44:27.357
&lt;b&gt;better solve this kind of&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:27.357 --> 01:44:29.426
&lt;b&gt;uh, spatial intelligence problem.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:30.160 --> 01:44:33.330
&lt;b&gt;Well, recently we have another paper called Cambrian-S,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:33.430 --> 01:44:35.765
&lt;b&gt;and this paper also addresses&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:35.765 --> 01:44:38.468
&lt;b&gt;questions about video —&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:38.468 --> 01:44:40.637
&lt;b&gt;how do we define problems,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:40.637 --> 01:44:42.272
&lt;b&gt;which problems are actually important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:42.572 --> 01:44:43.373
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:43.373 --> 01:44:46.610
&lt;b&gt;I think this collaboration with her&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:46.643 --> 01:44:48.778
&lt;b&gt;has also helped expand the boundaries of my research.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:49.279 --> 01:44:51.781
&lt;b&gt;How did you come to know Professor Fei-Fei well?&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:52.182 --> 01:44:54.050
&lt;b&gt;Uh, it was all quite serendipitous.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:54.050 --> 01:44:55.785
&lt;b&gt;She came to New York on a business trip once,&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:55.785 --> 01:44:58.355
&lt;b&gt;and we had a meal together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:44:58.788 --> 01:45:00.056
&lt;b&gt;And she told me&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:00.056 --> 01:45:01.558
&lt;b&gt;a lot of things.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:01.758 --> 01:45:05.028
&lt;b&gt;Right. And she would often come to New York later,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:05.028 --> 01:45:07.163
&lt;b&gt;and because she's also starting a company,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:07.163 --> 01:45:08.732
&lt;b&gt;we would often get together&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:08.732 --> 01:45:09.599
&lt;b&gt;and chat.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:09.799 --> 01:45:10.867
&lt;b&gt;Right, roughly that.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:10.934 --> 01:45:12.869
&lt;b&gt;And normally we'd have&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:12.869 --> 01:45:13.770
&lt;b&gt;some research meetings.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:14.671 --> 01:45:16.273
&lt;b&gt;Mm. I'm curious about something,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:16.273 --> 01:45:19.276
&lt;b&gt;and I think many people are curious about this too.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:19.276 --> 01:45:24.714
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;How did you go from being a very young&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:24.948 --> 01:45:27.784
&lt;b&gt;researcher just starting out in academia,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:27.784 --> 01:45:28.518
&lt;b&gt;and gradually,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:28.518 --> 01:45:31.922
&lt;b&gt;come to be alongside these well-known names in AI,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:31.922 --> 01:45:32.622
&lt;b&gt;come together with them&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:32.622 --> 01:45:34.057
&lt;b&gt;and stand alongside them?&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:34.057 --> 01:45:34.524
&lt;b&gt;That is,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:34.524 --> 01:45:37.761
&lt;b&gt;how did you enter the core of AI?&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:39.329 --> 01:45:40.030
&lt;b&gt;I&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:40.030 --> 01:45:41.731
&lt;b&gt;I still don't feel I'm at the core of AI,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:41.731 --> 01:45:44.034
&lt;b&gt;or that I've gotten close to it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:44.034 --> 01:45:45.635
&lt;b&gt;Mm. But the people you just mentioned,&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:45.635 --> 01:45:47.570
&lt;b&gt;certainly many people would love to collaborate with them.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:49.039 --> 01:45:49.839
&lt;b&gt;Is that so?&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:50.073 --> 01:45:51.207
&lt;b&gt;Ah, of course.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:51.207 --> 01:45:52.609
&lt;b&gt;Right. I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:52.676 --> 01:45:55.812
&lt;b&gt;And look — all of it was serendipity.&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:55.979 --> 01:45:57.847
&lt;b&gt;With Kaiming it was just happening to be there&lt;/b&gt;

01:45:57.948 --> 01:46:00.383
&lt;b&gt;as an intern and getting him to open up.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:00.383 --> 01:46:01.951
&lt;b&gt;And with Professor Fei-Fei,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:01.951 --> 01:46:03.720
&lt;b&gt;you just had one meal together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:03.787 --> 01:46:05.722
&lt;b&gt;How did you get them to open up to you?&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:08.391 --> 01:46:10.760
&lt;b&gt;I think this is very hard to do intentionally.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:11.328 --> 01:46:14.764
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Or this is a bit mysterious.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:14.764 --> 01:46:18.335
&lt;b&gt;You could call it some kind of law of attraction.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:18.335 --> 01:46:19.602
&lt;b&gt;Or you could think of it as&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:21.271 --> 01:46:23.006
&lt;b&gt;people whose thoughts align&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:23.006 --> 01:46:25.775
&lt;b&gt;ultimately converging together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:26.009 --> 01:46:27.344
&lt;b&gt;Though you may have countless small streams,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:27.344 --> 01:46:30.213
&lt;b&gt;in the end, they may all converge into one river.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:31.014 --> 01:46:32.415
&lt;b&gt;I think, for example,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:32.782 --> 01:46:34.751
&lt;b&gt;uh, all the people I've mentioned,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:34.751 --> 01:46:37.520
&lt;b&gt;at least they're all working on vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:37.520 --> 01:46:38.555
&lt;b&gt;Or rather,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:38.555 --> 01:46:39.389
&lt;b&gt;Even including Yann,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:39.389 --> 01:46:41.324
&lt;b&gt;who can be seen as doing general AI,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:41.324 --> 01:46:42.192
&lt;b&gt;but his starting point, right,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:42.192 --> 01:46:43.827
&lt;b&gt;was also digit recognition,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:43.827 --> 01:46:45.662
&lt;b&gt;which is also a visual problem.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:46.196 --> 01:46:46.730
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:46.730 --> 01:46:49.432
&lt;b&gt;I think everyone's foundation&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:49.432 --> 01:46:52.435
&lt;b&gt;is very, very aligned.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:53.169 --> 01:46:54.537
&lt;b&gt;So I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:54.537 --> 01:46:57.240
&lt;b&gt;I really didn't make these things happen intentionally.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:57.340 --> 01:46:57.741
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:57.741 --> 01:46:58.341
&lt;b&gt;And many things,&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:58.341 --> 01:46:59.009
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:46:59.009 --> 01:47:00.977
&lt;b&gt;don't need to be made to happen intentionally.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:00.977 --> 01:47:04.414
&lt;b&gt;Everyone is just based on these research questions,&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:04.414 --> 01:47:06.516
&lt;b&gt;and their understanding of these questions,&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:06.516 --> 01:47:08.385
&lt;b&gt;collaborating together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:08.551 --> 01:47:09.786
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:09.786 --> 01:47:10.820
&lt;b&gt;I would think of it this way.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:10.820 --> 01:47:11.955
&lt;b&gt;The thing is that&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:11.955 --> 01:47:12.789
&lt;b&gt;from the outside,&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:12.789 --> 01:47:14.391
&lt;b&gt;I'd see you as someone very goal-oriented&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:14.391 --> 01:47:16.059
&lt;b&gt;and very logical.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:16.159 --> 01:47:17.827
&lt;b&gt;But through our conversation just now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:17.827 --> 01:47:20.463
&lt;b&gt;I find you're someone whose choices are quite disorderly.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:20.463 --> 01:47:21.264
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:21.431 --> 01:47:21.798
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:21.798 --> 01:47:24.067
&lt;b&gt;I think there's a certain disorder.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:24.067 --> 01:47:24.567
&lt;b&gt;Mm. But I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:24.567 --> 01:47:26.770
&lt;b&gt;this is also a by-design process.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:26.770 --> 01:47:29.272
&lt;b&gt;I choose this disorder.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:29.272 --> 01:47:30.974
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:31.041 --> 01:47:31.474
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:31.474 --> 01:47:34.177
&lt;b&gt;Using this clichéd phrase:&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:34.177 --> 01:47:35.178
&lt;b&gt;"follow your heart."&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:35.178 --> 01:47:37.614
&lt;b&gt;Right. But in many cases&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:38.048 --> 01:47:39.749
&lt;b&gt;right, there's no way around it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:39.783 --> 01:47:42.585
&lt;b&gt;Many of my choices couldn't truly optimize&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:42.585 --> 01:47:44.454
&lt;b&gt;for a result.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:44.454 --> 01:47:47.657
&lt;b&gt;I think this is the source of the disorder.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:48.191 --> 01:47:49.159
&lt;b&gt;So in&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:49.159 --> 01:47:50.760
&lt;b&gt;these disorderly choices,&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:50.760 --> 01:47:53.329
&lt;b&gt;can you string together all of your research journey&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:53.329 --> 01:47:54.664
&lt;b&gt;into a single thread?&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:54.931 --> 01:47:56.933
&lt;b&gt;We've actually already discussed a few works.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:56.933 --> 01:47:57.467
&lt;b&gt;Yes. Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:57.467 --> 01:47:58.435
&lt;b&gt;Yes, right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:47:58.435 --> 01:48:00.336
&lt;b&gt;I think we can go through it bit by bit.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:00.336 --> 01:48:02.539
&lt;b&gt;I think one benefit is&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:02.539 --> 01:48:03.473
&lt;b&gt;I don't have that many papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:03.473 --> 01:48:07.010
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;so maybe it's relatively easy to string together.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:07.010 --> 01:48:10.380
&lt;b&gt;And I think indeed, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:10.380 --> 01:48:13.783
&lt;b&gt;I can't say there's a hidden thread,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:13.783 --> 01:48:16.019
&lt;b&gt;but there really is a thread in the background&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:17.387 --> 01:48:19.055
&lt;b&gt;guiding me to keep doing this.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:19.122 --> 01:48:21.357
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, before talking about these papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:21.524 --> 01:48:22.258
&lt;b&gt;before —&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:22.258 --> 01:48:22.725
&lt;b&gt;I want to say,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:22.725 --> 01:48:25.962
&lt;b&gt;computer vision has developed for such a long time,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:25.962 --> 01:48:27.230
&lt;b&gt;right, I have many friends&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:27.530 --> 01:48:30.934
&lt;b&gt;who are slowly exploring new directions,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:31.167 --> 01:48:34.237
&lt;b&gt;like doing some&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:34.637 --> 01:48:35.972
&lt;b&gt;robotics, right,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:35.972 --> 01:48:38.608
&lt;b&gt;or 3D vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:38.608 --> 01:48:42.078
&lt;b&gt;I'm also trying to expand my boundaries outward.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:42.278 --> 01:48:43.246
&lt;b&gt;But looking back,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:43.246 --> 01:48:44.948
&lt;b&gt;I find on this main thread,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:44.948 --> 01:48:46.616
&lt;b&gt;right, I think this main thread for me&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:46.616 --> 01:48:48.384
&lt;b&gt;— representation learning —&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:48.384 --> 01:48:49.018
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:49.018 --> 01:48:51.254
&lt;b&gt;there are too many unsolved problems. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:51.254 --> 01:48:53.523
&lt;b&gt;So I want to stay on this main thread&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:53.556 --> 01:48:55.725
&lt;b&gt;and push forward what we're doing.&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:56.326 --> 01:48:58.328
&lt;b&gt;So the starting point of all this,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:58.328 --> 01:48:59.829
&lt;b&gt;if we trace it back,&lt;/b&gt;

01:48:59.829 --> 01:49:01.598
&lt;b&gt;of course involves Deep Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:01.598 --> 01:49:02.765
&lt;b&gt;involves Deep Neural Networks,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:02.765 --> 01:49:04.667
&lt;b&gt;the design of these architectures.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:04.667 --> 01:49:05.435
&lt;b&gt;I think this part&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:05.435 --> 01:49:08.438
&lt;b&gt;is of course related to representation learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:08.605 --> 01:49:09.172
&lt;b&gt;Mm. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:09.172 --> 01:49:11.074
&lt;b&gt;this is also what I think, in the past,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:11.074 --> 01:49:12.742
&lt;b&gt;everyone has been working toward.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:12.742 --> 01:49:13.877
&lt;b&gt;Not just me.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:13.877 --> 01:49:15.478
&lt;b&gt;Right. And everyone,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:15.545 --> 01:49:16.613
&lt;b&gt;everyone is doing this —&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:16.613 --> 01:49:19.349
&lt;b&gt;how to design a better architecture&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:19.349 --> 01:49:21.584
&lt;b&gt;so we can learn better representations&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:21.651 --> 01:49:22.952
&lt;b&gt;and better solve&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:22.952 --> 01:49:24.821
&lt;b&gt;problems.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:24.821 --> 01:49:25.321
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:25.321 --> 01:49:27.624
&lt;b&gt;Right. And then, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:27.624 --> 01:49:28.658
&lt;b&gt;later on,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:28.658 --> 01:49:30.260
&lt;b&gt;actually, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:30.260 --> 01:49:32.395
&lt;b&gt;things start to change.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:32.395 --> 01:49:33.196
&lt;b&gt;We find&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:33.363 --> 01:49:36.833
&lt;b&gt;that architecture itself isn't necessarily the most important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:36.866 --> 01:49:38.368
&lt;b&gt;It's definitely important,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:38.368 --> 01:49:39.602
&lt;b&gt;but not necessarily the most important,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:39.602 --> 01:49:41.671
&lt;b&gt;or it's not everything.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:42.272 --> 01:49:44.674
&lt;b&gt;So there are at least several different things&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:44.674 --> 01:49:45.808
&lt;b&gt;that intertwine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:45.808 --> 01:49:47.877
&lt;b&gt;Right, architecture is one thing,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:47.877 --> 01:49:49.412
&lt;b&gt;your architecture is one thing,&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:49.779 --> 01:49:52.015
&lt;b&gt;and your data is also important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:52.015 --> 01:49:52.782
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:52.782 --> 01:49:54.517
&lt;b&gt;And there's also your objective —&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:54.517 --> 01:49:56.319
&lt;b&gt;your goal is also very important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:56.386 --> 01:49:57.654
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:49:57.654 --> 01:50:01.324
&lt;b&gt;I think architecture determines&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:01.324 --> 01:50:02.325
&lt;b&gt;what you use for training.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:02.325 --> 01:50:03.826
&lt;b&gt;We can imagine it as&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:03.826 --> 01:50:06.262
&lt;b&gt;having a massive engine.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:06.362 --> 01:50:08.665
&lt;b&gt;And the hardware of this engine&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:08.665 --> 01:50:10.833
&lt;b&gt;is essentially the architecture of a neural network.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:10.833 --> 01:50:11.634
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:11.634 --> 01:50:13.870
&lt;b&gt;But having just the engine's architecture&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:13.870 --> 01:50:14.871
&lt;b&gt;is actually useless.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:15.138 --> 01:50:16.005
&lt;b&gt;You have no fuel.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:16.005 --> 01:50:17.073
&lt;b&gt;You can't start it.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:17.340 --> 01:50:19.242
&lt;b&gt;Right. So, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:19.242 --> 01:50:20.443
&lt;b&gt;there's the data dimension&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:20.443 --> 01:50:22.045
&lt;b&gt;and there's the objective dimension,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:22.045 --> 01:50:24.647
&lt;b&gt;the objective function considerations.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:25.415 --> 01:50:27.383
&lt;b&gt;And, so&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:27.383 --> 01:50:28.618
&lt;b&gt;My subsequent research&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:28.618 --> 01:50:31.020
&lt;b&gt;has also followed this main thread —&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:31.020 --> 01:50:32.555
&lt;b&gt;representation learning as the main thread —&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:32.622 --> 01:50:37.527
&lt;b&gt;advancing around architecture, data, and objective.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:37.527 --> 01:50:38.628
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:39.128 --> 01:50:40.563
&lt;b&gt;And, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:40.563 --> 01:50:42.966
&lt;b&gt;During the time at FAIR,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:42.966 --> 01:50:43.766
&lt;b&gt;I think FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:44.367 --> 01:50:45.268
&lt;b&gt;— this full-time job,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:45.268 --> 01:50:46.736
&lt;b&gt;in the full-time work process —&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:46.736 --> 01:50:49.872
&lt;b&gt;I think one core aspect was&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:49.872 --> 01:50:51.641
&lt;b&gt;that I worked with Kaiming,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:51.641 --> 01:50:54.043
&lt;b&gt;and Kaiming was leading some&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:54.043 --> 01:50:55.211
&lt;b&gt;self-supervised learning&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:55.211 --> 01:50:56.012
&lt;b&gt;such work,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:56.379 --> 01:50:56.980
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:56.980 --> 01:50:58.514
&lt;b&gt;And actually, again,&lt;/b&gt;

01:50:58.514 --> 01:51:01.517
&lt;b&gt;now everyone says Scaling is&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:01.517 --> 01:51:01.985
&lt;b&gt;is&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:01.985 --> 01:51:02.986
&lt;b&gt;already a buzzword.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:02.986 --> 01:51:04.721
&lt;b&gt;Everybody's talking about scaling.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:04.721 --> 01:51:05.555
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:05.588 --> 01:51:09.058
&lt;b&gt;But actually the first person who really told me&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:09.158 --> 01:51:11.127
&lt;b&gt;that we need a scalable model,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:11.127 --> 01:51:14.397
&lt;b&gt;that we need to make the model bigger and bigger,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:14.397 --> 01:51:15.632
&lt;b&gt;these were Kaiming's exact words.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:15.665 --> 01:51:16.265
&lt;b&gt;Bigger and bigger.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:16.265 --> 01:51:18.067
&lt;b&gt;Right, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:18.067 --> 01:51:19.202
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming told me this.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:19.369 --> 01:51:20.803
&lt;b&gt;What year did he tell you?&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:20.803 --> 01:51:25.008
&lt;b&gt;Uh, roughly around 2018 or 2019.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:25.008 --> 01:51:25.742
&lt;b&gt;Right. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:25.742 --> 01:51:28.144
&lt;b&gt;So from the very beginning his conviction was&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:28.144 --> 01:51:28.478
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:28.478 --> 01:51:32.615
&lt;b&gt;we must make models bigger,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:32.615 --> 01:51:34.450
&lt;b&gt;make data bigger,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:34.484 --> 01:51:36.619
&lt;b&gt;and this would allow us to get&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:36.619 --> 01:51:38.221
&lt;b&gt;a better result.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:38.388 --> 01:51:40.023
&lt;b&gt;I think very early on,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:40.023 --> 01:51:41.124
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming already had this vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:41.224 --> 01:51:42.025
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:42.592 --> 01:51:43.593
&lt;b&gt;Uh. And then&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:43.593 --> 01:51:44.961
&lt;b&gt;so we also&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:44.961 --> 01:51:47.964
&lt;b&gt;made some efforts along this path.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:47.964 --> 01:51:49.766
&lt;b&gt;And so I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:49.766 --> 01:51:52.068
&lt;b&gt;initially, the discussion about self-supervised learning —&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:52.068 --> 01:51:53.569
&lt;b&gt;including Yann,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:53.870 --> 01:51:54.203
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:54.203 --> 01:51:56.272
&lt;b&gt;he's a big advocate.&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:56.272 --> 01:51:57.206
&lt;b&gt;That is, he is&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:57.273 --> 01:51:58.608
&lt;b&gt;very invested in&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:58.608 --> 01:51:59.008
&lt;b&gt;self-supervised learning —&lt;/b&gt;

01:51:59.008 --> 01:52:01.611
&lt;b&gt;He has this classic cake analogy.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:01.611 --> 01:52:02.578
&lt;b&gt;This metaphor.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:02.578 --> 01:52:04.947
&lt;b&gt;Right, the base layer is&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:05.114 --> 01:52:07.216
&lt;b&gt;the body of the cake,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:07.250 --> 01:52:09.919
&lt;b&gt;and this part must be Self-Supervised Learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:10.219 --> 01:52:12.588
&lt;b&gt;On top of that you can have Supervised Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:12.722 --> 01:52:14.991
&lt;b&gt;right, this is the icing on the cake,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:15.024 --> 01:52:18.528
&lt;b&gt;the cream on your cake.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:18.528 --> 01:52:21.564
&lt;b&gt;And further on top is Reinforcement Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:21.564 --> 01:52:22.865
&lt;b&gt;it's just the cherry on top,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:22.865 --> 01:52:24.867
&lt;b&gt;just a little cherry at the very top.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:24.867 --> 01:52:25.768
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:26.169 --> 01:52:29.639
&lt;b&gt;Each layer of this cake is actually important,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:29.772 --> 01:52:31.441
&lt;b&gt;but they're not ranked by importance.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:32.041 --> 01:52:32.642
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:32.642 --> 01:52:35.344
&lt;b&gt;If you don't have the cake's base,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:35.344 --> 01:52:37.914
&lt;b&gt;you can't get to intelligence&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:38.014 --> 01:52:39.415
&lt;b&gt;relying only on the cherry on top.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:39.415 --> 01:52:40.216
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:40.516 --> 01:52:42.552
&lt;b&gt;Right. So because we were at FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:42.552 --> 01:52:43.786
&lt;b&gt;doing vision,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:44.187 --> 01:52:46.589
&lt;b&gt;we were actually paying attention to this very early.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:47.156 --> 01:52:49.425
&lt;b&gt;But the process of this research went like this:&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:49.425 --> 01:52:52.228
&lt;b&gt;around 2015 and 2016,&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:52.528 --> 01:52:55.998
&lt;b&gt;people already knew that self-supervised learning&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:55.998 --> 01:52:57.967
&lt;b&gt;was actually a future for vision.&lt;/b&gt;

01:52:58.634 --> 01:53:00.336
&lt;b&gt;So at that time, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:00.336 --> 01:53:01.237
&lt;b&gt;people would design&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:01.237 --> 01:53:02.672
&lt;b&gt;all kinds of&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:02.672 --> 01:53:05.074
&lt;b&gt;what we call pretext tasks,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:05.074 --> 01:53:07.043
&lt;b&gt;or proxy&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:07.043 --> 01:53:09.345
&lt;b&gt;objective goals,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:09.345 --> 01:53:10.646
&lt;b&gt;some proxy tasks.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:10.813 --> 01:53:12.014
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:12.014 --> 01:53:13.116
&lt;b&gt;what is self-supervised learning?&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:13.116 --> 01:53:16.119
&lt;b&gt;I don't have a label to directly give you,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:16.352 --> 01:53:18.421
&lt;b&gt;unlike ImageNet,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:18.421 --> 01:53:20.123
&lt;b&gt;where I have 1000 classes&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:20.123 --> 01:53:21.424
&lt;b&gt;and can directly train&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:21.424 --> 01:53:23.192
&lt;b&gt;a supervised classifier&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:23.326 --> 01:53:26.662
&lt;b&gt;and get a representation this way.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:26.996 --> 01:53:28.064
&lt;b&gt;In the old days,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:28.064 --> 01:53:29.532
&lt;b&gt;this is what everyone was doing.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:29.532 --> 01:53:32.368
&lt;b&gt;Through 1000 class labels, by the way,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:32.368 --> 01:53:35.138
&lt;b&gt;within these 1000 classes there are 200 dog&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:35.138 --> 01:53:36.572
&lt;b&gt;different breeds.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:36.572 --> 01:53:37.673
&lt;b&gt;Even so,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:37.673 --> 01:53:39.242
&lt;b&gt;this is why&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:39.242 --> 01:53:40.576
&lt;b&gt;ImageNet is so powerful.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:40.576 --> 01:53:42.578
&lt;b&gt;Right? Even with that distribution,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:42.578 --> 01:53:44.380
&lt;b&gt;it can still let&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:44.380 --> 01:53:46.215
&lt;b&gt;our neural networks learn good representations.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:46.249 --> 01:53:47.650
&lt;b&gt;I think this is extremely impressive.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:47.650 --> 01:53:49.786
&lt;b&gt;But people also see the limitations.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:50.153 --> 01:53:52.622
&lt;b&gt;Once everything is just Supervised Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:52.855 --> 01:53:55.792
&lt;b&gt;there are many things you can't capture.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:55.825 --> 01:53:56.826
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:56.826 --> 01:53:58.161
&lt;b&gt;Because what it learns&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:58.161 --> 01:53:59.462
&lt;b&gt;— for example, we're sitting here now,&lt;/b&gt;

01:53:59.462 --> 01:54:00.797
&lt;b&gt;we see these chairs,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:00.830 --> 01:54:01.564
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:01.564 --> 01:54:04.133
&lt;b&gt;and we now have a lot of images,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:04.133 --> 01:54:05.802
&lt;b&gt;of different chairs.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:05.802 --> 01:54:07.436
&lt;b&gt;Some chairs might be quite ordinary,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:07.436 --> 01:54:08.971
&lt;b&gt;chairs in a studio like ours,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:08.971 --> 01:54:10.373
&lt;b&gt;or chairs in a home,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:10.473 --> 01:54:13.009
&lt;b&gt;or some designer chairs,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:13.009 --> 01:54:15.344
&lt;b&gt;right, or like an avocado chair,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:15.344 --> 01:54:16.813
&lt;b&gt;a chair shaped like an avocado.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:17.380 --> 01:54:19.549
&lt;b&gt;For supervised learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:19.549 --> 01:54:21.451
&lt;b&gt;you need to map all of this&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:21.617 --> 01:54:23.319
&lt;b&gt;to a single label,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:23.319 --> 01:54:26.155
&lt;b&gt;this label is called "chair".&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:26.189 --> 01:54:28.925
&lt;b&gt;So what your network has to learn,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:28.925 --> 01:54:30.326
&lt;b&gt;this mapping,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:30.326 --> 01:54:31.961
&lt;b&gt;is actually very, very difficult.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:32.328 --> 01:54:33.129
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:33.229 --> 01:54:34.831
&lt;b&gt;And it's an infinite mapping.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:34.831 --> 01:54:35.932
&lt;b&gt;It's an infinite mapping.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:35.932 --> 01:54:36.766
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:36.799 --> 01:54:41.270
&lt;b&gt;So it can only either memorize,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:41.270 --> 01:54:42.605
&lt;b&gt;just remember,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:42.605 --> 01:54:44.373
&lt;b&gt;recite all the chairs it's ever seen,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:46.209 --> 01:54:48.344
&lt;b&gt;or&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:48.444 --> 01:54:49.812
&lt;b&gt;this,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:49.812 --> 01:54:52.548
&lt;b&gt;through what we call spurious correlations,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:52.548 --> 01:54:55.318
&lt;b&gt;some false correlations,&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:55.418 --> 01:54:56.752
&lt;b&gt;tell you it's a chair.&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:56.752 --> 01:54:58.321
&lt;b&gt;For example, it may not look at the chair itself&lt;/b&gt;

01:54:58.321 --> 01:55:00.756
&lt;b&gt;but look at the background behind the chair,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:00.756 --> 01:55:02.225
&lt;b&gt;or it thinks&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:02.225 --> 01:55:04.460
&lt;b&gt;all chairs will be next to a table,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:04.460 --> 01:55:07.129
&lt;b&gt;so it uses that to make a decision boundary&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:07.129 --> 01:55:07.730
&lt;b&gt;and says,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:07.730 --> 01:55:08.598
&lt;b&gt;hey, this is a chair.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:08.731 --> 01:55:10.199
&lt;b&gt;But this is not what we want.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:10.366 --> 01:55:11.534
&lt;b&gt;What we want to achieve&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:11.534 --> 01:55:15.004
&lt;b&gt;is, from this very diverse visual knowledge,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:15.004 --> 01:55:18.274
&lt;b&gt;these visual observations, to gain some kind of common sense,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:18.274 --> 01:55:22.445
&lt;b&gt;some kind of intuition.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:22.545 --> 01:55:23.779
&lt;b&gt;Mm. Intuition.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:24.046 --> 01:55:26.582
&lt;b&gt;Right. Or some kind of common understanding.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:26.716 --> 01:55:29.819
&lt;b&gt;So this is why people initially wanted to do&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:29.819 --> 01:55:30.953
&lt;b&gt;so-called Self-Supervised Learning&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:30.953 --> 01:55:31.854
&lt;b&gt;or Unsupervised Learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:31.854 --> 01:55:34.824
&lt;b&gt;A common misconception back then was&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:34.824 --> 01:55:35.658
&lt;b&gt;people say&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:35.658 --> 01:55:36.993
&lt;b&gt;we want to do Unsupervised Learning&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:36.993 --> 01:55:40.129
&lt;b&gt;because labeling data&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:40.229 --> 01:55:41.430
&lt;b&gt;is too hard and too expensive.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:41.430 --> 01:55:42.598
&lt;b&gt;We need to hire people&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:42.832 --> 01:55:45.001
&lt;b&gt;to label,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:45.001 --> 01:55:46.002
&lt;b&gt;spending money and time.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:46.002 --> 01:55:47.003
&lt;b&gt;We don't want to do that.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:47.003 --> 01:55:47.436
&lt;b&gt;But that's just&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:47.436 --> 01:55:49.772
&lt;b&gt;one very small part of the problem.&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:49.939 --> 01:55:53.743
&lt;b&gt;The bigger issue is, in the eyes of computer vision researchers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:53.776 --> 01:55:54.143
&lt;b&gt;ah,&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:54.143 --> 01:55:56.245
&lt;b&gt;everyone knew long ago&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:56.245 --> 01:55:57.580
&lt;b&gt;that only through this path&lt;/b&gt;

01:55:57.613 --> 01:56:01.150
&lt;b&gt;there's no way to give AI systems this kind of common sense.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:01.150 --> 01:56:03.519
&lt;b&gt;So in 2015 and 2016,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:03.519 --> 01:56:04.754
&lt;b&gt;everyone was very, very creative.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:04.754 --> 01:56:05.354
&lt;b&gt;That period&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:05.354 --> 01:56:07.323
&lt;b&gt;was actually a quite creative era.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:07.456 --> 01:56:09.158
&lt;b&gt;People would design&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:09.158 --> 01:56:10.860
&lt;b&gt;all kinds of crazy tasks.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:10.860 --> 01:56:11.327
&lt;b&gt;These tasks —&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:11.327 --> 01:56:13.863
&lt;b&gt;for example, you take an image,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:13.863 --> 01:56:14.797
&lt;b&gt;rotate it 90 degrees,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:14.797 --> 01:56:15.831
&lt;b&gt;or 180 degrees,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:15.831 --> 01:56:17.166
&lt;b&gt;or 270 degrees.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:17.400 --> 01:56:19.135
&lt;b&gt;You don't give these images a label,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:19.201 --> 01:56:20.369
&lt;b&gt;but because you designed&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:20.536 --> 01:56:22.605
&lt;b&gt;how to rotate these images,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:22.672 --> 01:56:25.641
&lt;b&gt;right, and these images&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:25.641 --> 01:56:27.043
&lt;b&gt;and their&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:27.043 --> 01:56:28.744
&lt;b&gt;rotation angles&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:28.878 --> 01:56:31.781
&lt;b&gt;can form a valid pretext task.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:31.814 --> 01:56:34.717
&lt;b&gt;You can predict how these rotated images&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:34.784 --> 01:56:36.118
&lt;b&gt;were actually rotated.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:36.519 --> 01:56:38.054
&lt;b&gt;This becomes a so-called&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:38.054 --> 01:56:39.121
&lt;b&gt;proxy task.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:39.121 --> 01:56:39.922
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:39.956 --> 01:56:42.024
&lt;b&gt;Similar proxy tasks also include&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:42.024 --> 01:56:43.426
&lt;b&gt;giving an image,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:43.426 --> 01:56:45.528
&lt;b&gt;converting it to grayscale,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:45.528 --> 01:56:46.929
&lt;b&gt;removing all its colors,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:47.263 --> 01:56:49.565
&lt;b&gt;but then using a neural network&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:49.632 --> 01:56:51.400
&lt;b&gt;to reconstruct the original colors.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:51.400 --> 01:56:52.802
&lt;b&gt;Essentially, from a grayscale image,&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:52.835 --> 01:56:54.370
&lt;b&gt;how do you predict&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:54.370 --> 01:56:56.238
&lt;b&gt;the color of each object&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:56.238 --> 01:56:57.740
&lt;b&gt;as it should be.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:58.040 --> 01:56:58.841
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:56:58.841 --> 01:57:01.143
&lt;b&gt;And there are other similar examples,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:01.143 --> 01:57:02.211
&lt;b&gt;too many to count.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:02.211 --> 01:57:04.914
&lt;b&gt;Another example, one last one:&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:04.914 --> 01:57:05.748
&lt;b&gt;let me give one more example.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:05.748 --> 01:57:07.149
&lt;b&gt;The so-called Context Encoder —&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:07.149 --> 01:57:09.552
&lt;b&gt;you take an image, cut out a piece in the middle,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:09.552 --> 01:57:10.419
&lt;b&gt;make it white,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:10.753 --> 01:57:13.222
&lt;b&gt;and then train a neural network&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:13.356 --> 01:57:15.358
&lt;b&gt;to fill in this empty part.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:15.358 --> 01:57:16.459
&lt;b&gt;Fill it in.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:16.459 --> 01:57:16.826
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:16.826 --> 01:57:20.830
&lt;b&gt;The rationale behind all these pretext tasks is&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:20.830 --> 01:57:22.264
&lt;b&gt;that&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:22.264 --> 01:57:24.367
&lt;b&gt;humans can actually do this.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:24.667 --> 01:57:25.935
&lt;b&gt;The reason humans can do this,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:25.935 --> 01:57:27.169
&lt;b&gt;the reason humans know,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:27.169 --> 01:57:27.570
&lt;b&gt;hey,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:27.570 --> 01:57:30.239
&lt;b&gt;whether this image was rotated 90 or 180 degrees,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:30.339 --> 01:57:33.676
&lt;b&gt;or what color the butterfly&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:33.676 --> 01:57:34.443
&lt;b&gt;or house in this image should be,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:34.443 --> 01:57:35.845
&lt;b&gt;what color should it have,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:36.045 --> 01:57:36.379
&lt;b&gt;or&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:36.379 --> 01:57:39.515
&lt;b&gt;you can predict the information missing in the middle —&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:39.915 --> 01:57:41.050
&lt;b&gt;all these things&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:41.050 --> 01:57:41.784
&lt;b&gt;is because humans,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:42.018 --> 01:57:45.221
&lt;b&gt;based on some understanding of the physical world,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:45.221 --> 01:57:46.322
&lt;b&gt;have this common sense,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:46.322 --> 01:57:48.958
&lt;b&gt;so they can guess&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:48.958 --> 01:57:50.559
&lt;b&gt;these corrupted signals,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:50.559 --> 01:57:52.828
&lt;b&gt;these already lost signals,&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:52.828 --> 01:57:54.764
&lt;b&gt;how they should be reconstructed.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:54.764 --> 01:57:55.798
&lt;b&gt;The masked signals.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:55.798 --> 01:57:56.432
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:56.432 --> 01:57:58.768
&lt;b&gt;But back then the problem was a hundred flowers blooming —&lt;/b&gt;

01:57:58.768 --> 01:57:59.869
&lt;b&gt;all kinds of papers,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:00.236 --> 01:58:01.037
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:01.137 --> 01:58:03.939
&lt;b&gt;but none of them worked well.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:03.939 --> 01:58:06.075
&lt;b&gt;All the results were actually quite poor,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:06.075 --> 01:58:09.578
&lt;b&gt;all worse than ImageNet pre-training,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:09.578 --> 01:58:11.781
&lt;b&gt;by roughly 15-20 percentage points.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:11.781 --> 01:58:13.349
&lt;b&gt;Percentage points.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:13.349 --> 01:58:15.584
&lt;b&gt;So people were making some progress,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:15.584 --> 01:58:17.153
&lt;b&gt;moving forward step by step,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:17.420 --> 01:58:19.588
&lt;b&gt;but the gap&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:19.789 --> 01:58:22.925
&lt;b&gt;uh, what ImageNet could achieve through Supervised Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:22.925 --> 01:58:25.227
&lt;b&gt;learned on large-scale data,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:25.227 --> 01:58:26.429
&lt;b&gt;with labels,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:26.595 --> 01:58:26.996
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:26.996 --> 01:58:29.632
&lt;b&gt;the representation learned with labels,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:29.732 --> 01:58:30.966
&lt;b&gt;was still far, far better.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:31.267 --> 01:58:31.834
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:31.834 --> 01:58:33.235
&lt;b&gt;So, uh,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:33.235 --> 01:58:35.471
&lt;b&gt;we did something at that time,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:35.471 --> 01:58:36.739
&lt;b&gt;and this was done together with Kaiming.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:36.739 --> 01:58:37.173
&lt;b&gt;And this,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:37.173 --> 01:58:38.340
&lt;b&gt;this architecture is called&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:38.340 --> 01:58:39.275
&lt;b&gt;called MoCo,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:39.341 --> 01:58:39.742
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:39.742 --> 01:58:41.210
&lt;b&gt;Momentum Contrast,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:41.377 --> 01:58:42.478
&lt;b&gt;momentum contrastive learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:42.478 --> 01:58:43.112
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:44.046 --> 01:58:45.915
&lt;b&gt;Even the Chinese name sounds interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:45.915 --> 01:58:46.348
&lt;b&gt;Right, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:46.348 --> 01:58:48.718
&lt;b&gt;Yes, momentum contrastive learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:48.718 --> 01:58:49.752
&lt;b&gt;Uh, I think&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:49.752 --> 01:58:51.020
&lt;b&gt;you don't need to dig into&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:51.020 --> 01:58:52.655
&lt;b&gt;the specific technical details.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:52.655 --> 01:58:53.389
&lt;b&gt;Because now&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:53.389 --> 01:58:55.324
&lt;b&gt;much of it is no longer important.&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:55.324 --> 01:58:56.392
&lt;b&gt;But in short,&lt;/b&gt;

01:58:56.392 --> 01:59:00.529
&lt;b&gt;it was the first to take what's called contrastive learning&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:00.529 --> 01:59:01.564
&lt;b&gt;as a framework&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:01.564 --> 01:59:04.033
&lt;b&gt;and make it actually work, as a paper.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:05.234 --> 01:59:06.936
&lt;b&gt;And what is contrastive learning?&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:06.936 --> 01:59:07.436
&lt;b&gt;Also quite simple.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:07.436 --> 01:59:10.673
&lt;b&gt;We're now in this Representation Space,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:10.673 --> 01:59:12.608
&lt;b&gt;in this representation space,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:12.608 --> 01:59:14.176
&lt;b&gt;there are different points.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:14.810 --> 01:59:17.746
&lt;b&gt;These points may be the same object&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:17.913 --> 01:59:19.815
&lt;b&gt;or completely different objects.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:20.182 --> 01:59:21.050
&lt;b&gt;For example,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:21.050 --> 01:59:23.586
&lt;b&gt;I have several images of this chair,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:23.586 --> 01:59:24.453
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:24.453 --> 01:59:26.455
&lt;b&gt;and also some that may be tables,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:26.455 --> 01:59:28.791
&lt;b&gt;or images of cats or dogs.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:29.258 --> 01:59:31.794
&lt;b&gt;These images are all different,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:31.794 --> 01:59:33.229
&lt;b&gt;but in this space,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:33.262 --> 01:59:35.131
&lt;b&gt;we can measure their distances.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:35.331 --> 01:59:36.732
&lt;b&gt;Or we know&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:36.732 --> 01:59:38.367
&lt;b&gt;all these different chairs —&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:38.534 --> 01:59:41.370
&lt;b&gt;their images should be closer,&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:41.370 --> 01:59:43.072
&lt;b&gt;their representations should be closer.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:43.439 --> 01:59:45.774
&lt;b&gt;But a chair and a cat&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:45.774 --> 01:59:47.076
&lt;b&gt;should be farther apart.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:47.143 --> 01:59:48.177
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:48.177 --> 01:59:49.078
&lt;b&gt;So this is the basic&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:49.078 --> 01:59:51.580
&lt;b&gt;logic of contrastive learning.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:51.580 --> 01:59:52.214
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:52.214 --> 01:59:53.382
&lt;b&gt;is actually not new.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:53.682 --> 01:59:54.350
&lt;b&gt;This&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:54.350 --> 01:59:57.286
&lt;b&gt;It's been done for many, many years.&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:57.286 --> 01:59:59.655
&lt;b&gt;By the way, this&lt;/b&gt;

01:59:59.655 --> 02:00:00.823
&lt;b&gt;early work&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:00.823 --> 02:00:03.692
&lt;b&gt;was actually Yann who first worked with his students&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:03.692 --> 02:00:04.793
&lt;b&gt;to do it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:04.793 --> 02:00:06.295
&lt;b&gt;That's very interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:06.295 --> 02:00:07.429
&lt;b&gt;Of course the problem being solved&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:07.429 --> 02:00:09.431
&lt;b&gt;wasn't directly Representation Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:09.465 --> 02:00:11.433
&lt;b&gt;but some Metric Learning problems.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:11.433 --> 02:00:14.069
&lt;b&gt;Some metric learning problems.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:14.069 --> 02:00:15.938
&lt;b&gt;But that's okay.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:15.938 --> 02:00:18.874
&lt;b&gt;This was around 2019,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:18.874 --> 02:00:20.943
&lt;b&gt;I think we gave contrastive learning&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:20.943 --> 02:00:21.877
&lt;b&gt;some new meaning.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:22.044 --> 02:00:25.748
&lt;b&gt;But&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;But this didn't come out of nowhere.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:25.881 --> 02:00:27.082
&lt;b&gt;Actually before that,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:27.249 --> 02:00:29.985
&lt;b&gt;the entire field was slowly moving in this direction,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:29.985 --> 02:00:31.453
&lt;b&gt;expanding.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:31.453 --> 02:00:34.423
&lt;b&gt;For example, there was a paper called CPC,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:34.423 --> 02:00:37.626
&lt;b&gt;and another paper called Memory Bank.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:37.626 --> 02:00:41.063
&lt;b&gt;These two papers were already moving in this direction —&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:41.063 --> 02:00:43.966
&lt;b&gt;using contrastive learning to do&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:43.966 --> 02:00:46.535
&lt;b&gt;self-supervised learning, having already taken several steps.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:46.669 --> 02:00:48.204
&lt;b&gt;Right, and then&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:48.204 --> 02:00:52.808
&lt;b&gt;this is&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;where I can't help but admire Kaiming's ability.&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:52.808 --> 02:00:53.876
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:53.876 --> 02:00:56.212
&lt;b&gt;I think this is also&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:56.212 --> 02:00:58.147
&lt;b&gt;a moment that made me think, wow,&lt;/b&gt;

02:00:58.147 --> 02:01:02.084
&lt;b&gt;what a top-tier researcher&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:02.084 --> 02:01:02.585
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:02.585 --> 02:01:04.687
&lt;b&gt;— or rather, I can't just say top-tier researcher.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:04.687 --> 02:01:06.188
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming in my heart&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:06.255 --> 02:01:09.191
&lt;b&gt;is simply the best researcher.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:09.191 --> 02:01:10.826
&lt;b&gt;How does he actually work day-to-day?&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:10.826 --> 02:01:11.560
&lt;b&gt;Mm, okay.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:11.560 --> 02:01:12.761
&lt;b&gt;I think there are several points.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:12.761 --> 02:01:14.396
&lt;b&gt;Maybe we can briefly talk about it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:14.396 --> 02:01:14.697
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:14.697 --> 02:01:20.135
&lt;b&gt;I think he has a kind of extreme focus.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:20.402 --> 02:01:24.406
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;This focus allows him to have a kind of flow state,&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:24.406 --> 02:01:25.608
&lt;b&gt;called this kind of mind flow,&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:25.608 --> 02:01:29.044
&lt;b&gt;right, he can immerse himself in a problem&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:29.211 --> 02:01:31.580
&lt;b&gt;without needing to consider what's happening&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:31.580 --> 02:01:32.648
&lt;b&gt;in the rest of the world.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:33.215 --> 02:01:34.016
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:34.350 --> 02:01:35.551
&lt;b&gt;And I find this particularly&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:35.551 --> 02:01:36.819
&lt;b&gt;particularly admirable.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:37.052 --> 02:01:39.955
&lt;b&gt;And another thing is&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:39.955 --> 02:01:42.091
&lt;b&gt;how does his focus manifest?&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:42.558 --> 02:01:45.027
&lt;b&gt;I think his focus shows in that&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:45.861 --> 02:01:46.795
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:47.463 --> 02:01:49.598
&lt;b&gt;every day, apart from this one problem,&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:49.598 --> 02:01:51.333
&lt;b&gt;he won't think about anything else.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:51.800 --> 02:01:54.737
&lt;b&gt;He'll grab the people collaborating with him&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:54.737 --> 02:01:55.871
&lt;b&gt;to talk about it,&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:55.971 --> 02:01:58.474
&lt;b&gt;and grab other people to talk about it too.&lt;/b&gt;

02:01:58.474 --> 02:02:01.410
&lt;b&gt;In any case, this topic is the main subject&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:01.410 --> 02:02:02.745
&lt;b&gt;of his thinking.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:02.745 --> 02:02:03.646
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:03.646 --> 02:02:05.948
&lt;b&gt;And most of his mental cycles&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:05.948 --> 02:02:07.049
&lt;b&gt;are allocated to&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:07.049 --> 02:02:08.550
&lt;b&gt;this one specific problem.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:08.817 --> 02:02:09.451
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:09.451 --> 02:02:10.552
&lt;b&gt;This is very difficult.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:10.552 --> 02:02:11.754
&lt;b&gt;I think it's extremely, extremely hard.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:11.754 --> 02:02:12.488
&lt;b&gt;Right, because&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:12.488 --> 02:02:14.690
&lt;b&gt;thoughts are often very hard to control.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:14.757 --> 02:02:15.658
&lt;b&gt;Yes, yes, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:15.658 --> 02:02:16.225
&lt;b&gt;Ah right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:16.225 --> 02:02:18.160
&lt;b&gt;This is related to world models.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:18.160 --> 02:02:19.161
&lt;b&gt;Thoughts are hard to control.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:19.161 --> 02:02:21.230
&lt;b&gt;That's a good point.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:21.230 --> 02:02:22.665
&lt;b&gt;But Kaiming is actually someone very&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:22.665 --> 02:02:24.934
&lt;b&gt;capable of this kind of focused decision-making,&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:24.934 --> 02:02:26.168
&lt;b&gt;able to concentrate.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:26.168 --> 02:02:26.669
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:26.669 --> 02:02:28.604
&lt;b&gt;I actually think there are several points.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:28.604 --> 02:02:31.006
&lt;b&gt;I think a top researcher&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:31.140 --> 02:02:33.742
&lt;b&gt;needs this ability to varying degrees.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:33.742 --> 02:02:35.844
&lt;b&gt;They need sufficient focus,&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:36.245 --> 02:02:37.646
&lt;b&gt;they need good&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:37.646 --> 02:02:39.281
&lt;b&gt;research taste.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:39.281 --> 02:02:39.982
&lt;b&gt;How do you define that?&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:39.982 --> 02:02:41.016
&lt;b&gt;We can talk about it later.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:41.016 --> 02:02:41.550
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:41.550 --> 02:02:44.586
&lt;b&gt;And they also need a certain steadfastness —&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:44.787 --> 02:02:46.755
&lt;b&gt;you can't just go with the flow&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:46.755 --> 02:02:47.556
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:48.023 --> 02:02:51.393
&lt;b&gt;do what others are interested in.&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:51.660 --> 02:02:53.095
&lt;b&gt;And of course&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:53.095 --> 02:02:56.999
&lt;b&gt;you also need strong engineering skills,&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:57.132 --> 02:02:59.201
&lt;b&gt;research intuition,&lt;/b&gt;

02:02:59.201 --> 02:03:01.236
&lt;b&gt;including when you read literature,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:01.236 --> 02:03:02.805
&lt;b&gt;you know what's important&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:02.805 --> 02:03:03.772
&lt;b&gt;and what's not.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:03.772 --> 02:03:04.807
&lt;b&gt;This is very important.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:04.807 --> 02:03:06.575
&lt;b&gt;You also know&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:06.575 --> 02:03:07.209
&lt;b&gt;that this&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:07.209 --> 02:03:08.744
&lt;b&gt;is actually something quite odd&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:08.744 --> 02:03:10.212
&lt;b&gt;about academia.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:10.212 --> 02:03:11.013
&lt;b&gt;That is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:11.013 --> 02:03:12.081
&lt;b&gt;you have to be able to highlight the key points.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:12.281 --> 02:03:13.182
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:13.182 --> 02:03:15.851
&lt;b&gt;The main reason is also that people often don't state them clearly.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:15.851 --> 02:03:18.620
&lt;b&gt;You know?&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sometimes people simply can't articulate the key points,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:18.620 --> 02:03:21.056
&lt;b&gt;sometimes people are unwilling to state them,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:21.056 --> 02:03:21.757
&lt;b&gt;and sometimes&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:21.757 --> 02:03:23.959
&lt;b&gt;people haven't realized what the key points are.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:23.959 --> 02:03:26.028
&lt;b&gt;But Kaiming's ability is&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:26.028 --> 02:03:29.331
&lt;b&gt;he can peel away the layers&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:29.331 --> 02:03:30.065
&lt;b&gt;and extract these key points,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:30.065 --> 02:03:30.933
&lt;b&gt;then tell you,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:31.633 --> 02:03:32.868
&lt;b&gt;and establish&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:32.868 --> 02:03:35.337
&lt;b&gt;these connections in this high-dimensional abstract space.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:35.337 --> 02:03:36.038
&lt;b&gt;These connections.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:36.038 --> 02:03:36.672
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:36.672 --> 02:03:38.240
&lt;b&gt;I find this extremely, extremely impressive.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:38.440 --> 02:03:39.775
&lt;b&gt;Right. So&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:39.775 --> 02:03:40.542
&lt;b&gt;many times&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:40.542 --> 02:03:42.211
&lt;b&gt;each of Kaiming's ideas&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:42.211 --> 02:03:45.381
&lt;b&gt;didn't come from sitting in some corner somewhere,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:45.381 --> 02:03:47.950
&lt;b&gt;dreaming them up at home.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:48.150 --> 02:03:50.185
&lt;b&gt;They actually come&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:50.185 --> 02:03:51.854
&lt;b&gt;from constant exploration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:52.087 --> 02:03:53.756
&lt;b&gt;extensive reading,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:53.856 --> 02:03:55.557
&lt;b&gt;extensive thinking,&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:55.557 --> 02:03:57.192
&lt;b&gt;derived little by little.&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:57.960 --> 02:03:58.627
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

02:03:58.627 --> 02:04:01.296
&lt;b&gt;I think truly deeply&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:01.296 --> 02:04:01.830
&lt;b&gt;—&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:01.830 --> 02:04:03.532
&lt;b&gt;influenced the way I do research,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:03.532 --> 02:04:05.401
&lt;b&gt;and what I now tell my students&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:05.401 --> 02:04:07.236
&lt;b&gt;about how research should be done.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:07.236 --> 02:04:08.670
&lt;b&gt;It's about increasing input.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:08.737 --> 02:04:10.005
&lt;b&gt;Increasing input.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:10.005 --> 02:04:10.572
&lt;b&gt;And&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:10.572 --> 02:04:12.574
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;there's actually a paradigm here.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:12.574 --> 02:04:13.208
&lt;b&gt;Mm, in this,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:13.208 --> 02:04:15.244
&lt;b&gt;this paradigm is also something Kaiming taught me.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:15.244 --> 02:04:16.211
&lt;b&gt;Right, he said&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:16.412 --> 02:04:18.847
&lt;b&gt;actually all these ideas&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:18.881 --> 02:04:21.016
&lt;b&gt;you can't just sit there and think up,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:21.016 --> 02:04:23.051
&lt;b&gt;because if you want to come up with an idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:23.619 --> 02:04:24.420
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:24.653 --> 02:04:26.755
&lt;b&gt;by just thinking, it's definitely not a good idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:27.056 --> 02:04:29.758
&lt;b&gt;There are really only a few possibilities.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:29.758 --> 02:04:32.161
&lt;b&gt;The first possibility:&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:32.161 --> 02:04:33.896
&lt;b&gt;you're smarter than everyone else in the world,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:33.896 --> 02:04:36.265
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;you come up with an incredibly brilliant idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:36.265 --> 02:04:38.534
&lt;b&gt;that no one else can think of.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:38.534 --> 02:04:40.269
&lt;b&gt;But I think the probability of this is extremely small.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:40.636 --> 02:04:43.405
&lt;b&gt;So the more likely two possibilities: first,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:43.405 --> 02:04:45.240
&lt;b&gt;while you're thinking of this idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:45.240 --> 02:04:46.442
&lt;b&gt;100 people,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:46.442 --> 02:04:47.142
&lt;b&gt;1000 people,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:47.142 --> 02:04:48.977
&lt;b&gt;10,000 people in the world are thinking the same idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:49.244 --> 02:04:51.046
&lt;b&gt;So you'll have to compete with them,&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:51.380 --> 02:04:53.248
&lt;b&gt;and your execution speed may not be faster than theirs.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:53.649 --> 02:04:55.284
&lt;b&gt;The second possibility:&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:55.451 --> 02:04:56.985
&lt;b&gt;this is a very bad idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:57.152 --> 02:04:59.054
&lt;b&gt;that others have already tried many times&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:59.188 --> 02:04:59.788
&lt;b&gt;unsuccessfully.&lt;/b&gt;

02:04:59.788 --> 02:05:00.589
&lt;b&gt;unsuccessfully.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:00.656 --> 02:05:01.356
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:01.356 --> 02:05:02.825
&lt;b&gt;Then you probably don't need to try either.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:02.825 --> 02:05:03.992
&lt;b&gt;Mm. So&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:03.992 --> 02:05:06.061
&lt;b&gt;So I think Kaiming's greatest influence on me is&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:06.061 --> 02:05:09.364
&lt;b&gt;he taught me how to find a research idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:09.364 --> 02:05:10.365
&lt;b&gt;Mm. How?&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:10.566 --> 02:05:12.267
&lt;b&gt;I think this is a process of seeking.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:12.668 --> 02:05:13.635
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:13.635 --> 02:05:14.636
&lt;b&gt;Now I,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:14.636 --> 02:05:15.571
&lt;b&gt;when new students come in,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:15.571 --> 02:05:16.071
&lt;b&gt;I will tell everyone&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:16.071 --> 02:05:19.174
&lt;b&gt;about a research cycle.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:19.408 --> 02:05:21.243
&lt;b&gt;Uh, of course I hope it could be longer,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:21.243 --> 02:05:23.745
&lt;b&gt;but in today's competitive environment,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:24.046 --> 02:05:25.647
&lt;b&gt;there might be at most 6 months.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:26.849 --> 02:05:27.783
&lt;b&gt;That is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:27.816 --> 02:05:29.251
&lt;b&gt;from the beginning of 6 months,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:29.251 --> 02:05:30.786
&lt;b&gt;you need to start thinking about an idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:31.153 --> 02:05:32.421
&lt;b&gt;and then later&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:32.421 --> 02:05:34.890
&lt;b&gt;you need to write this idea into a paper&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:35.023 --> 02:05:36.091
&lt;b&gt;and publish it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:36.225 --> 02:05:38.494
&lt;b&gt;This whole cycle is about 6 months.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:40.762 --> 02:05:42.431
&lt;b&gt;What does this process look like?&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:42.564 --> 02:05:44.366
&lt;b&gt;You need to have a general direction,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:44.366 --> 02:05:45.267
&lt;b&gt;you need to know what you want to do.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:45.267 --> 02:05:47.069
&lt;b&gt;You can't know nothing at all&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:47.069 --> 02:05:48.804
&lt;b&gt;just saying "I want to do research" isn't enough.&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:48.871 --> 02:05:49.638
&lt;b&gt;This&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:49.638 --> 02:05:52.841
&lt;b&gt;can be achieved by talking with your advisor,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:52.975 --> 02:05:54.943
&lt;b&gt;or with your peers,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:54.943 --> 02:05:56.945
&lt;b&gt;discussing with your classmates,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:57.012 --> 02:05:58.881
&lt;b&gt;or through your own reading,&lt;/b&gt;

02:05:58.981 --> 02:06:00.883
&lt;b&gt;developing some general direction,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:00.883 --> 02:06:02.818
&lt;b&gt;this directional understanding.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:02.818 --> 02:06:03.819
&lt;b&gt;Mm, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:04.186 --> 02:06:05.053
&lt;b&gt;But&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:05.053 --> 02:06:09.825
&lt;b&gt;you must give yourself enough time and space&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:10.158 --> 02:06:12.227
&lt;b&gt;to explore.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:12.361 --> 02:06:13.395
&lt;b&gt;And this exploration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:13.395 --> 02:06:14.863
&lt;b&gt;this exploration phase,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:14.963 --> 02:06:15.330
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:15.330 --> 02:06:17.366
&lt;b&gt;should last at least one to two months.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:17.432 --> 02:06:18.834
&lt;b&gt;What should you do during the exploration phase?&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:19.067 --> 02:06:19.635
&lt;b&gt;The exploration phase —&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:19.635 --> 02:06:21.436
&lt;b&gt;good question. What do you do during exploration?&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:21.570 --> 02:06:23.338
&lt;b&gt;You can't just sit there thinking.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:23.672 --> 02:06:24.540
&lt;b&gt;What you need to explore is&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:24.540 --> 02:06:27.809
&lt;b&gt;constantly hacking things,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:27.809 --> 02:06:29.044
&lt;b&gt;ah,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:29.177 --> 02:06:29.845
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:29.845 --> 02:06:32.748
&lt;b&gt;you really have to be like a hacker,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:32.748 --> 02:06:34.416
&lt;b&gt;playing with things,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:34.416 --> 02:06:36.251
&lt;b&gt;messing around with things.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:36.251 --> 02:06:38.287
&lt;b&gt;Treat research like a game,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:38.287 --> 02:06:39.555
&lt;b&gt;like a toy to play with.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:39.555 --> 02:06:41.056
&lt;b&gt;Mm, this might involve, for example,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:41.056 --> 02:06:42.691
&lt;b&gt;working through formulas,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:42.758 --> 02:06:44.192
&lt;b&gt;reading more papers,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:44.192 --> 02:06:45.961
&lt;b&gt;finding some connections,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:45.961 --> 02:06:46.295
&lt;b&gt;of course,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:46.295 --> 02:06:49.998
&lt;b&gt;and perhaps more importantly, actually doing things,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:50.465 --> 02:06:52.801
&lt;b&gt;writing code.&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:52.968 --> 02:06:54.970
&lt;b&gt;But when you're writing code,&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:55.270 --> 02:06:57.005
&lt;b&gt;what you need to note is&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:57.005 --> 02:06:57.639
&lt;b&gt;the code you write&lt;/b&gt;

02:06:57.639 --> 02:07:00.809
&lt;b&gt;is not your initial starting idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:00.809 --> 02:07:03.011
&lt;b&gt;or direction,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:03.011 --> 02:07:04.680
&lt;b&gt;but an exploration process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:04.680 --> 02:07:05.447
&lt;b&gt;So the code you write&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:05.447 --> 02:07:07.849
&lt;b&gt;might simply reproduce a baseline,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:08.350 --> 02:07:10.786
&lt;b&gt;take what someone else's paper is doing&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:10.786 --> 02:07:13.221
&lt;b&gt;and reproduce it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:13.589 --> 02:07:14.590
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:14.590 --> 02:07:17.159
&lt;b&gt;And it might also be on the basis of this baseline&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:17.159 --> 02:07:19.027
&lt;b&gt;to make some kind of extension.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:19.027 --> 02:07:19.828
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:19.861 --> 02:07:21.430
&lt;b&gt;And the most important thing in all this&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:21.430 --> 02:07:23.432
&lt;b&gt;is to find a signal.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:23.432 --> 02:07:23.765
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:23.765 --> 02:07:26.635
&lt;b&gt;it's still a bit like what you just said —&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:26.635 --> 02:07:28.337
&lt;b&gt;all of this decision-making process&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:28.537 --> 02:07:31.073
&lt;b&gt;is actually a quite disorderly exploration process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:31.673 --> 02:07:32.641
&lt;b&gt;It's a&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:32.641 --> 02:07:34.142
&lt;b&gt;what we call stochastic gradient descent.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:34.142 --> 02:07:35.544
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:35.644 --> 02:07:38.380
&lt;b&gt;This is a cornerstone of all machine learning,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:38.380 --> 02:07:42.484
&lt;b&gt;but it equally applies to research itself&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:42.484 --> 02:07:43.752
&lt;b&gt;and to our lives.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:43.785 --> 02:07:44.253
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:44.253 --> 02:07:46.355
&lt;b&gt;In everyone's pursuit of their ultimate goal,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:46.355 --> 02:07:48.390
&lt;b&gt;they're all going through a stochastic&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:48.390 --> 02:07:49.691
&lt;b&gt;gradient descent process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:49.691 --> 02:07:50.592
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:50.792 --> 02:07:52.894
&lt;b&gt;And I think research is the same.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:52.961 --> 02:07:53.629
&lt;b&gt;For you,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:53.629 --> 02:07:55.163
&lt;b&gt;the most important thing in research&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:55.597 --> 02:07:58.533
&lt;b&gt;is not going from point A to point B.&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:58.533 --> 02:07:59.534
&lt;b&gt;For example, A is an idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:07:59.534 --> 02:08:00.469
&lt;b&gt;B is a paper,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:00.636 --> 02:08:02.204
&lt;b&gt;but rather in this process,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:02.271 --> 02:08:04.006
&lt;b&gt;what kind of signal can you find?&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:04.206 --> 02:08:04.940
&lt;b&gt;Your gradient,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:04.940 --> 02:08:06.208
&lt;b&gt;where exactly is your gradient?&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:06.475 --> 02:08:08.010
&lt;b&gt;Right. So&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:08.410 --> 02:08:10.812
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming's view is&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:10.812 --> 02:08:12.281
&lt;b&gt;this gradient itself&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:12.281 --> 02:08:15.984
&lt;b&gt;is the source of your real idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:16.752 --> 02:08:18.453
&lt;b&gt;When you've gone through constant exploration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:18.453 --> 02:08:20.255
&lt;b&gt;tried many things,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:20.255 --> 02:08:21.223
&lt;b&gt;possibly unsuccessful,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:21.223 --> 02:08:21.890
&lt;b&gt;possibly successful,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:21.890 --> 02:08:24.092
&lt;b&gt;by the way, it doesn't have to be a successful experiment&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:24.092 --> 02:08:25.027
&lt;b&gt;to give you this gradient.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:25.027 --> 02:08:26.561
&lt;b&gt;Sometimes a failed experiment&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:26.561 --> 02:08:27.996
&lt;b&gt;gives you a larger gradient.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:28.130 --> 02:08:29.131
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:29.131 --> 02:08:30.599
&lt;b&gt;That is, as long as&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:30.599 --> 02:08:32.534
&lt;b&gt;the most feared thing is not knowing which direction to go.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:32.534 --> 02:08:33.335
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:33.335 --> 02:08:34.670
&lt;b&gt;So a good result,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:34.670 --> 02:08:36.004
&lt;b&gt;a bad result,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:36.004 --> 02:08:37.072
&lt;b&gt;are both good results.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:37.072 --> 02:08:38.373
&lt;b&gt;For research,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:38.540 --> 02:08:39.408
&lt;b&gt;a surprise,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:39.408 --> 02:08:41.376
&lt;b&gt;something surprising,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:41.376 --> 02:08:42.577
&lt;b&gt;such an observation,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:42.577 --> 02:08:44.413
&lt;b&gt;is always for a researcher&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:44.413 --> 02:08:45.947
&lt;b&gt;— for a researcher —&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:46.048 --> 02:08:47.215
&lt;b&gt;the most joyful thing.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:47.215 --> 02:08:48.550
&lt;b&gt;Something unexpected that you observed.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:48.550 --> 02:08:50.385
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;You saw something unexpected.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:50.385 --> 02:08:51.186
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:51.787 --> 02:08:53.255
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:53.255 --> 02:08:53.955
&lt;b&gt;he said,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:53.955 --> 02:08:55.757
&lt;b&gt;It's after this kind of exploration&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:55.757 --> 02:08:57.259
&lt;b&gt;In this process,&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:57.426 --> 02:08:58.960
&lt;b&gt;the ideas you discover&lt;/b&gt;

02:08:58.960 --> 02:09:00.696
&lt;b&gt;are the truly your own ideas.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:01.630 --> 02:09:04.032
&lt;b&gt;The idea you started with isn't your idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:04.066 --> 02:09:05.233
&lt;b&gt;That thing doesn't belong to you.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:05.434 --> 02:09:07.636
&lt;b&gt;The idea found in exploration is your own idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:07.936 --> 02:09:09.371
&lt;b&gt;And the research process&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:09.438 --> 02:09:11.973
&lt;b&gt;is about finding&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:11.973 --> 02:09:13.375
&lt;b&gt;your own idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:13.542 --> 02:09:14.242
&lt;b&gt;But&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:14.242 --> 02:09:15.377
&lt;b&gt;this word,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:15.744 --> 02:09:16.845
&lt;b&gt;you need to see&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:16.845 --> 02:09:18.346
&lt;b&gt;it belongs to — this thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:18.346 --> 02:09:20.349
&lt;b&gt;is truly your own.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:20.349 --> 02:09:22.050
&lt;b&gt;Like heaven gave you an inspiration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:22.084 --> 02:09:23.251
&lt;b&gt;injected it into your head.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:23.251 --> 02:09:25.287
&lt;b&gt;Right, on one hand heaven gives you inspiration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:25.287 --> 02:09:25.887
&lt;b&gt;on the other hand,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:25.887 --> 02:09:29.157
&lt;b&gt;it's also based on extensive empirical work and practice.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:29.157 --> 02:09:30.292
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:30.292 --> 02:09:32.427
&lt;b&gt;There's no free lunch here.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:32.427 --> 02:09:33.695
&lt;b&gt;Maybe you're truly a genius,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:33.695 --> 02:09:36.198
&lt;b&gt;or maybe you're extremely lucky,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:36.331 --> 02:09:37.532
&lt;b&gt;God holding your hand&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:37.532 --> 02:09:38.767
&lt;b&gt;wrote this formula.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:38.934 --> 02:09:40.335
&lt;b&gt;It can happen.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:40.335 --> 02:09:42.804
&lt;b&gt;But most of the time, most progress,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:42.838 --> 02:09:44.806
&lt;b&gt;even most work that has great&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:44.806 --> 02:09:46.808
&lt;b&gt;influence on the field,&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:46.808 --> 02:09:50.345
&lt;b&gt;I think still happens step by step.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:50.379 --> 02:09:52.781
&lt;b&gt;You can always trace back&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:52.781 --> 02:09:54.549
&lt;b&gt;to find its starting point.&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:54.549 --> 02:09:56.218
&lt;b&gt;So I also tell students&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:56.418 --> 02:09:59.187
&lt;b&gt;what's actually the worst kind of research?&lt;/b&gt;

02:09:59.287 --> 02:10:02.691
&lt;b&gt;It's when you define a problem at the start,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:02.824 --> 02:10:04.192
&lt;b&gt;say this is my idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:04.593 --> 02:10:06.027
&lt;b&gt;and in the end publish a paper&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:06.461 --> 02:10:07.629
&lt;b&gt;whose idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:07.629 --> 02:10:09.765
&lt;b&gt;is exactly the same as what you started with.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:10.198 --> 02:10:11.633
&lt;b&gt;You didn't encounter any obstacles,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:12.000 --> 02:10:13.402
&lt;b&gt;you didn't encounter any difficulties.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:14.069 --> 02:10:15.270
&lt;b&gt;Why is it the worst?&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:15.437 --> 02:10:16.471
&lt;b&gt;Because this shows&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:16.471 --> 02:10:18.540
&lt;b&gt;your idea is a boring idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:18.673 --> 02:10:21.343
&lt;b&gt;and your published paper is a boring paper.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:21.576 --> 02:10:22.644
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:22.644 --> 02:10:24.946
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:24.946 --> 02:10:26.081
&lt;b&gt;after many years of observation,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:26.081 --> 02:10:27.582
&lt;b&gt;this is indeed very, very accurate.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:27.649 --> 02:10:29.484
&lt;b&gt;So I think this is also why&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:29.484 --> 02:10:30.485
&lt;b&gt;I tell students this —&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:30.485 --> 02:10:30.852
&lt;b&gt;because&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:30.852 --> 02:10:33.655
&lt;b&gt;people sometimes can't accept this fact.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:33.655 --> 02:10:34.823
&lt;b&gt;People always think&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:35.190 --> 02:10:38.193
&lt;b&gt;I should start by thinking of a clever trick,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:38.360 --> 02:10:40.162
&lt;b&gt;then implement it,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:40.162 --> 02:10:41.062
&lt;b&gt;make it work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:41.062 --> 02:10:41.997
&lt;b&gt;publish a paper,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:41.997 --> 02:10:42.597
&lt;b&gt;I've succeeded,&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:42.597 --> 02:10:45.033
&lt;b&gt;and I move on to the next thing.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:45.033 --> 02:10:47.269
&lt;b&gt;But what this can give for personal accumulation&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:47.269 --> 02:10:48.637
&lt;b&gt;is actually very, very limited.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:49.004 --> 02:10:50.772
&lt;b&gt;The exploration process is actually very difficult.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:50.806 --> 02:10:52.374
&lt;b&gt;Many people don't know how to explore.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:52.374 --> 02:10:53.141
&lt;b&gt;Exploration is very hard.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:53.141 --> 02:10:54.476
&lt;b&gt;And this is why&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:54.476 --> 02:10:57.379
&lt;b&gt;all these papers in my view are nonlinear.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:57.379 --> 02:10:59.181
&lt;b&gt;This nonlinearity shows in two aspects.&lt;/b&gt;

02:10:59.414 --> 02:11:03.618
&lt;b&gt;The first is your 6 months of time —&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:03.685 --> 02:11:06.087
&lt;b&gt;by the 5th month,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:06.087 --> 02:11:07.255
&lt;b&gt;like I just told you,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:07.255 --> 02:11:08.023
&lt;b&gt;your mindset collapses.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:08.023 --> 02:11:09.624
&lt;b&gt;This ResNeXt story —&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:09.624 --> 02:11:11.626
&lt;b&gt;on one hand people hear, wow,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:11.626 --> 02:11:13.662
&lt;b&gt;you changed direction in the last month&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:13.662 --> 02:11:15.897
&lt;b&gt;and made it work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:15.897 --> 02:11:18.033
&lt;b&gt;That time period is so short,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:18.033 --> 02:11:19.434
&lt;b&gt;and you still managed to do it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:19.468 --> 02:11:21.002
&lt;b&gt;It sounds unbelievable.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:21.469 --> 02:11:23.438
&lt;b&gt;But once you know this happens too often,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:23.438 --> 02:11:25.574
&lt;b&gt;you find there really is a pattern.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:25.574 --> 02:11:26.541
&lt;b&gt;You often go through this.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:26.541 --> 02:11:27.209
&lt;b&gt;I often go through this.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:27.209 --> 02:11:29.478
&lt;b&gt;Or rather, my best work always happens this way.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:29.845 --> 02:11:32.080
&lt;b&gt;So how do you maintain your mindset for the first 5 months?&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:32.147 --> 02:11:33.281
&lt;b&gt;Uh, there's no way around it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:33.281 --> 02:11:35.350
&lt;b&gt;You have to accept this fact,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:35.350 --> 02:11:39.054
&lt;b&gt;you have to be able to tell yourself&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:39.054 --> 02:11:42.023
&lt;b&gt;this is a normal research process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:42.023 --> 02:11:44.960
&lt;b&gt;Would you consider switching direction in the first 5 months?&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:44.960 --> 02:11:47.762
&lt;b&gt;I might go for that boring idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:47.762 --> 02:11:48.430
&lt;b&gt;I think you would.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:48.430 --> 02:11:50.232
&lt;b&gt;And&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:50.232 --> 02:11:52.434
&lt;b&gt;changing direction is actually very, very important.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:52.467 --> 02:11:54.169
&lt;b&gt;You must learn to pivot.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:54.169 --> 02:11:54.769
&lt;b&gt;Because I just said,&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:54.769 --> 02:11:55.570
&lt;b&gt;the worst work is&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:55.570 --> 02:11:57.172
&lt;b&gt;when your starting idea is the same idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:57.172 --> 02:11:58.440
&lt;b&gt;as your ending idea.&lt;/b&gt;

02:11:58.440 --> 02:12:00.041
&lt;b&gt;The best work is&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:00.208 --> 02:12:03.645
&lt;b&gt;when you've gone all around,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:03.645 --> 02:12:05.213
&lt;b&gt;jumping here and there,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:05.213 --> 02:12:06.882
&lt;b&gt;taken a long, winding road,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:06.882 --> 02:12:08.016
&lt;b&gt;and only then arrived at this point.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:08.016 --> 02:12:08.750
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:08.750 --> 02:12:11.086
&lt;b&gt;Though this road is very bumpy,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:11.086 --> 02:12:13.788
&lt;b&gt;from the final destination&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:13.788 --> 02:12:14.823
&lt;b&gt;step by step&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:14.823 --> 02:12:16.625
&lt;b&gt;you can always trace back to the very beginning.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:16.625 --> 02:12:18.226
&lt;b&gt;Only then can it be connected into a line.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:18.226 --> 02:12:18.627
&lt;b&gt;Only then can you&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:18.627 --> 02:12:20.262
&lt;b&gt;but during the process, you can't.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:20.395 --> 02:12:22.197
&lt;b&gt;Yes, during the process&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:22.197 --> 02:12:22.731
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:22.731 --> 02:12:23.665
&lt;b&gt;you're in the process —&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:23.665 --> 02:12:24.332
&lt;b&gt;because you don't know,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:24.332 --> 02:12:25.166
&lt;b&gt;you can't predict the future.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:25.166 --> 02:12:27.469
&lt;b&gt;So this is always an exploration process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:27.636 --> 02:12:30.472
&lt;b&gt;So I think about two months of exploration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:30.472 --> 02:12:32.207
&lt;b&gt;gradually forming an idea,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:32.374 --> 02:12:33.975
&lt;b&gt;then gradually expanding,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:33.975 --> 02:12:35.610
&lt;b&gt;then scaling up,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:35.610 --> 02:12:36.545
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:36.545 --> 02:12:37.846
&lt;b&gt;then supplementing experiments sufficiently,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:37.846 --> 02:12:38.246
&lt;b&gt;This thing,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:38.246 --> 02:12:40.582
&lt;b&gt;might take another two to three months,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:40.582 --> 02:12:41.349
&lt;b&gt;and finally writing the paper&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:41.349 --> 02:12:43.385
&lt;b&gt;— then spending one to two months —&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:43.418 --> 02:12:43.952
&lt;b&gt;this is&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:43.952 --> 02:12:44.986
&lt;b&gt;already a very&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:44.986 --> 02:12:47.188
&lt;b&gt;smooth research process.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:47.188 --> 02:12:48.156
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:48.290 --> 02:12:49.391
&lt;b&gt;And I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:49.391 --> 02:12:51.793
&lt;b&gt;this, again,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:51.793 --> 02:12:52.894
&lt;b&gt;in today's era,&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:52.894 --> 02:12:54.863
&lt;b&gt;faces many, many challenges.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:54.863 --> 02:12:57.532
&lt;b&gt;People face all kinds of pressure.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:57.532 --> 02:12:59.801
&lt;b&gt;Right? I think the competitive pressure now is too great.&lt;/b&gt;

02:12:59.801 --> 02:13:01.236
&lt;b&gt;The competitive pressure is too great.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:01.236 --> 02:13:02.003
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:02.003 --> 02:13:02.804
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:02.804 --> 02:13:04.139
&lt;b&gt;It makes people feel&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:04.339 --> 02:13:07.609
&lt;b&gt;they must chase the cutting edge&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:07.609 --> 02:13:09.811
&lt;b&gt;and finish things as soon as possible,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:09.811 --> 02:13:10.879
&lt;b&gt;seize the opportunity.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:10.879 --> 02:13:11.546
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:11.546 --> 02:13:12.614
&lt;b&gt;Claim the territory.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:12.814 --> 02:13:15.250
&lt;b&gt;but looking back,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:15.250 --> 02:13:17.252
&lt;b&gt;I think, as I just said,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:17.252 --> 02:13:19.955
&lt;b&gt;Professor Fei-Fei's greatest strength&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:20.555 --> 02:13:24.192
&lt;b&gt;is&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;that she's someone who can define problems.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:24.192 --> 02:13:26.361
&lt;b&gt;If you lose the ability to define problems,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:26.594 --> 02:13:28.964
&lt;b&gt;you essentially also lose much of the ability to innovate,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:28.964 --> 02:13:31.666
&lt;b&gt;essentially also lose the ability to do research.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:31.933 --> 02:13:33.602
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:33.602 --> 02:13:36.671
&lt;b&gt;I just said research is nonlinear,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:36.671 --> 02:13:38.073
&lt;b&gt;that's in terms of time.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:38.139 --> 02:13:39.374
&lt;b&gt;But in terms of results,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:39.374 --> 02:13:40.542
&lt;b&gt;it's also nonlinear.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:40.542 --> 02:13:41.343
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:41.443 --> 02:13:43.011
&lt;b&gt;That is, this&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:43.011 --> 02:13:45.246
&lt;b&gt;is actually MIT professor Bill Freeman —&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:45.246 --> 02:13:47.415
&lt;b&gt;he has a very classic&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:47.415 --> 02:13:48.216
&lt;b&gt;plot,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:48.216 --> 02:13:51.386
&lt;b&gt;an illustration,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:51.386 --> 02:13:52.187
&lt;b&gt;this kind of graphic.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:52.187 --> 02:13:54.389
&lt;b&gt;He often talks about it when giving talks.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:54.856 --> 02:13:55.857
&lt;b&gt;So,&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:55.857 --> 02:13:57.826
&lt;b&gt;This graphic has a horizontal axis&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:57.826 --> 02:13:58.994
&lt;b&gt;and a vertical axis.&lt;/b&gt;

02:13:59.461 --> 02:14:02.597
&lt;b&gt;The horizontal axis starts from a very poor work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:02.998 --> 02:14:04.366
&lt;b&gt;a decent work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:04.566 --> 02:14:05.834
&lt;b&gt;a very good work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:06.167 --> 02:14:08.003
&lt;b&gt;an exceptionally impressive work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:08.003 --> 02:14:09.037
&lt;b&gt;This is the horizontal axis.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:09.804 --> 02:14:10.538
&lt;b&gt;The vertical axis&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:10.538 --> 02:14:14.009
&lt;b&gt;is the impact on your entire career.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:14.009 --> 02:14:16.044
&lt;b&gt;The impact of this paper on your career.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:16.945 --> 02:14:17.879
&lt;b&gt;So you can guess&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:17.879 --> 02:14:19.581
&lt;b&gt;what this curve actually looks like.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:19.581 --> 02:14:21.182
&lt;b&gt;Right? It's not a linear curve.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:21.483 --> 02:14:24.252
&lt;b&gt;It's not that a very poor work&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:24.252 --> 02:14:26.655
&lt;b&gt;has a very bad career impact,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:26.855 --> 02:14:27.555
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:27.555 --> 02:14:28.690
&lt;b&gt;and the best work&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:28.690 --> 02:14:30.291
&lt;b&gt;or a fairly good work&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:30.291 --> 02:14:32.394
&lt;b&gt;gives you a very good return,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:32.394 --> 02:14:33.361
&lt;b&gt;gradually increasing.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:33.361 --> 02:14:34.162
&lt;b&gt;It's not linear.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:34.162 --> 02:14:35.063
&lt;b&gt;It's not linear.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:35.230 --> 02:14:36.331
&lt;b&gt;It's saying&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:36.965 --> 02:14:38.633
&lt;b&gt;basically, a very poor work&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:38.633 --> 02:14:40.201
&lt;b&gt;actually won't hurt you much,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:40.201 --> 02:14:41.569
&lt;b&gt;nobody cares.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:41.569 --> 02:14:41.936
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:41.936 --> 02:14:42.971
&lt;b&gt;No one will notice.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:43.571 --> 02:14:44.806
&lt;b&gt;A decent work —&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:44.806 --> 02:14:45.874
&lt;b&gt;no one notices either.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:45.874 --> 02:14:47.409
&lt;b&gt;The gains it brings you are also small.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:47.409 --> 02:14:48.209
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:48.843 --> 02:14:49.644
&lt;b&gt;But sometimes,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:49.644 --> 02:14:52.580
&lt;b&gt;when you produce a very good piece of work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:52.981 --> 02:14:53.982
&lt;b&gt;an exceptionally impressive work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:53.982 --> 02:14:55.817
&lt;b&gt;work that everyone knows about,&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:55.850 --> 02:14:57.685
&lt;b&gt;your impact&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:57.685 --> 02:14:59.587
&lt;b&gt;— I said I don't like the word impact —&lt;/b&gt;

02:14:59.587 --> 02:15:00.622
&lt;b&gt;reaches the top.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:00.889 --> 02:15:01.456
&lt;b&gt;This thing,&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:01.456 --> 02:15:02.190
&lt;b&gt;this,&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:02.190 --> 02:15:03.591
&lt;b&gt;immediately shoots up to&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:03.591 --> 02:15:04.259
&lt;b&gt;the top.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:04.259 --> 02:15:04.826
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:04.826 --> 02:15:06.461
&lt;b&gt;So we often say in academia&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:06.461 --> 02:15:08.430
&lt;b&gt;what people measure is the so-called signature work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:08.830 --> 02:15:10.131
&lt;b&gt;Or another way to put it:&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:10.131 --> 02:15:10.865
&lt;b&gt;people say&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:10.865 --> 02:15:14.402
&lt;b&gt;what you optimize for is not an average —&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:14.402 --> 02:15:16.171
&lt;b&gt;not the average of all your previous work —&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:16.171 --> 02:15:17.739
&lt;b&gt;an average.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:17.739 --> 02:15:19.040
&lt;b&gt;But&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:19.040 --> 02:15:20.775
&lt;b&gt;what you're optimizing is&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:20.775 --> 02:15:22.210
&lt;b&gt;the maximum of your work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:22.210 --> 02:15:24.379
&lt;b&gt;Right, the highest point.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:24.813 --> 02:15:27.282
&lt;b&gt;I think this illustrates&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:27.282 --> 02:15:29.384
&lt;b&gt;the research game's&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:29.384 --> 02:15:31.219
&lt;b&gt;nonlinear characteristic.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:31.219 --> 02:15:31.986
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:31.986 --> 02:15:33.488
&lt;b&gt;So is the highest point good or not?&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:33.488 --> 02:15:34.289
&lt;b&gt;Of course it's good!&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:34.289 --> 02:15:35.090
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:35.090 --> 02:15:35.557
&lt;b&gt;You&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:35.557 --> 02:15:36.424
&lt;b&gt;you only need to&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:36.424 --> 02:15:38.460
&lt;b&gt;succeed just once in your lifetime.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:38.460 --> 02:15:39.861
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:39.861 --> 02:15:42.397
&lt;b&gt;I actually gave a talk about this at CVPR,&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:42.397 --> 02:15:44.332
&lt;b&gt;I called it research: the infinite game.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:44.332 --> 02:15:44.766
&lt;b&gt;Mm, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:44.766 --> 02:15:45.734
&lt;b&gt;This&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:45.734 --> 02:15:47.035
&lt;b&gt;got quite a strong response from everyone.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:47.035 --> 02:15:49.237
&lt;b&gt;I think actually&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:49.237 --> 02:15:51.272
&lt;b&gt;I rarely give these non-technical&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:51.272 --> 02:15:52.073
&lt;b&gt;talks,&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:52.073 --> 02:15:54.409
&lt;b&gt;because this is more about philosophical thinking&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:54.409 --> 02:15:55.744
&lt;b&gt;and some summaries.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:55.744 --> 02:15:57.245
&lt;b&gt;That one was actually quite good.&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:57.412 --> 02:15:59.280
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:15:59.280 --> 02:16:01.382
&lt;b&gt;But it also&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:01.382 --> 02:16:03.752
&lt;b&gt;contained everything I talked about above.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:03.752 --> 02:16:05.420
&lt;b&gt;Because think about it,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:05.420 --> 02:16:06.621
&lt;b&gt;research as a&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:06.621 --> 02:16:07.455
&lt;b&gt;career,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:07.455 --> 02:16:08.756
&lt;b&gt;a researcher as a&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:08.890 --> 02:16:09.891
&lt;b&gt;profession,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:09.991 --> 02:16:11.059
&lt;b&gt;what is its&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:11.059 --> 02:16:12.794
&lt;b&gt;true essence?&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:13.194 --> 02:16:13.661
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:13.661 --> 02:16:15.563
&lt;b&gt;It's not a chess player,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:15.797 --> 02:16:18.266
&lt;b&gt;it's not&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;even a Winter Olympics athlete.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:18.466 --> 02:16:21.336
&lt;b&gt;Because for a chess player and an athlete,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:21.770 --> 02:16:24.572
&lt;b&gt;your final achievement depends on your worst step&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:25.073 --> 02:16:26.074
&lt;b&gt;to some extent.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:26.407 --> 02:16:27.809
&lt;b&gt;You have to ensure every step,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:27.809 --> 02:16:29.210
&lt;b&gt;your moves must be correct.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:29.544 --> 02:16:31.279
&lt;b&gt;If you make even a small mistake in the middle,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:31.279 --> 02:16:34.215
&lt;b&gt;if you make a small error in chess,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:34.215 --> 02:16:35.583
&lt;b&gt;placed a piece wrong once,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:35.583 --> 02:16:36.151
&lt;b&gt;you've lost.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:36.151 --> 02:16:36.885
&lt;b&gt;You've lost.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:36.885 --> 02:16:37.786
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:37.786 --> 02:16:39.954
&lt;b&gt;So this is a finite game.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:40.088 --> 02:16:41.256
&lt;b&gt;In this process,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:41.256 --> 02:16:42.157
&lt;b&gt;there are always winners&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:42.157 --> 02:16:42.957
&lt;b&gt;and always losers.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:44.159 --> 02:16:46.861
&lt;b&gt;But a researcher is more like an inventor:&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:47.395 --> 02:16:48.396
&lt;b&gt;you in your lifetime&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:48.396 --> 02:16:50.965
&lt;b&gt;truly only need to succeed once.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:50.965 --> 02:16:51.800
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:51.866 --> 02:16:53.001
&lt;b&gt;If you're lucky enough,&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:53.001 --> 02:16:54.269
&lt;b&gt;you can succeed a few times.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:54.269 --> 02:16:57.338
&lt;b&gt;Twice maybe. But you don't need to succeed 100 times.&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:57.338 --> 02:16:58.640
&lt;b&gt;Two times gets you to the top?&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:58.640 --> 02:16:59.340
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:16:59.340 --> 02:17:00.008
&lt;b&gt;I think so.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:00.008 --> 02:17:01.142
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:01.142 --> 02:17:01.876
&lt;b&gt;So I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:01.876 --> 02:17:03.845
&lt;b&gt;this is actually quite interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:03.845 --> 02:17:04.412
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:04.412 --> 02:17:05.079
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:05.079 --> 02:17:08.183
&lt;b&gt;as the entire field moves forward,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:08.183 --> 02:17:10.585
&lt;b&gt;there needs to be some reflection.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:10.585 --> 02:17:12.287
&lt;b&gt;I think now,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:12.820 --> 02:17:14.823
&lt;b&gt;the traditional academic world,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:15.190 --> 02:17:16.825
&lt;b&gt;whether it's its social responsibility&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:16.825 --> 02:17:17.192
&lt;b&gt;Or rather,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:17.192 --> 02:17:19.194
&lt;b&gt;its positioning in the entire research landscape,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:19.194 --> 02:17:20.595
&lt;b&gt;its positioning,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:20.762 --> 02:17:22.797
&lt;b&gt;was always the one setting the rules of the game,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:22.797 --> 02:17:26.668
&lt;b&gt;always the one deciding where we go next.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:27.001 --> 02:17:27.802
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:27.802 --> 02:17:28.870
&lt;b&gt;Now it's completely different.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:28.870 --> 02:17:32.006
&lt;b&gt;Now the ones deciding where things go&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:32.173 --> 02:17:33.374
&lt;b&gt;are OpenAI,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:33.374 --> 02:17:34.342
&lt;b&gt;ah,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:34.342 --> 02:17:36.077
&lt;b&gt;maybe Google,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:36.077 --> 02:17:38.546
&lt;b&gt;or Meta or other major companies.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:38.546 --> 02:17:40.448
&lt;b&gt;Right, they're playing a finite game —&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:40.782 --> 02:17:43.651
&lt;b&gt;they're playing a finite game against each other.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:43.651 --> 02:17:46.888
&lt;b&gt;But this has caused them to drag academia into&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:46.888 --> 02:17:47.956
&lt;b&gt;a finite game,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:48.089 --> 02:17:50.391
&lt;b&gt;this kind of decision-making chain.&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:50.391 --> 02:17:51.192
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:51.226 --> 02:17:51.993
&lt;b&gt;So you see&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:51.993 --> 02:17:54.262
&lt;b&gt;many times when a major company releases something,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:54.262 --> 02:17:56.097
&lt;b&gt;whether it's called some o-series,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:56.097 --> 02:17:58.633
&lt;b&gt;or some GPT series,&lt;/b&gt;

02:17:58.633 --> 02:18:00.168
&lt;b&gt;or the Nano Banana series,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:00.468 --> 02:18:02.470
&lt;b&gt;a specific piece of work,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:02.470 --> 02:18:03.805
&lt;b&gt;a product launch,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:04.072 --> 02:18:07.942
&lt;b&gt;immediately everyone in academia swarms in&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:08.042 --> 02:18:11.379
&lt;b&gt;saying, how can we within this paradigm,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:11.946 --> 02:18:15.049
&lt;b&gt;using what you'd call peanuts of resources,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:15.049 --> 02:18:17.285
&lt;b&gt;resources as few as peanuts,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:17.418 --> 02:18:18.219
&lt;b&gt;these resources,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:18.219 --> 02:18:18.887
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:18.887 --> 02:18:20.455
&lt;b&gt;try to chase it?&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:20.788 --> 02:18:22.023
&lt;b&gt;Oh, chasing.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:22.190 --> 02:18:23.291
&lt;b&gt;What's the point?&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:23.291 --> 02:18:25.460
&lt;b&gt;Reproduce, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:25.460 --> 02:18:28.429
&lt;b&gt;Or maybe people don't believe they can&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:28.663 --> 02:18:30.531
&lt;b&gt;people might also — right, as you said —&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:30.531 --> 02:18:31.599
&lt;b&gt;they probably can't catch up anyway.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:31.599 --> 02:18:32.634
&lt;b&gt;So it becomes some kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:32.634 --> 02:18:34.235
&lt;b&gt;reproduction in a sense,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:34.369 --> 02:18:37.839
&lt;b&gt;or building on top of it through&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:37.839 --> 02:18:38.806
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:38.806 --> 02:18:40.008
&lt;b&gt;this kind of research process&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:40.008 --> 02:18:42.010
&lt;b&gt;is actually very, very painful.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:42.010 --> 02:18:43.211
&lt;b&gt;Because there's one more thing I haven't mentioned.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:43.211 --> 02:18:46.214
&lt;b&gt;For the past two years at NYU,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:46.214 --> 02:18:47.815
&lt;b&gt;I've actually also been working part-time at Google.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:47.815 --> 02:18:48.616
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:48.683 --> 02:18:49.684
&lt;b&gt;Working part-time.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:49.951 --> 02:18:50.952
&lt;b&gt;And this&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:50.952 --> 02:18:52.387
&lt;b&gt;was in the Nano Banana team,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:52.587 --> 02:18:53.955
&lt;b&gt;right, in the Nano Banana team,&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:53.955 --> 02:18:55.890
&lt;b&gt;the team within GenAI.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:55.890 --> 02:18:57.025
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:57.025 --> 02:18:58.226
&lt;b&gt;This went on for two years.&lt;/b&gt;

02:18:58.226 --> 02:19:00.295
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:00.762 --> 02:19:01.996
&lt;b&gt;Not sure if I should share this,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:01.996 --> 02:19:04.666
&lt;b&gt;but let's share. Sometimes I tell some friends,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:04.666 --> 02:19:07.869
&lt;b&gt;the reason I went to do this work at Google&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:07.869 --> 02:19:10.738
&lt;b&gt;is I wanted to see what people at Google were doing,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:10.738 --> 02:19:12.407
&lt;b&gt;so I would know what&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:12.407 --> 02:19:13.441
&lt;b&gt;not to do in academia.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:15.043 --> 02:19:15.843
&lt;b&gt;Oh.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:16.077 --> 02:19:17.779
&lt;b&gt;That is, I need to know what you're doing,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:17.779 --> 02:19:18.579
&lt;b&gt;so I know what not to do.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:18.579 --> 02:19:20.448
&lt;b&gt;Because if I know you're doing this,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:20.448 --> 02:19:21.883
&lt;b&gt;why would I do it alongside you?&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:21.883 --> 02:19:22.550
&lt;b&gt;Makes sense.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:22.550 --> 02:19:23.785
&lt;b&gt;Because they have more resources.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:23.785 --> 02:19:24.285
&lt;b&gt;it has more resources.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:24.285 --> 02:19:25.853
&lt;b&gt;No need to compete with them.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:25.853 --> 02:19:27.155
&lt;b&gt;Yes, yes, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:27.288 --> 02:19:29.657
&lt;b&gt;So this is also something that guides us.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:29.657 --> 02:19:33.561
&lt;b&gt;Right, I don't want to be too preachy.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:33.561 --> 02:19:35.196
&lt;b&gt;By the way, this disclaimer:&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:35.196 --> 02:19:36.130
&lt;b&gt;all of what I've said&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:36.130 --> 02:19:38.132
&lt;b&gt;is only based on my experience at NYU,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:38.132 --> 02:19:39.567
&lt;b&gt;not particularly successful,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:39.567 --> 02:19:42.170
&lt;b&gt;just sharing some experience.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:42.170 --> 02:19:45.673
&lt;b&gt;It doesn't represent the diversity&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:45.673 --> 02:19:46.374
&lt;b&gt;and complexity of research worldwide.&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:46.374 --> 02:19:49.043
&lt;b&gt;And looking back,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:49.043 --> 02:19:50.345
&lt;b&gt;I can also say&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:50.345 --> 02:19:52.146
&lt;b&gt;some papers I do want to&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:52.146 --> 02:19:53.448
&lt;b&gt;share with everyone,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:53.648 --> 02:19:55.783
&lt;b&gt;but looking back,&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:55.783 --> 02:19:59.354
&lt;b&gt;I haven't produced a paper&lt;/b&gt;

02:19:59.754 --> 02:20:02.990
&lt;b&gt;that I truly think has real value.&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:02.990 --> 02:20:05.560
&lt;b&gt;You're saying this to tell everyone&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:05.560 --> 02:20:06.761
&lt;b&gt;I haven't reached the highest point yet,&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:06.761 --> 02:20:08.363
&lt;b&gt;I haven't reached that Max yet.&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:08.363 --> 02:20:09.063
&lt;b&gt;You're right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:09.063 --> 02:20:11.733
&lt;b&gt;I'm still young.&lt;/b&gt;

02:20:11.733 --> 02:20:13.067
&lt;b&gt;[laughs]&lt;/b&gt;

