02:26:06.754 --> 02:26:07.388
&lt;b&gt;V1, V2,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:07.388 --> 02:26:08.189
&lt;b&gt;V3, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:08.189 --> 02:26:09.223
&lt;b&gt;And in V3,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:09.223 --> 02:26:10.091
&lt;b&gt;we used Transformer,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:10.858 --> 02:26:11.959
&lt;b&gt;and we scaled up,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:12.293 --> 02:26:12.893
&lt;b&gt;Uh,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:12.893 --> 02:26:15.696
&lt;b&gt;actually already better than the representation ImageNet could get,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:15.696 --> 02:26:18.499
&lt;b&gt;across all kinds of tasks.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:18.499 --> 02:26:20.568
&lt;b&gt;This for us was&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:20.568 --> 02:26:22.470
&lt;b&gt;actually a major surprise.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:22.470 --> 02:26:23.337
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:23.437 --> 02:26:24.238
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:24.238 --> 02:26:25.573
&lt;b&gt;At that time,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:25.573 --> 02:26:26.841
&lt;b&gt;at that point,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:27.041 --> 02:26:28.676
&lt;b&gt;I thought, wow,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:28.676 --> 02:26:31.412
&lt;b&gt;everything is flourishing again.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:31.412 --> 02:26:34.415
&lt;b&gt;Our problem can basically be answered.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:34.749 --> 02:26:36.183
&lt;b&gt;We found a way —&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:36.183 --> 02:26:36.884
&lt;b&gt;self-supervised learning —&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:36.884 --> 02:26:37.752
&lt;b&gt;that can work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:37.952 --> 02:26:38.285
&lt;b&gt;Going forward,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:38.285 --> 02:26:40.621
&lt;b&gt;we just need to scale up what we're doing now,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:40.788 --> 02:26:41.222
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:41.222 --> 02:26:42.857
&lt;b&gt;the future is incredibly bright.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:42.857 --> 02:26:43.891
&lt;b&gt;But unfortunately,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:43.891 --> 02:26:44.959
&lt;b&gt;this also didn't happen.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:44.959 --> 02:26:45.960
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:45.993 --> 02:26:47.027
&lt;b&gt;But before that,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:47.027 --> 02:26:47.962
&lt;b&gt;we had another paper,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:47.962 --> 02:26:48.863
&lt;b&gt;also&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:48.863 --> 02:26:51.966
&lt;b&gt;MoCo and MAE by the way were both projects Kaiming led.&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:52.166 --> 02:26:54.235
&lt;b&gt;Actually, people say&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:54.235 --> 02:26:55.469
&lt;b&gt;what does it mean to lead a project?&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:55.469 --> 02:26:56.937
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:56.937 --> 02:26:59.039
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming truly demonstrated this leadership —&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:59.039 --> 02:26:59.840
&lt;b&gt;that is,&lt;/b&gt;

02:26:59.840 --> 02:27:04.412
&lt;b&gt;he truly took on 80-90% of the first-author&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:04.412 --> 02:27:05.613
&lt;b&gt;plus last-author&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:05.613 --> 02:27:07.081
&lt;b&gt;or corresponding-author responsibilities.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:07.081 --> 02:27:08.883
&lt;b&gt;The corresponding author's responsibilities.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:09.083 --> 02:27:12.052
&lt;b&gt;He needed to write the baseline himself,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:12.086 --> 02:27:14.422
&lt;b&gt;run many, many experiments himself,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:14.555 --> 02:27:16.490
&lt;b&gt;finalize the paper himself,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:16.490 --> 02:27:18.092
&lt;b&gt;tell the story, present it,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:18.092 --> 02:27:20.027
&lt;b&gt;all of these things&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:20.027 --> 02:27:22.863
&lt;b&gt;basically Kaiming did single-handedly.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:22.963 --> 02:27:23.798
&lt;b&gt;And accomplished it.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:23.798 --> 02:27:25.032
&lt;b&gt;So what about others?&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:25.032 --> 02:27:26.133
&lt;b&gt;Others,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:26.167 --> 02:27:27.134
&lt;b&gt;we&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:27.134 --> 02:27:28.536
&lt;b&gt;of course also participated&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:28.536 --> 02:27:29.370
&lt;b&gt;and made contributions.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:29.370 --> 02:27:30.137
&lt;b&gt;But I'm just saying&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:30.137 --> 02:27:32.339
&lt;b&gt;this is a path Kaiming led.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:32.406 --> 02:27:34.275
&lt;b&gt;Right, we&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:34.742 --> 02:27:38.546
&lt;b&gt;accelerated the progress of this,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:38.546 --> 02:27:40.214
&lt;b&gt;and may have made the results much better too.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:40.214 --> 02:27:40.881
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:40.881 --> 02:27:42.683
&lt;b&gt;But it doesn't change the essence of this.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:42.983 --> 02:27:44.285
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:44.285 --> 02:27:45.586
&lt;b&gt;So this is Kaiming.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:45.586 --> 02:27:48.155
&lt;b&gt;Even now, for example, just a couple days ago he told me&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:48.255 --> 02:27:49.690
&lt;b&gt;he really enjoys this kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:49.690 --> 02:27:51.492
&lt;b&gt;IC work — individual contributor,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:51.492 --> 02:27:53.160
&lt;b&gt;the individual contributor&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:53.160 --> 02:27:54.695
&lt;b&gt;type of role.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:54.695 --> 02:27:55.262
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:55.262 --> 02:27:58.165
&lt;b&gt;He doesn't enjoy managing a large team,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:58.165 --> 02:27:58.999
&lt;b&gt;getting everyone together,&lt;/b&gt;

02:27:58.999 --> 02:28:01.335
&lt;b&gt;just being a manager pointing the direction.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:01.335 --> 02:28:02.636
&lt;b&gt;He doesn't like that.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:02.636 --> 02:28:03.838
&lt;b&gt;How many people does he manage now?&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:03.838 --> 02:28:04.839
&lt;b&gt;He has many, many people.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:04.839 --> 02:28:06.640
&lt;b&gt;He now has many undergraduates&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:06.640 --> 02:28:07.475
&lt;b&gt;visiting him,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:07.641 --> 02:28:09.543
&lt;b&gt;and he&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:09.543 --> 02:28:11.412
&lt;b&gt;is also doing a lot of really great work.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:11.412 --> 02:28:12.646
&lt;b&gt;So I actually don't believe him.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:12.646 --> 02:28:13.480
&lt;b&gt;I tell him,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:13.480 --> 02:28:15.649
&lt;b&gt;"You're actually a very good manager."&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:15.649 --> 02:28:17.184
&lt;b&gt;At least for me,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:17.184 --> 02:28:18.652
&lt;b&gt;even though you never really managed me,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:18.652 --> 02:28:21.689
&lt;b&gt;just being around you,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:21.689 --> 02:28:25.826
&lt;b&gt;I could feel my own efficiency improving,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:25.826 --> 02:28:27.862
&lt;b&gt;feeling like I was getting smarter.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:27.962 --> 02:28:28.496
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:28.496 --> 02:28:30.531
&lt;b&gt;If I were going to have a manager,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:30.531 --> 02:28:32.967
&lt;b&gt;I'd want one like that —&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:32.967 --> 02:28:37.238
&lt;b&gt;Right?&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;one who can empower the people around him to get better.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:37.438 --> 02:28:38.072
&lt;b&gt;Right.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:38.072 --> 02:28:39.073
&lt;b&gt;I think this is Kaiming.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:39.073 --> 02:28:40.341
&lt;b&gt;So MAE —&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:40.341 --> 02:28:41.275
&lt;b&gt;in any case,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:41.408 --> 02:28:43.644
&lt;b&gt;we explored the Contrastive Learning path,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:43.644 --> 02:28:44.545
&lt;b&gt;and found&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:44.545 --> 02:28:46.046
&lt;b&gt;it couldn't scale up.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:46.046 --> 02:28:47.481
&lt;b&gt;So we wanted to switch directions.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:47.681 --> 02:28:49.083
&lt;b&gt;So we went back&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:49.083 --> 02:28:50.417
&lt;b&gt;and used a simpler approach,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:50.417 --> 02:28:52.486
&lt;b&gt;which is a kind of denoising&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:52.486 --> 02:28:53.354
&lt;b&gt;autoencoder,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:53.354 --> 02:28:55.055
&lt;b&gt;this kind of autoencoder,&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:55.055 --> 02:28:57.892
&lt;b&gt;the Masked Autoencoder (MAE).&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:57.892 --> 02:28:59.026
&lt;b&gt;This method is even simpler.&lt;/b&gt;

02:28:59.026 --> 02:29:01.195
&lt;b&gt;Everyone can go read the paper,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:01.395 --> 02:29:02.897
&lt;b&gt;But in short,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:02.897 --> 02:29:04.598
&lt;b&gt;but basically by taking some images&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:04.598 --> 02:29:06.000
&lt;b&gt;and corrupting them,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:06.000 --> 02:29:08.469
&lt;b&gt;then reconstructing&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:08.469 --> 02:29:09.436
&lt;b&gt;these&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:09.436 --> 02:29:10.571
&lt;b&gt;noisy images,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:10.571 --> 02:29:11.539
&lt;b&gt;cropped images,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:11.539 --> 02:29:13.340
&lt;b&gt;or masked images,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:13.340 --> 02:29:13.974
&lt;b&gt;to learn representations.&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:13.974 --> 02:29:14.408
&lt;b&gt;Mm.&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:14.408 --> 02:29:15.009
&lt;b&gt;This&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:15.009 --> 02:29:17.778
&lt;b&gt;fundamentally different from Contrastive Learning,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:17.878 --> 02:29:19.747
&lt;b&gt;but its results were also very good,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:19.747 --> 02:29:21.749
&lt;b&gt;although it has very different characteristics.&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:21.815 --> 02:29:23.951
&lt;b&gt;For example, it doesn't explicitly&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:23.951 --> 02:29:25.486
&lt;b&gt;model certain environments&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:25.486 --> 02:29:26.954
&lt;b&gt;this kind of invariance&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:27.021 --> 02:29:29.490
&lt;b&gt;which causes it, when doing linear probing,&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:29.490 --> 02:29:31.425
&lt;b&gt;to perform slightly worse&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:31.425 --> 02:29:34.194
&lt;b&gt;but with untuned fine-tuning&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:34.194 --> 02:29:35.963
&lt;b&gt;these are two different ways to test representations&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:36.030 --> 02:29:38.065
&lt;b&gt;right, in that case the results would be much better&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:38.232 --> 02:29:39.667
&lt;b&gt;in any case, they have different properties&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:39.667 --> 02:29:41.869
&lt;b&gt;the representations they learn also look different&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:42.036 --> 02:29:44.038
&lt;b&gt;and these things&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:44.338 --> 02:29:46.240
&lt;b&gt;would have far-reaching consequences down the line&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:46.240 --> 02:29:47.408
&lt;b&gt;we can talk more about this later&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:47.408 --> 02:29:48.576
&lt;b&gt;but this was MAE&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:48.576 --> 02:29:49.810
&lt;b&gt;at the time we thought&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:49.810 --> 02:29:52.246
&lt;b&gt;wow, MAE is incredible&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:52.246 --> 02:29:54.982
&lt;b&gt;MAE should at least win a best paper award, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:54.982 --> 02:29:57.051
&lt;b&gt;turns out it didn't&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:57.051 --> 02:29:59.586
&lt;b&gt;scaling up MAE would solve all problems, right?&lt;/b&gt;

02:29:59.586 --> 02:30:02.056
&lt;b&gt;turned out it didn't scale up either&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:02.056 --> 02:30:02.690
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:02.690 --> 02:30:04.491
&lt;b&gt;actually I heard&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:04.491 --> 02:30:06.694
&lt;b&gt;you and Xiangyu (chief scientist at StepFun) had talked about this before&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:06.694 --> 02:30:08.696
&lt;b&gt;because he also paid attention to self-supervised learning&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:08.696 --> 02:30:10.297
&lt;b&gt;he actually also&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:10.297 --> 02:30:10.931
&lt;b&gt;talked a lot about&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:10.931 --> 02:30:13.334
&lt;b&gt;why self-supervised learning can't scale up&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:13.334 --> 02:30:14.168
&lt;b&gt;some of the reasons&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:14.168 --> 02:30:16.337
&lt;b&gt;I won't go into it again here&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:16.337 --> 02:30:18.739
&lt;b&gt;feel free to go back and relisten to that episode&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:18.806 --> 02:30:19.807
&lt;b&gt;but anyway, in short,&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:19.807 --> 02:30:23.043
&lt;b&gt;back then there was this kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:23.043 --> 02:30:24.211
&lt;b&gt;rollercoaster ride&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:24.211 --> 02:30:27.081
&lt;b&gt;on the one hand, we got some really good results&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:27.247 --> 02:30:30.884
&lt;b&gt;but on the other hand, these papers were just papers&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:30.884 --> 02:30:31.986
&lt;b&gt;we were never able to&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:31.986 --> 02:30:34.621
&lt;b&gt;truly deliver something real&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:34.688 --> 02:30:37.391
&lt;b&gt;right, like GPT&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:37.491 --> 02:30:39.493
&lt;b&gt;that could point everything toward a completely different&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:39.593 --> 02:30:41.495
&lt;b&gt;scalable paradigm for the future&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:41.495 --> 02:30:42.396
&lt;b&gt;yeah, right&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:42.429 --> 02:30:43.630
&lt;b&gt;I think this whole thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:43.831 --> 02:30:46.400
&lt;b&gt;had, at that point, kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:46.400 --> 02:30:47.067
&lt;b&gt;come to a close&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:47.067 --> 02:30:47.835
&lt;b&gt;of course, at that time I also did&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:47.835 --> 02:30:48.869
&lt;b&gt;some other work&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:48.869 --> 02:30:51.538
&lt;b&gt;for example, I extended self-supervised learning&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:51.538 --> 02:30:52.873
&lt;b&gt;for what you could call the first time&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:52.873 --> 02:30:53.874
&lt;b&gt;into the 3D domain, for instance&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:53.874 --> 02:30:56.443
&lt;b&gt;I also did some work on point clouds&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:56.443 --> 02:30:57.611
&lt;b&gt;these&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:57.611 --> 02:30:58.812
&lt;b&gt;were called Point Contrast&lt;/b&gt;

02:30:58.812 --> 02:31:01.548
&lt;b&gt;but these works were perhaps more about&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:01.548 --> 02:31:03.650
&lt;b&gt;demonstrating that representation learning&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:03.650 --> 02:31:04.585
&lt;b&gt;as a concept&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:04.818 --> 02:31:07.087
&lt;b&gt;is not just a problem for the image domain&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:07.388 --> 02:31:09.757
&lt;b&gt;it's a very universal&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:09.757 --> 02:31:11.492
&lt;b&gt;approach&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:11.492 --> 02:31:12.659
&lt;b&gt;or rather, a methodology&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:13.093 --> 02:31:14.895
&lt;b&gt;it doesn't only work with images&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:15.029 --> 02:31:16.630
&lt;b&gt;it also works in 3D space&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:16.697 --> 02:31:17.031
&lt;b&gt;later on&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:17.031 --> 02:31:19.533
&lt;b&gt;many people tried it on all kinds of medical imaging&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:19.733 --> 02:31:22.369
&lt;b&gt;and also on robotics tasks&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:22.836 --> 02:31:23.671
&lt;b&gt;all kinds of domains&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:23.671 --> 02:31:24.471
&lt;b&gt;it holds up&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:24.672 --> 02:31:25.773
&lt;b&gt;so this thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:26.006 --> 02:31:29.843
&lt;b&gt;I don't see it as a failure&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:29.843 --> 02:31:32.079
&lt;b&gt;because it really has been&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:32.079 --> 02:31:33.681
&lt;b&gt;influencing many many different&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:33.681 --> 02:31:36.250
&lt;b&gt;fields beyond what we were focused on&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:36.250 --> 02:31:39.553
&lt;b&gt;like computer vision itself&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:39.553 --> 02:31:40.754
&lt;b&gt;but on the other hand&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:40.954 --> 02:31:43.424
&lt;b&gt;it still hasn't achieved the same kind of impact as LLMs&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:43.424 --> 02:31:45.059
&lt;b&gt;in terms of influence&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:45.059 --> 02:31:45.859
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:46.960 --> 02:31:48.429
&lt;b&gt;so then&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:48.429 --> 02:31:51.031
&lt;b&gt;after all that, what came next?&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:51.198 --> 02:31:51.999
&lt;b&gt;right, yeah&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:51.999 --> 02:31:54.835
&lt;b&gt;it seems like we went back to&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:54.968 --> 02:31:56.070
&lt;b&gt;an exploration phase&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:56.437 --> 02:31:57.805
&lt;b&gt;all of this was at FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:57.805 --> 02:31:59.740
&lt;b&gt;all done at FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

02:31:59.740 --> 02:32:01.175
&lt;b&gt;you were there for 4 years during that phase&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:01.175 --> 02:32:01.742
&lt;b&gt;4 years&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:01.742 --> 02:32:02.643
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:02.976 --> 02:32:05.979
&lt;b&gt;so was that the end of your FAIR chapter?&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:05.979 --> 02:32:06.447
&lt;b&gt;not yet&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:06.447 --> 02:32:07.014
&lt;b&gt;still early, still early&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:07.014 --> 02:32:10.417
&lt;b&gt;that was probably the first year or two&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:10.551 --> 02:32:11.018
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:11.018 --> 02:32:13.687
&lt;b&gt;there's another fun story,&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:13.687 --> 02:32:15.689
&lt;b&gt;let me brag about Kaiming again&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:15.689 --> 02:32:16.490
&lt;b&gt;[laughter]&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:17.191 --> 02:32:18.358
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:18.358 --> 02:32:20.627
&lt;b&gt;back then, resources were always an issue&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:20.627 --> 02:32:22.162
&lt;b&gt;GPUs were always in short supply&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:22.296 --> 02:32:23.897
&lt;b&gt;and then FAIR made a decision&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:23.897 --> 02:32:26.433
&lt;b&gt;to give TPUs a try&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:26.433 --> 02:32:27.401
&lt;b&gt;see if this thing is any good&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:27.401 --> 02:32:28.736
&lt;b&gt;Google had been using them&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:28.736 --> 02:32:29.069
&lt;b&gt;they&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:29.069 --> 02:32:31.572
&lt;b&gt;had fully transitioned to using TPUs&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:32.372 --> 02:32:33.874
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:33.874 --> 02:32:37.745
&lt;b&gt;we got about 5,000 TPU chips&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:37.745 --> 02:32:39.079
&lt;b&gt;these chips&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:39.079 --> 02:32:40.147
&lt;b&gt;not bought, more like rented&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:40.147 --> 02:32:42.082
&lt;b&gt;on Google Cloud&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:42.349 --> 02:32:42.783
&lt;b&gt;and then&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:42.783 --> 02:32:45.552
&lt;b&gt;it was originally set up for people doing language models&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:45.753 --> 02:32:47.354
&lt;b&gt;people played around with it&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:47.354 --> 02:32:47.888
&lt;b&gt;and quickly found&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:47.888 --> 02:32:48.622
&lt;b&gt;ugh, it's way too hard to use&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:48.622 --> 02:32:49.556
&lt;b&gt;really not user-friendly&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:49.823 --> 02:32:50.557
&lt;b&gt;okay&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:50.557 --> 02:32:53.026
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming stepped up and said, let me handle it&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:53.026 --> 02:32:56.797
&lt;b&gt;so he truly, single-handedly&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:56.797 --> 02:32:58.432
&lt;b&gt;I mean, again&lt;/b&gt;

02:32:58.432 --> 02:33:00.167
&lt;b&gt;all on his own&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:00.167 --> 02:33:01.969
&lt;b&gt;from start to finish&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:02.035 --> 02:33:05.005
&lt;b&gt;built an entire infrastructure on TPUs&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:05.239 --> 02:33:06.406
&lt;b&gt;which enabled us to do&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:06.406 --> 02:33:08.208
&lt;b&gt;all the subsequent work&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:08.208 --> 02:33:09.276
&lt;b&gt;including MoCo&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:09.276 --> 02:33:11.345
&lt;b&gt;including MAE&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:11.345 --> 02:33:13.781
&lt;b&gt;including the later DiT&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:13.981 --> 02:33:15.082
&lt;b&gt;all of it&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:15.082 --> 02:33:16.283
&lt;b&gt;happened on top of TPUs&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:16.583 --> 02:33:18.085
&lt;b&gt;so for me, this was&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:18.085 --> 02:33:21.889
&lt;b&gt;a really important lesson&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:21.889 --> 02:33:22.756
&lt;b&gt;which is&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:23.891 --> 02:33:25.058
&lt;b&gt;how to summarize it...&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:26.960 --> 02:33:28.395
&lt;b&gt;it's like&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:28.395 --> 02:33:29.296
&lt;b&gt;a craftsman who wants to do good work&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:29.296 --> 02:33:30.230
&lt;b&gt;must first sharpen his tools&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:30.931 --> 02:33:31.598
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:31.598 --> 02:33:35.202
&lt;b&gt;one thing Kaiming taught me was&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:36.003 --> 02:33:37.671
&lt;b&gt;the ceiling of your research&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:37.671 --> 02:33:39.807
&lt;b&gt;actually depends on how good your baseline is&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:39.940 --> 02:33:40.741
&lt;b&gt;oh&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:41.575 --> 02:33:43.443
&lt;b&gt;because if your baseline is weak&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:43.544 --> 02:33:45.612
&lt;b&gt;you can easily fool yourself&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:46.013 --> 02:33:46.580
&lt;b&gt;oh&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:46.580 --> 02:33:48.048
&lt;b&gt;you won't produce anything meaningful&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:48.081 --> 02:33:50.250
&lt;b&gt;if you haven't put enough thought&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:50.350 --> 02:33:51.985
&lt;b&gt;into the baseline level&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:51.985 --> 02:33:55.689
&lt;b&gt;into building this system properly&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:55.689 --> 02:33:57.825
&lt;b&gt;into pushing the engineering to its limits&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:58.091 --> 02:33:59.760
&lt;b&gt;you don't have a platform&lt;/b&gt;

02:33:59.760 --> 02:34:01.395
&lt;b&gt;to do real exploration&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:01.628 --> 02:34:04.832
&lt;b&gt;because you might find an interesting&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:04.832 --> 02:34:06.066
&lt;b&gt;seemingly valuable signal&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:06.333 --> 02:34:07.835
&lt;b&gt;but that signal could be completely wrong&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:07.835 --> 02:34:09.336
&lt;b&gt;the reason being your baseline&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:09.570 --> 02:34:11.805
&lt;b&gt;your benchmark itself wasn't good enough&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:11.805 --> 02:34:12.940
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:12.940 --> 02:34:14.875
&lt;b&gt;so this is actually quite counterintuitive&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:15.075 --> 02:34:15.976
&lt;b&gt;because people always say&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:15.976 --> 02:34:17.411
&lt;b&gt;if my baseline is a bit weaker&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:17.444 --> 02:34:18.979
&lt;b&gt;then the performance gains I can show&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:18.979 --> 02:34:20.581
&lt;b&gt;would be larger&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:20.581 --> 02:34:22.049
&lt;b&gt;so it's easier for me to publish papers&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:22.382 --> 02:34:25.219
&lt;b&gt;right, but Kaiming doesn't think this way&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:25.219 --> 02:34:26.019
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:26.053 --> 02:34:27.087
&lt;b&gt;he thinks about how to&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:27.087 --> 02:34:29.623
&lt;b&gt;push the baseline as high as it can go&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:30.257 --> 02:34:31.992
&lt;b&gt;and then starting from that foundation&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:31.992 --> 02:34:33.460
&lt;b&gt;whatever new things we build&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:34.027 --> 02:34:35.495
&lt;b&gt;that's groundbreaking work&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:35.495 --> 02:34:37.030
&lt;b&gt;that's a genuine breakthrough&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:37.397 --> 02:34:38.165
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:38.165 --> 02:34:39.967
&lt;b&gt;anything you build on top of a weak baseline&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:39.967 --> 02:34:41.201
&lt;b&gt;any improvement&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:41.401 --> 02:34:43.437
&lt;b&gt;might just be a throwaway paper&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:43.770 --> 02:34:45.272
&lt;b&gt;so this thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:45.339 --> 02:34:46.974
&lt;b&gt;has also been an inspiration to me&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:46.974 --> 02:34:49.810
&lt;b&gt;including when they were working on detection&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:49.810 --> 02:34:51.445
&lt;b&gt;I wasn't part of that work&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:51.445 --> 02:34:52.746
&lt;b&gt;I was still doing my PhD&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:52.813 --> 02:34:54.815
&lt;b&gt;but all of those&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:54.881 --> 02:34:57.684
&lt;b&gt;Fast R-CNN, Mask R-CNN&lt;/b&gt;

02:34:57.751 --> 02:35:00.587
&lt;b&gt;Focal Loss, and the whole series of work&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:00.587 --> 02:35:03.290
&lt;b&gt;all of that work was because they&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:03.290 --> 02:35:04.891
&lt;b&gt;including Ross Girshick&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:04.891 --> 02:35:05.559
&lt;b&gt;including Kaiming&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:05.559 --> 02:35:07.194
&lt;b&gt;including Wu Yuxin&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:07.194 --> 02:35:07.995
&lt;b&gt;who is now at Kimi&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:08.061 --> 02:35:11.465
&lt;b&gt;they put enormous effort into building the infra&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:11.798 --> 02:35:13.467
&lt;b&gt;and building that codebase&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:13.467 --> 02:35:16.203
&lt;b&gt;so that the baselines&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:16.203 --> 02:35:17.671
&lt;b&gt;the baselines for these methods&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:17.938 --> 02:35:20.641
&lt;b&gt;already far exceeded all of those&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:20.641 --> 02:35:22.276
&lt;b&gt;random mediocre CVPR papers&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:22.476 --> 02:35:23.277
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:23.277 --> 02:35:25.178
&lt;b&gt;our baseline was already stronger than yours&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:25.178 --> 02:35:26.380
&lt;b&gt;so if I take one more step up&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:26.380 --> 02:35:29.182
&lt;b&gt;of course I'm going to go even further&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:29.182 --> 02:35:29.950
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:29.950 --> 02:35:30.350
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:30.350 --> 02:35:33.186
&lt;b&gt;I think I've always maintained this kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:33.186 --> 02:35:35.255
&lt;b&gt;methodology&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:35.255 --> 02:35:38.158
&lt;b&gt;I think I place a lot of importance on&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:38.592 --> 02:35:39.393
&lt;b&gt;this kind of&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:39.393 --> 02:35:41.828
&lt;b&gt;I don't want to call it engineering&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:41.828 --> 02:35:44.331
&lt;b&gt;because it's not entirely just about&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:44.331 --> 02:35:46.633
&lt;b&gt;the codebase itself&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:46.633 --> 02:35:47.567
&lt;b&gt;it's not&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:47.601 --> 02:35:49.569
&lt;b&gt;like building a codebase at a product company&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:49.569 --> 02:35:50.604
&lt;b&gt;that kind of relationship&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:50.604 --> 02:35:51.238
&lt;b&gt;it's more like&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:51.238 --> 02:35:53.941
&lt;b&gt;the scaffolding for a research breakthrough&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:54.207 --> 02:35:55.976
&lt;b&gt;if your scaffolding is unstable&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:56.176 --> 02:35:57.377
&lt;b&gt;you can't build anything&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:58.378 --> 02:35:58.779
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:58.779 --> 02:35:59.546
&lt;b&gt;this thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:35:59.546 --> 02:36:01.214
&lt;b&gt;also influences what we do now&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:01.214 --> 02:36:02.449
&lt;b&gt;but anyway, the point is&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:02.449 --> 02:36:04.351
&lt;b&gt;Kaiming in terms of building this scaffolding&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:04.351 --> 02:36:06.853
&lt;b&gt;was also truly exceptional&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:06.853 --> 02:36:08.255
&lt;b&gt;I think you were so lucky&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:08.255 --> 02:36:10.657
&lt;b&gt;because very early on someone told you&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:10.657 --> 02:36:11.892
&lt;b&gt;a lot of the right ways to do things&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:11.892 --> 02:36:14.861
&lt;b&gt;so in many areas&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:14.861 --> 02:36:16.229
&lt;b&gt;you avoided a lot of wrong turns&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:16.296 --> 02:36:17.597
&lt;b&gt;I think I was incredibly lucky&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:17.597 --> 02:36:19.599
&lt;b&gt;but I also hope&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:19.833 --> 02:36:21.368
&lt;b&gt;though I think a lot of this really is&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:21.368 --> 02:36:22.836
&lt;b&gt;on one hand, common sense&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:22.836 --> 02:36:24.871
&lt;b&gt;but as you said, on the other hand&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:24.871 --> 02:36:27.240
&lt;b&gt;for a student&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:27.240 --> 02:36:28.842
&lt;b&gt;this might not be so obvious&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:28.842 --> 02:36:29.643
&lt;b&gt;not so apparent&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:29.643 --> 02:36:30.577
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:30.744 --> 02:36:32.446
&lt;b&gt;like with this scaffolding thing&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:32.446 --> 02:36:34.481
&lt;b&gt;when we were at FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:34.481 --> 02:36:36.049
&lt;b&gt;there was a running joke&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:36.049 --> 02:36:38.185
&lt;b&gt;kind of a joke, sort of&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:38.185 --> 02:36:41.288
&lt;b&gt;the story goes that the first lesson for everyone interning at FAIR&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:41.288 --> 02:36:42.389
&lt;b&gt;guess what it was?&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:42.823 --> 02:36:43.490
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:43.490 --> 02:36:44.624
&lt;b&gt;the first lesson&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:44.891 --> 02:36:45.826
&lt;b&gt;was to use a certain tool&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:45.826 --> 02:36:47.027
&lt;b&gt;guess what that tool was?&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:47.694 --> 02:36:48.662
&lt;b&gt;no idea&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:48.662 --> 02:36:49.863
&lt;b&gt;that tool was&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:49.896 --> 02:36:52.032
&lt;b&gt;an Excel spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:52.032 --> 02:36:52.566
&lt;b&gt;[chuckles]&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:52.566 --> 02:36:55.202
&lt;b&gt;this thing is also quite interesting&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:55.202 --> 02:36:55.769
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:55.769 --> 02:36:59.806
&lt;b&gt;we'd have this whole system for tracking experiments&lt;/b&gt;

02:36:59.806 --> 02:37:02.409
&lt;b&gt;of course, this might be a bit outdated now&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:02.409 --> 02:37:03.844
&lt;b&gt;because nowadays there might be better&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:03.844 --> 02:37:04.644
&lt;b&gt;tools like Feishu&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:05.012 --> 02:37:07.014
&lt;b&gt;many better tools&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:07.014 --> 02:37:08.682
&lt;b&gt;but back then&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:08.682 --> 02:37:11.251
&lt;b&gt;we would meticulously&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:11.251 --> 02:37:12.586
&lt;b&gt;build this kind of template&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:12.586 --> 02:37:13.887
&lt;b&gt;and this template was just an Excel file&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:14.021 --> 02:37:16.023
&lt;b&gt;so sometimes we felt like office clerks&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:16.023 --> 02:37:17.557
&lt;b&gt;I do research every day&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:17.557 --> 02:37:19.893
&lt;b&gt;but it's not screen full of code&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:19.893 --> 02:37:21.628
&lt;b&gt;writing some fancy stuff&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:21.895 --> 02:37:24.031
&lt;b&gt;instead, staring at this spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:24.031 --> 02:37:24.464
&lt;b&gt;this Excel file&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:24.464 --> 02:37:25.565
&lt;b&gt;the spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:25.632 --> 02:37:28.869
&lt;b&gt;looking at what each row represents&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:29.436 --> 02:37:31.671
&lt;b&gt;the research part of this&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:31.671 --> 02:37:33.407
&lt;b&gt;is how you design the spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:33.440 --> 02:37:34.841
&lt;b&gt;how do you make sure&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:34.941 --> 02:37:37.277
&lt;b&gt;every experiment gives you&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:37.277 --> 02:37:38.779
&lt;b&gt;what I just called this gradient&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:38.879 --> 02:37:39.880
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:39.880 --> 02:37:41.481
&lt;b&gt;because you can always hit two extremes&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:41.481 --> 02:37:43.950
&lt;b&gt;one extreme is you run too few experiments&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:43.950 --> 02:37:45.218
&lt;b&gt;so your signal is unclear&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:45.218 --> 02:37:46.219
&lt;b&gt;you don't know anything&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:46.787 --> 02:37:49.423
&lt;b&gt;the other extreme is&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:49.423 --> 02:37:51.858
&lt;b&gt;I don't care at all what experiments I'm running&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:51.858 --> 02:37:52.893
&lt;b&gt;I just run experiments blindly&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:52.893 --> 02:37:53.260
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:53.260 --> 02:37:54.661
&lt;b&gt;I have all these resources&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:54.661 --> 02:37:56.096
&lt;b&gt;I just maximize my resources&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:56.096 --> 02:37:57.297
&lt;b&gt;run all the jobs&lt;/b&gt;

02:37:57.297 --> 02:38:00.133
&lt;b&gt;dump all the results&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:00.133 --> 02:38:02.035
&lt;b&gt;just throw everything into the spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:02.369 --> 02:38:04.037
&lt;b&gt;and then feel satisfied&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:04.037 --> 02:38:05.839
&lt;b&gt;thinking my research is done&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:05.872 --> 02:38:09.576
&lt;b&gt;both of these are a pretty poor&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:09.776 --> 02:38:11.845
&lt;b&gt;pattern for a student's research&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:11.845 --> 02:38:12.345
&lt;b&gt;mm&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:12.345 --> 02:38:14.347
&lt;b&gt;but back then, by watching how Kaiming&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:14.347 --> 02:38:16.283
&lt;b&gt;built that kind of spreadsheet&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:16.383 --> 02:38:17.751
&lt;b&gt;I learned an enormous amount&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:17.951 --> 02:38:18.618
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:18.618 --> 02:38:21.455
&lt;b&gt;because you really have to make some decisions&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:22.088 --> 02:38:24.825
&lt;b&gt;those decisions being, for my&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:24.825 --> 02:38:27.461
&lt;b&gt;what metrics should I actually focus on&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:27.461 --> 02:38:28.361
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:28.361 --> 02:38:29.830
&lt;b&gt;what should I actually be recording&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:29.830 --> 02:38:30.630
&lt;b&gt;what columns should there be&lt;/b&gt;

02:38:30.630 --> 02:38:31.431
&lt;b&gt;how should I define control variables&lt;/b&gt;

