05:49:21.570 --> 05:49:24.006
&lt;b&gt;we'll have another paper to release&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:24.006 --> 05:49:25.774
&lt;b&gt;the paper is called Solaris&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:25.774 --> 05:49:27.309
&lt;b&gt;Solaris (from Stanisław Lem's 1961 novel)&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:27.376 --> 05:49:30.112
&lt;b&gt;this is actually a sci-fi novel&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:30.112 --> 05:49:31.113
&lt;b&gt;a novel by Lem, and&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:31.113 --> 05:49:33.315
&lt;b&gt;later adapted into a film by Tarkovsky&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:33.582 --> 05:49:35.684
&lt;b&gt;and the reason we chose this name&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:35.684 --> 05:49:38.487
&lt;b&gt;is because we're building a so-called&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:38.487 --> 05:49:39.688
&lt;b&gt;video generation model&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:39.788 --> 05:49:42.491
&lt;b&gt;and the film is also about&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:42.491 --> 05:49:43.458
&lt;b&gt;an ocean&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:43.458 --> 05:49:44.026
&lt;b&gt;this ocean&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:44.026 --> 05:49:46.328
&lt;b&gt;that can read the subconscious memories of people&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:46.528 --> 05:49:49.665
&lt;b&gt;and ultimately materialize and generate things from them&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:49.765 --> 05:49:52.401
&lt;b&gt;I think that's really fascinating&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:52.401 --> 05:49:53.201
&lt;b&gt;of course&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:53.201 --> 05:49:54.903
&lt;b&gt;in Tarkovsky's film&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:54.903 --> 05:49:56.071
&lt;b&gt;the message is&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:56.605 --> 05:49:58.574
&lt;b&gt;our greatest enemy&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:58.574 --> 05:49:59.708
&lt;b&gt;is not some alien civilization&lt;/b&gt;

05:49:59.708 --> 05:50:00.976
&lt;b&gt;or the unknowable&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:00.976 --> 05:50:03.412
&lt;b&gt;the ocean is actually humanity itself&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:03.612 --> 05:50:08.417
&lt;b&gt;it is humanity's own suffering and memories&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:08.450 --> 05:50:09.284
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:09.284 --> 05:50:12.087
&lt;b&gt;the ocean is just a projection of humanity onto itself&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:12.087 --> 05:50:13.422
&lt;b&gt;I want to bring this up because&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:13.422 --> 05:50:14.623
&lt;b&gt;I think this&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:14.790 --> 05:50:18.493
&lt;b&gt;film parallels what happens with LLMs so closely&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:18.493 --> 05:50:21.263
&lt;b&gt;I think LLMs may not actually be understanding humans&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:21.263 --> 05:50:23.065
&lt;b&gt;it's just a projection of humanity&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:23.065 --> 05:50:24.232
&lt;b&gt;just a reflection&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:24.466 --> 05:50:25.968
&lt;b&gt;but what I want to say is&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:25.968 --> 05:50:27.502
&lt;b&gt;in relation to Yann&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:27.502 --> 05:50:28.670
&lt;b&gt;one day I said to him, hey&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:28.670 --> 05:50:29.604
&lt;b&gt;this paper of ours&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:29.638 --> 05:50:31.173
&lt;b&gt;what do you think of this name?&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:31.406 --> 05:50:33.909
&lt;b&gt;and I wanted to see if he knew the film&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:34.276 --> 05:50:35.077
&lt;b&gt;and he said, oh&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:35.077 --> 05:50:36.778
&lt;b&gt;you know this is a film title, right?&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:36.812 --> 05:50:37.379
&lt;b&gt;I said yes&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:37.379 --> 05:50:37.713
&lt;b&gt;that's exactly&lt;/b&gt;

05:50:37.713 --> 05:50:39.615
&lt;b&gt;why I chose this name&lt;/b&gt;

