&lt;b&gt;200 billion parameters&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:33.252 --> 06:38:34.987
&lt;b&gt;and with an MoE architecture&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:35.087 --> 06:38:37.490
&lt;b&gt;and they were able to ingest all that data&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:37.523 --> 06:38:38.691
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:38.691 --> 06:38:40.226
&lt;b&gt;that's incredibly, incredibly impressive&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:40.226 --> 06:38:41.093
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:41.093 --> 06:38:44.029
&lt;b&gt;but all these generative models&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:45.564 --> 06:38:47.766
&lt;b&gt;90% is still a data problem&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:48.467 --> 06:38:50.169
&lt;b&gt;architecture doesn't matter much&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:50.903 --> 06:38:54.073
&lt;b&gt;90%, or let me say 95%&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:54.373 --> 06:38:55.574
&lt;b&gt;it's all a data problem&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:55.574 --> 06:38:57.510
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, their data is inherently abundant&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:57.510 --> 06:38:58.978
&lt;b&gt;their data itself is more&lt;/b&gt;

06:38:58.978 --> 06:39:00.045
&lt;b&gt;but volume alone isn't enough&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:00.045 --> 06:39:00.846
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:00.980 --> 06:39:03.015
&lt;b&gt;they must have done enormous work&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:03.015 --> 06:39:05.084
&lt;b&gt;to clean the data&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:05.384 --> 06:39:07.052
&lt;b&gt;to do captioning&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:07.219 --> 06:39:11.190
&lt;b&gt;to calibrate the data distribution&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:11.190 --> 06:39:13.092
&lt;b&gt;their diversity-quality balance&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:13.292 --> 06:39:16.095
&lt;b&gt;as well as their prompt alignment with language&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:16.095 --> 06:39:17.863
&lt;b&gt;the degree of that&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:17.863 --> 06:39:18.330
&lt;b&gt;I believe&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:18.330 --> 06:39:21.600
&lt;b&gt;a large number of people must have been involved in this work&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:21.600 --> 06:39:22.902
&lt;b&gt;and done an enormous amount&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:23.168 --> 06:39:23.969
&lt;b&gt;right&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:23.969 --> 06:39:26.372
&lt;b&gt;but once you've done all these things well&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:26.372 --> 06:39:28.107
&lt;b&gt;subsequent things&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:28.107 --> 06:39:29.375
&lt;b&gt;become much simpler&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:29.642 --> 06:39:30.643
&lt;b&gt;but I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:31.443 --> 06:39:33.379
&lt;b&gt;I think Seedance is very impressive&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:33.379 --> 06:39:35.648
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:35.881 --> 06:39:36.615
&lt;b&gt;including Sora&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:36.615 --> 06:39:37.616
&lt;b&gt;including Veo&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:37.683 --> 06:39:39.785
&lt;b&gt;wanting to surpass them&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:39.985 --> 06:39:41.654
&lt;b&gt;I don't think it's necessarily that simple&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:41.921 --> 06:39:44.490
&lt;b&gt;Our studio is called Language and World Studio&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:44.490 --> 06:39:45.925
&lt;b&gt;what comes to mind when you hear that name?&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:45.925 --> 06:39:47.993
&lt;b&gt;what are you thinking?&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:47.993 --> 06:39:50.329
&lt;b&gt;I see you wrote me a line: let go of&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:50.329 --> 06:39:51.430
&lt;b&gt;uh, called&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:51.830 --> 06:39:53.098
&lt;b&gt;let go of Wittgenstein&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:53.098 --> 06:39:54.300
&lt;b&gt;let go of Wittgenstein&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:54.300 --> 06:39:55.834
&lt;b&gt;well, that's not a great way to end&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:55.834 --> 06:39:56.802
&lt;b&gt;I'm going to start complaining again&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:56.802 --> 06:39:58.270
&lt;b&gt;right, go ahead&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;you complain — I say, let go of Wittgenstein&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:58.270 --> 06:39:59.104
&lt;b&gt;means you shouldn't&lt;/b&gt;

06:39:59.104 --> 06:40:00.673
&lt;b&gt;people shouldn't take Wittgenstein&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:00.673 --> 06:40:01.674
&lt;b&gt;and really stretch him&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:01.674 --> 06:40:05.110
&lt;b&gt;using it as a language boundary&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:05.110 --> 06:40:06.312
&lt;b&gt;meaning the limits of my world&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:06.312 --> 06:40:08.581
&lt;b&gt;and use that quote as endorsement for LLMs&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:08.581 --> 06:40:10.316
&lt;b&gt;or linguistic determinism&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:10.316 --> 06:40:12.851
&lt;b&gt;so that's completely absurd&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:12.851 --> 06:40:13.585
&lt;b&gt;and likewise&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:13.585 --> 06:40:14.787
&lt;b&gt;there are other quotes&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:14.787 --> 06:40:16.088
&lt;b&gt;like people citing Feynman&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:16.088 --> 06:40:18.424
&lt;b&gt;Feynman said what I cannot create&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:18.424 --> 06:40:19.892
&lt;b&gt;I do not understand&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:19.892 --> 06:40:20.426
&lt;b&gt;this&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:20.426 --> 06:40:22.661
&lt;b&gt;being used to endorse unified models&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:22.695 --> 06:40:23.429
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:23.429 --> 06:40:25.664
&lt;b&gt;both of these things are really unacceptable to me&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:26.098 --> 06:40:27.700
&lt;b&gt;what's the first thing?&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:27.700 --> 06:40:29.468
&lt;b&gt;the first is Wittgenstein, right?&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:29.601 --> 06:40:31.904
&lt;b&gt;when he spoke of the limits of language&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:31.904 --> 06:40:33.272
&lt;b&gt;as the limits of my world&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:33.305 --> 06:40:34.840
&lt;b&gt;there were strong preconditions&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:34.840 --> 06:40:36.709
&lt;b&gt;in his Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:36.709 --> 06:40:39.612
&lt;b&gt;what he discussed in the Tractatus&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:39.612 --> 06:40:42.214
&lt;b&gt;was that&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:42.214 --> 06:40:43.582
&lt;b&gt;your&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:43.582 --> 06:40:48.387
&lt;b&gt;the language he referred to targets what can be captured in propositions&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:48.387 --> 06:40:50.589
&lt;b&gt;the limits of the world that can be described&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:50.622 --> 06:40:53.292
&lt;b&gt;and this does not represent the general&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:53.292 --> 06:40:55.928
&lt;b&gt;the entirety of what we call the world&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:56.095 --> 06:40:56.495
&lt;b&gt;[laughter]&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:56.495 --> 06:40:58.631
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

06:40:58.631 --> 06:41:00.432
&lt;b&gt;first, the language he spoke of&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:00.432 --> 06:41:02.067
&lt;b&gt;and the world he spoke of&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:02.067 --> 06:41:04.436
&lt;b&gt;are already different from the language in today's LLMs&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:04.436 --> 06:41:05.504
&lt;b&gt;and the world it refers to&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:05.637 --> 06:41:08.007
&lt;b&gt;second, in his later period Wittgenstein&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:08.007 --> 06:41:10.776
&lt;b&gt;had completely overturned his earlier&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:10.776 --> 06:41:13.412
&lt;b&gt;entire philosophical system&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:14.113 --> 06:41:15.714
&lt;b&gt;he later stopped saying that&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:16.215 --> 06:41:18.417
&lt;b&gt;and what he talked about instead was&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:18.417 --> 06:41:19.985
&lt;b&gt;language is actually a game&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:19.985 --> 06:41:22.488
&lt;b&gt;the so-called concept of language games&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:22.521 --> 06:41:25.057
&lt;b&gt;meaning language itself has no inherent meaning&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:25.057 --> 06:41:27.259
&lt;b&gt;these symbols themselves have no meaning&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:28.260 --> 06:41:29.695
&lt;b&gt;the reason they acquire meaning&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:29.695 --> 06:41:33.399
&lt;b&gt;is because they are connected to real-world practice&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:33.432 --> 06:41:34.466
&lt;b&gt;and engaged with it&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:34.466 --> 06:41:35.267
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:35.300 --> 06:41:37.503
&lt;b&gt;and this is very much the world model view&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:37.670 --> 06:41:39.004
&lt;b&gt;that is&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:39.004 --> 06:41:39.905
&lt;b&gt;we're not saying&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:39.905 --> 06:41:42.608
&lt;b&gt;that language can perfectly&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:42.608 --> 06:41:44.710
&lt;b&gt;represent the entire world&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:44.977 --> 06:41:48.814
&lt;b&gt;what we're saying is that the world's practice&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:48.814 --> 06:41:53.652
&lt;b&gt;the world's actions determine the game of language&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:53.686 --> 06:41:55.087
&lt;b&gt;its intension and extension&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:55.587 --> 06:41:57.856
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, again&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:57.856 --> 06:41:58.924
&lt;b&gt;I don't understand philosophy&lt;/b&gt;

06:41:58.924 --> 06:42:00.125
&lt;b&gt;I don't understand Wittgenstein either&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:00.325 --> 06:42:02.995
&lt;b&gt;but I just don't like seeing in people's papers&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:03.128 --> 06:42:05.864
&lt;b&gt;opening with a pulled quote&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:05.864 --> 06:42:08.667
&lt;b&gt;I think that doesn't fit my aesthetic sensibilities&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:08.834 --> 06:42:12.071
&lt;b&gt;the Feynman quote is the same&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:12.071 --> 06:42:13.305
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, he said&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:13.305 --> 06:42:14.606
&lt;b&gt;what I cannot create&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:14.606 --> 06:42:16.208
&lt;b&gt;I do not understand&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:16.208 --> 06:42:17.609
&lt;b&gt;that quote itself is not wrong&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:17.876 --> 06:42:22.081
&lt;b&gt;but the create and understand he's referring to mean&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:22.081 --> 06:42:23.415
&lt;b&gt;for example, we have a world&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:23.415 --> 06:42:25.984
&lt;b&gt;we want to understand this world&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:25.984 --> 06:42:27.052
&lt;b&gt;we want to transform this world&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:27.052 --> 06:42:28.587
&lt;b&gt;we want to understand the world&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:28.587 --> 06:42:29.621
&lt;b&gt;through transforming it&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:29.722 --> 06:42:30.723
&lt;b&gt;whatever&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:30.723 --> 06:42:31.857
&lt;b&gt;the things he was talking about&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:31.857 --> 06:42:34.426
&lt;b&gt;are still within a real, concrete world&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:34.493 --> 06:42:35.828
&lt;b&gt;requiring some kind of action&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:35.928 --> 06:42:37.696
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, even when you're in class&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:37.696 --> 06:42:39.832
&lt;b&gt;you go and make a PowerPoint&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:39.965 --> 06:42:41.800
&lt;b&gt;you're still engaged in a process of creation&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:42.067 --> 06:42:43.669
&lt;b&gt;but now many people take this quote&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:43.669 --> 06:42:46.572
&lt;b&gt;and use it to make this kind of, uh&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:46.572 --> 06:42:50.175
&lt;b&gt;endorsement for some simple unified system&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:50.175 --> 06:42:52.578
&lt;b&gt;that's logically untenable too&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:53.412 --> 06:42:56.415
&lt;b&gt;we can't simply reduce creation&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:56.515 --> 06:42:59.184
&lt;b&gt;to a diffusion model&lt;/b&gt;

06:42:59.518 --> 06:43:02.020
&lt;b&gt;its backpropagation loss&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:02.020 --> 06:43:03.922
&lt;b&gt;that's completely absurd&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:03.922 --> 06:43:04.723
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, right?&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:04.857 --> 06:43:07.593
&lt;b&gt;so&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:07.593 --> 06:43:08.594
&lt;b&gt;I don't know&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:08.594 --> 06:43:10.062
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:10.295 --> 06:43:12.131
&lt;b&gt;maybe it's like when I was a kid&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:12.131 --> 06:43:15.200
&lt;b&gt;overusing famous quotes in essays&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:15.200 --> 06:43:18.437
&lt;b&gt;now seeing these things gives me a bit of PTSD&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:18.570 --> 06:43:22.040
&lt;b&gt;and I think as Kaiming said&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:22.040 --> 06:43:23.809
&lt;b&gt;everyone should read more philosophy&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:23.976 --> 06:43:25.511
&lt;b&gt;I think that's quite worthwhile&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:25.511 --> 06:43:28.614
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, at the very start you said you believe in fate&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:28.614 --> 06:43:29.648
&lt;b&gt;and believe in it more and more&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:29.648 --> 06:43:31.650
&lt;b&gt;where do you feel fate is pushing you now?&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:32.718 --> 06:43:33.786
&lt;b&gt;Ah&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:34.987 --> 06:43:38.457
&lt;b&gt;I don't know&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:38.791 --> 06:43:40.492
&lt;b&gt;is fate pushing me?&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:40.492 --> 06:43:43.262
&lt;b&gt;it doesn't seem like it&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:43.262 --> 06:43:43.662
&lt;b&gt;I think&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:43.662 --> 06:43:46.665
&lt;b&gt;there's no feeling of being pushed by fate&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:46.665 --> 06:43:47.966
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, just&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:49.434 --> 06:43:54.039
&lt;b&gt;mm-hmm, when the next time I need to make a choice comes&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:54.039 --> 06:43:55.574
&lt;b&gt;I just hope for good fortune&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:55.808 --> 06:43:58.010
&lt;b&gt;Is this world a giant world model?&lt;/b&gt;

06:43:58.310 --> 06:44:00.078
&lt;b&gt;of course the world is a giant world model&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:00.279 --> 06:44:02.047
&lt;b&gt;can you predict fate then?&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:02.581 --> 06:44:04.483
&lt;b&gt;uh, I don't think so&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:04.883 --> 06:44:05.717
&lt;b&gt;why not?&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:06.185 --> 06:44:06.685
&lt;b&gt;Mm-hmm&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:06.685 --> 06:44:08.287
&lt;b&gt;because we don't have enough resources&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:09.254 --> 06:44:09.588
&lt;b&gt;Oh&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:09.588 --> 06:44:12.991
&lt;b&gt;you'd need a computer as large as the Earth&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:12.991 --> 06:44:14.126
&lt;b&gt;or you'd need a computer&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:14.126 --> 06:44:16.061
&lt;b&gt;the size of the entire universe&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:16.261 --> 06:44:22.701
&lt;b&gt;to tell you the answer about life&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:22.834 --> 06:44:23.835
&lt;b&gt;about the universe&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:23.835 --> 06:44:25.370
&lt;b&gt;about anything&lt;/b&gt;

06:44:25.971 --> 06:44:27.573
&lt;b&gt;and the answer might ultimately be 42&lt;/b&gt;

